HT_DATA.load("detail", {"LQRAfJyEsko":{"wy":"HN commenters push it, often forcefully, as the video to watch to see what factory farming really involves, and some say it changed their diet.","q":{"x":"this documentary made me re-evaluate my diet.","i":25210464,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":35270239,"author":"socraticmethod","month":"2023-03","story":"GOOD Meat gets green light from FDA for cultivated meat","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary showing animals mutilated and slaughtered in farming.","quote":"Watch Dominion and tell us these animals are not being mutilated, mangled, tormented, slaughtered and murdered.","text":"That's not the full definition of murder. Here's the rest: - 1: to kill (a human being) unlawfully and with premeditated malice - 2: to slaughter wantonly : SLAY - 3a: to put an end to - 3b: TEASE, TORMENT - 3c: MUTILATE, MANGLE [VIDEO] Watch Dominion and tell us these animals are not being mutilated, mangled, tormented, slaughtered and murdered. Animals do not want to die. The will ALL naturally cry, scream, run, fight and generally do anything possible to avoid getting killed. Killing them is murder. Discrimination or unjustified treatment of animals based on an individual's species membership is called Speciesism. The belief that humans alone possess intrinsic value or are the central entity in the universe is called Anthropocentrism."},{"id":34584805,"author":"f3b5","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Something you’ve done your whole life that you realized is wrong?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary showing abuse in food industry; changed commenter's eating.","quote":"If you disagree, I challenge you - watch 15min and then post your reply","text":"Not waking up earlier to the fact that the meat section in the supermarket is a product of systemic, gruesome torture of intelligent beings. Like probably many of you who read this, I usually felt a bit guilty when I was confronted with the ugly facts we are all vaguely familiar with. I justified my meat consumption with specieist arguments (humans are so much better than pigs, cows, etc). My wife challenged me to watch at least a little bit of a documentary, and if I still wanted to eat salami, so be it. Afterwards I didn't want to eat salami anymore. I now think that the extreme abuse of animals by the food industry on behalf of ignorant consumers is probably the worst evil of the 21st century. If you disagree, I challenge you - watch 15min and then post your reply: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32800841,"author":"gday2020","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on cruelty in animal industries.","quote":"Watching that has changed my life quite a bit.","text":"Dominion: [VIDEO] It’s about the cruelty that happens in the animal industry. Minks, dogs, gooses, cows, pigs, horses, camels and a lot more. Watching that has changed my life quite a bit."},{"id":30572307,"author":"atweiden","month":"2022-03","story":"1 in 10 Americans say they don’t eat meat – a growing share of the population","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Challenge to watch it and not be curious about plant-based eating.","quote":"I challenge anyone to watch both The Dominion [1] and Forks Over Knives [2]","text":"Farming is a way for folks who work with their hands to achieve some degree of self-sovereignty and freedom — laudable goals. Unfortunately, this is often accomplished through gross ethical violations. All we can do is educate consumers about the benefits of a whole food plant-based (WFPB) diet. I challenge anyone to watch both The Dominion [1] and Forks Over Knives [2], and not walk away from the experience at least semi-curious about WFPB. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":27515566,"author":"sweetheart","month":"2021-06","story":"Animal testing is exploitative and largely ineffective","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows the suffering behind meat and dairy.","quote":"Literally just watch Dominion.","text":"Because it involves causing extreme suffering, and we definitively do not need to do it to live long healthy lives. Literally just watch Dominion. Watch it to see the suffering I’m talking about. Why do you think animals deserve this so that you can eat a meal? Is that agony they exist in for their whole life worth a hamburger, or a piece of cheese? Vegans say no. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25210464,"author":"beigeoak","month":"2020-11","story":"World's first no-kill eggs go on sale in Berlin (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary with chick culling scene; changed commenter's diet.","quote":"this documentary made me re-evaluate my diet.","text":"> If you can determine the sex of a hatching egg you can entirely dispense with the culling of live male chicks Here is the video, timestamped (24m30s) to the \"culling\" of male chicks. Trigger Warnings: NSFL, Gore. [VIDEO] On a purely personal note, this documentary made me re-evaluate my diet. I'm still not strong enough to be off meat even though that is what I want, but I have successfully abstained from chicken, pig and cow for the past 2 years."},{"id":24581756,"author":"rewq4321","month":"2020-09","story":"Australia to produce seaweed cattle feed that reduces methane emissions by 80%","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows animal cruelty in farming for those who doubt it.","quote":"If you think it's not that bad, watch Dominion","text":"By which definition of \"relevant\" are animal cruelty[0] and pandemic-level diseases not included? COVID-19 is very \"tame\" compared to what's possible. There's a sense in which we're lucky that we got this pandemic as a practice round for something which we know has the potential to be much, much worse. This isn't controversial at all. [0] If you think it's not that bad, watch Dominion: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29871891,"author":"neuronic","month":"2022-01","story":"UCLA Study Finds Laughter in 65 Species, from Rats to Cows","stance":"rec","why":"Recently watched; deepened concern for animal abuse.","quote":"I recently watched Dominion documentary [1]","text":"This is probably me not being able to “read the room” but this study both makes me very happy and very sad. I recently watched Dominion documentary [1] and the more we learn about animals cognition the more I feel the weight of their massive abuse especially in the last 150 years. (The recent story of an HNer interacting with a spider on his desk was really cool though, I don’t have a link anymore.) Without judging anyone or their behavior in particular I just feel repelled by our treatment of other clearly cognitive beings. The people working these places don’t seem like ones you would want around anyone in society either and I don’t buy the story that it’s only bad in some places. I’d wager that it’s really bad in most cases. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28035103,"author":"rewq4321","month":"2021-08","story":"An Animal’s Place (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Worth-watching documentary; requires sign-in due to age restriction.","quote":"This documentary is worth watching","text":"This documentary is worth watching: [VIDEO] (Dominion, released in 2018, unfortunately requires signing into youtube due to age retrictions)"},{"id":27495072,"author":"sweetheart","month":"2021-06","story":"Animal testing is exploitative and largely ineffective","stance":"rec","why":"Glimpse into animal suffering.","quote":"it offers just a glimpse into what that suffering includes","text":"Oh boy, let me tell you, animals absolutely know what we are doing to them. They suffer immensely. Check this out, it offers just a glimpse into what that suffering includes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27072746,"author":"atweiden","month":"2021-05","story":"Beyond Meat’s Beyond Burger life cycle assessment (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what really happens on factory farms.","quote":"the documentary Dominion [2]","text":"Human evolution is poorly understood, and the assumption that any choice $X supposedly made by early humans led to our forefathers’ anamolously rapid brain development is flatly speculative. > Unlike most great apes today, whose diet is primarily plant based, we can thank the incredible nutrient density in meat and our incredible hunting and tool creating ability for prefrontal cortex development. Complete and utter speculation. > We were pack hunters, forging tools and strategies capable of taking down mammoths. Call it 'diabolicaly evil' if you want, but this is simply nature and survival. Subsistence hunting amongst early human tribes is not comparable to modern day factory farming in any conceivable way. B12 doesn’t come from eating plants OR animals: it comes from bacteria, and ironically that bacteria is in such short supply on factory farms that the animals need to be injected with synthetic B12. > I don't personally believe highly processed plant alternatives such as Beyond meat are a long term option if you're even vaguely interested in your own health. I eat raw soybeans, legumes, grains, nuts and seeds. I’d recommend watching the documentary Forks Over Knives [1] if you care about your personal health, and the documentary Dominion [2], if you’d like to see what really happens on factory farms. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21052961,"author":"hombre_fatal","month":"2019-09","story":"Farm animals in India and China are becoming more resistant to antibiotics","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary contrasting with rosy assumptions about animal agriculture.","quote":"How happy are these animals in the documentary, Dominion?","text":"How happy are these animals in the documentary, Dominion? [VIDEO] Your post presents a nice rainbows and sunshine guess at how the animal ag industry works, and I really wish it worked like that, too."},{"id":19506511,"author":"pantalaimon","month":"2019-03","story":"CowToilet, a Toilet for Cows","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what farm animals endure while alive.","quote":"It's about what they have to endure while they are alive.","text":"It's not about how the animals look when they are dead. It's about what they have to endure while they are alive. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40245081,"author":"xvector","month":"2024-05","story":"Wild orangutan seen healing his wound with a plant","stance":"reference","why":"Footnoted as evidence of animals being tortured in farming.","quote":"","text":"> We eat meat. Purely for taste. > We need to eat meat. False. 10-20% of the world population does not and we're just fine. > So we need to kill animals for that. No, you want to kill animals because you want tasty food. Not because you \"need\" meat. No one living in an industrialized country does. It's trivial to create a plant-based balanced diet (and a tasty one at that.) > Zoos absolutely not required for humans to survive. Neither are factory farms. They exist to satisfy \"taste buds go brr.\" But that is not worth literally torturing[1] an animal to death. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30520283,"author":"dhruvbehl","month":"2022-03","story":"Meat-eating extends human life expectancy worldwide","stance":"reference","why":"Incidental link in a nutrition thread.","quote":"","text":"> i eat them bc i dont want to gave to take unregulated supplements to make up for nutrtional deficiencies you wouldnt have if you just ate meat. B12 isn’t reliably absorbed from meat. Everyone should be taking a b12 supplement unless they eat b12 fortified cereals or get regular blood work but supplements are the cheapest method for most. “ A careful look at 3,000 men and women in the ongoing Framingham Offspring Study found 39 percent with plasma B12 levels in the “low normal” range--below 258 picomoles per liter. While this is well above the currently accepted deficiency level of 148 pmol/L, some people exhibit neurological symptoms in the higher range, said study leader Katherine Tucker... The researchers also expected to find some connection between dietary intake and plasma levels, even though other studies found no association. And they did find a connection. Supplement use dropped the percentage of volunteers in the danger zone--plasma B12 below 185 pmol/L--from 20 percent to 8. Eating fortified cereals five or more times a week or being among the highest third for dairy intake reduced, by nearly half, the percentage of volunteers in that zone--from 23 and 24 percent, respectively, to 12 and 13 percent. Oddly, the researchers found no association between plasma B12 levels and meat, poultry, and fish intake, even though these foods supply the bulk of B12 in the diet. “It’s not because people aren’t eating enough meat,” Tucker said. “The vitamin isn’t getting absorbed.”"},{"id":30813631,"author":"xvector","month":"2022-03","story":"Pamplona Bull Runs Reveal Dynamics of Crowds in Danger","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of suffering in factory farms.","quote":"Factory farms are objectively the largest producers of suffering this planet has ever seen","text":"As far as hurting animals for entertainment goes, we can look at our meals for inspiration. The average person will cause magnitudes more suffering than the bull runs thanks to their dietary choices. Factory farms are objectively the largest producers of suffering this planet has ever seen [1, 2], and most people consume the meat they produce for pleasure as opposed to necessity. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":25544653,"author":"rewq4321","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: What startup/technology is on your 'to watch' list?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for unpleasant lives of factory-farmed animals.","quote":"animals that are raised in factory farms do not live pleasant lives, to put it lightly.[3]","text":"If you could grow and kill with a small fraction of the suffering that farmed animals endure today, then eating meat would be mostly fine (other than environmental issues, perhaps). Unfortunately that's impossible for all but a very small minority of people - those who can raise and kill animals themselves or have someone they trust very well to do it for them.[0][1] Well over 95% of meat is factory farmed[2], and animals that are raised in factory farms do not live pleasant lives, to put it lightly.[3] This is coming from someone who grew up on a farm, and whose parents are still farmers. [0] People think that buying from their local butcher or local farm must make their meat ethical. In fact, small abbatoirs often have very shoddy killing protocols compared to the large ones. You end up with farmers clubbing animals to death, or putting 5 bullets into a pig's head before it dies: [LINK] [1] Hunting isn't an ethical source of meat. Even professional hunters (e.g. those who kill kangaroos for farmers in AU) will let ~1 in 20 get away with a bullet in them. That could mean suffering for hours or even days if it clots but then dies due to internal bleeding or infection. I used to hunt and I would go to sleep thinking about the animals that got away wounded that day. Still, it took me far too long to realise that there is no need to eat animals. [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24451461,"author":"atweiden","month":"2020-09","story":"Wildlife in ‘catastrophic decline’ due to human destruction, scientists warn","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how bacon is made.","quote":"What is arguably shameful, however, is how the bacon gets made [2].","text":"I'm not here to be your enemy, so please don't make me out to be one. I wasn't raised vegetarian by any means, and I know fully well meat tastes amazingly good, and that having a varied diet is important. But heart disease, diabetes and many types of cancers are worryingly linked with the consumption of meat, dairy and eggs. As the documentary Forks Over Knives [1] makes abundantly clear, a whole foods plant-based diet is in every way a life extension technology. > Is that something to be ashamed of? Wanting to feel satiated is nothing to be ashamed of. What is arguably shameful, however, is how the bacon gets made [2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23236196,"author":"vzidex","month":"2020-05","story":"Millions of US farm animals to be culled by suffocation, drowning and shooting","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for cruelty of normal slaughter methods.","quote":"","text":"To anyone reading this thread and thinking \"The horror!\": Being culled through these various methods is no better or worse than they would have experienced under \"normal\" conditions - i.e. being raised until they are fit for slaughter and sale. No animal wants to die, and the methods used to kill them when being sold are just as cruel as the \"culling\" techniques listed here [1]. If you don't like what you're reading in this article, go vegan. Otherwise, you're paying for other people to inflict this sort of suffering on animals. [1] Dominion (2018): [VIDEO]"},{"id":23238034,"author":"vzidex","month":"2020-05","story":"Millions of US farm animals to be culled by suffocation, drowning and shooting","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of immense suffering in animal lives.","quote":"which is typically immense [2]","text":"> slaughter methods involve less suffering than all of us are likely to experience at our deaths, and likewise less than animals in nature generally experience Sure, but that says nothing about the suffering these animals experience during their (short [1]) lives, which is typically immense [2] - >=97% of farm animals are raised on factory farms (except for cows, at 78%) [3]. So yes, people should be aware of the facts. Out of curiosity, why did you shift away from a vegan diet? [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"}],"r":[["aMcjxSThD54",1],["DoA4Txr4GUs",1],["LZPKTaVB1IU",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["XpDsk374LDE",1],["np_ylvc8Zj8",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["F1Hq8eVOMHs",1]]},"HyznrdDSSGM":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends the series it launched as the best way to understand how a CPU works, praised for Eater's clear teaching.","q":{"x":"I think I learned more from Ben Eater’s “Building an 8-bit breadboard computer” series [0] than my intro to computer architecture course at university.","i":30057092,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30057092,"author":"teeray","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: How to begin learning assembler for university course?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Taught more than a university architecture course.","quote":"I think I learned more from Ben Eater’s “Building an 8-bit breadboard computer” series [0] than my intro to computer architecture course at university.","text":"I think I learned more from Ben Eater’s “Building an 8-bit breadboard computer” series [0] than my intro to computer architecture course at university. Highly recommended. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29267468,"author":"IanWard1","month":"2021-11","story":"Megaprocessor – A micro-processor built large (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing videos everyone should watch.","quote":"(which are amazing and everyone should watch)","text":"Love this kind of build, so I'm working on a little breadboard one myself [LINK] similar to the Ben Eater 8-bit CPU videos [VIDEO] (which are amazing and everyone should watch)"},{"id":27804311,"author":"rtrunck","month":"2021-07","story":"How does USB device discovery work? [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Taught the commenter a lot about electronics.","quote":"Ben Eater (Author of this Video) is fantastic. Learned so much on electronics and computers through this series","text":"Ben Eater (Author of this Video) is fantastic. Learned so much on electronics and computers through this series [VIDEO]"},{"id":24529159,"author":"dnautics","month":"2020-09","story":"Ask HN: Resources to encourage teen on becoming computer engineer?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Valuable foundational hardware knowledge regardless of career.","quote":"the video series on building an computer from scratch is fantastic.","text":"1) the video series on building an computer from scratch is fantastic. It's valuable information no matter where in the tech stack you wind up in (or even if you don't wind up in tech) but also having a deep knowledge of these systems will be of considerable value down the line, because we aren't training kids in hardware as much anymore. [VIDEO] 2) If their interest is a bit higher in the stack (like robotics e.g.), and they don't mind getting in the weeds and asking for help in the community, I would target learning with a raspberry pi system. My preferred target is Nerves; it's a bit more bare-bones, and there aren't drivers for everything, but the community is fantastic (the elixir slack / nerves is the place to be), and it's easy to get to a point where you are dropped into an IDE and you can just code ."},{"id":22844658,"author":"gtsteve","month":"2020-04","story":"“Hello world” from scratch on a 6502 (2019) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enlightening CPU-from-logic-gates series for software people.","quote":"As a pure software guy I found it very enlightening.","text":"I love this guy's videos, I've been watching them for a year or so now. He also made a series [0] where he built the CPU, microcode and all using logic gates and components. As a pure software guy I found it very enlightening. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18000552,"author":"brox","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: Favorite teachers on YouTube?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great educator and effort in material.","quote":"I find Ben Eater's 8-bit computer from scratch series really wonderful, mainly because of his skills as an educator","text":"I find Ben Eater's 8-bit computer from scratch series really wonderful, mainly because of his skills as an educator and the effort he puts into the material: [VIDEO] Check out his latest videos on low-level data transmission techniques as well."},{"id":34334704,"author":"santadakota","month":"2023-01","story":"What Happens When a CPU Starts","stance":"rec","why":"Fantastic series on breadboard computers.","quote":"Ben Eater's fantastic video series on building a breadboard 6502 based computer and an 8-bit breadboard computer from scratch","text":"Ben Eater's fantastic video series on building a breadboard 6502 based computer and an 8-bit breadboard computer from scratch might be appreciated in this thread. 6502 playlist: [LINK] 8-bit build playlist: [VIDEO] He also sells kits if one is interested in playing along."},{"id":34336682,"author":"ruslan","month":"2023-01","story":"What Happens When a CPU Starts","stance":"rec","why":"Educational, entertaining breadboard 6502 computer build.","quote":"I find Ben's Eater series on building simple 8-bit breadboard computer using 6502 CPU very educating and entertaining.","text":"Starting procedure on MOS6502 is not that simple as it is told in the article. It spends some 7 clocks on doing internal initialization and only then fetches address of first jump from reset vector (0xFFFC/0xFFFD). I find Ben's Eater series on building simple 8-bit breadboard computer using 6502 CPU very educating and entertaining. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28558956,"author":"mrspeaker","month":"2021-09","story":"Sir Clive Sinclair has died","stance":"rec","why":"Fun project evoking early home computing.","quote":"Really fun project","text":"I recently had the feeling of those early home-computing days from the Ben Eater's \"8-bit computer from scratch\" videos/kits. Really fun project: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24666203,"author":"bishopdynamics","month":"2020-10","story":"Can You Build a CPU?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why things work.","quote":"If you're looking to get a much better understanding of /why/ things work, you should check out Ben Eater's videos on YT","text":"If you're looking to get a much better understanding of /why/ things work, you should check out Ben Eater's videos on YT [VIDEO]"},{"id":24519455,"author":"klelatti","month":"2020-09","story":"Z80, the 8-bit Number Cruncher (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Helps understand how a microprocessor works.","quote":"Ben Eater's YouTube series on building an 8 bit computer is great to help build understanding how a microprocessor works.","text":"Not 100% sure this is precisely what you're looking for but Ben Eater's YouTube series on building an 8 bit computer is great to help build understanding how a microprocessor works. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23584902,"author":"bloodorange","month":"2020-06","story":"An Intro to Compilers (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Breadboard 8-bit computer with good explanations.","quote":"I find that his explanations are quite good as well.","text":"I have a feeling you might like this: [LINK] It's a popular course about building a computer from nand gates and eventually building a Tetris like game on it. All of this is done on a hardware simulator of course. If you are keen on real hardware, you might find this interesting: [VIDEO] It's a series of videos where Ben Eater explains how to build an 8-bit computer using breadboards and basic elements of circuits. I find that his explanations are quite good as well."},{"id":18845873,"author":"djmips","month":"2019-01","story":"MegaProcessor (2017) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Great series on building an 8-bit computer.","quote":"Ben Eater's great YouTube series on building an 8 bit computer.","text":"If this interests you, you'll probably really like or already have seen Ben Eater's great YouTube series on building an 8 bit computer. (2016 as well) [VIDEO]"},{"id":17180077,"author":"Bilters","month":"2018-05","story":"How long would it take to create a Windows 1.0 capable machine from scratch?","stance":"rec","why":"Enjoyable series building an 8-bit computer.","quote":"I really enjoyed the series Ben Eater has produced making his 8 bit computer.","text":"Don't really know. But if you decide to do so, please make a video series about it. I really enjoyed the series Ben Eater has produced making his 8 bit computer. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14931309,"author":"zurkog","month":"2017-08","story":"Build an 8-bit computer from scratch","stance":"reference","why":"Ben Eater's series builds Malvino's SAP computer.","quote":"Ben Eater did a fantastic job of making these videos, explaining each concept","text":"This was so cool as a book. Ben Eater did a fantastic job of making these videos, explaining each concept, but the computer he builds (the SAP, or Simple As Possible) is directly from \"Digital Computer Electronics\" (c) 1977 by Albert Paul Malvino. He even goes so far as to mention it and shows a copy of the book in his first video: [VIDEO] I stumbled across the book 4 years ago when I followed this instructable: [LINK] The instructable is nowhere near as detailed as Ben's videos, but it was all I had at the time, and it recommended that book. Note: when I purchased the book 4 years ago, it was only $5, now I'm seeing it for upwards of $99: [LINK] The paperback is only $22, but it lists an additional author not present on the 1977 version, so I don't know if it's the exact same book."}],"r":[["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["rEoc0YoALt0",1],["3MghYhf-GhU",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["fKSSY1gzCEs",1],["x1v2tX4_dkQ",1],["f61KMw5zVhg",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["yTkzNHF6rMs",1],["nLApFANtkHs",1],["C2RO34b_oPM",1],["URSWYvyc42M",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["dP1xVpMPn8M",1]]},"d_zt3kGW1NM":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a pointed critique of philanthropy and win-win thinking, delivered inside Google itself, and cite it across threads on wealth, tax and tech power.","q":{"x":"I strongly encourage everyone - especialy anyone employed at Google - to watch it.","i":19221081,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":48393774,"author":"abuob","month":"2026-06","story":"American capitalism has taken an apocalyptic turn","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Infamous talk about dismantling Google, among enlightening suggestions","quote":"a few suggestions that I personally found incredibly enlightening","text":"If you're genuinely curious, a few suggestions that I personally found incredibly enlightening: Anand Giridhardas, Winners Take All: The book is fantastic, there's also his now infamous google talk where he talks about dismantling google: [VIDEO] Thomas Piketty, Capital in the 21st century: Probably the most comprehensive (but at times also longwinded) outline on how wealth inequality comes to be, why it's not good for society and how it could be adressed"},{"id":26978300,"author":"Shacklz","month":"2021-04","story":"Artist faked being a billionaire to photograph New York City’s best views","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Critical analysis of the downsides of win-win mentality","quote":"He specifically addresses the negative points of the","text":"> It's way nicer on our side, where we do win-win deals, and know to be happy, the people around us need to be happy. If you would like this world-view to undergo some critical analysis, I strongly recommend Anand Giridharadas' take in \"Winners take All\" (condensed in this google-talk here: [VIDEO] ). He specifically addresses the negative points of the \"win-win\"-mentality, and in my opinion, makes a strong point that a society built on a \"win-win\" culture is bound to be doomed."},{"id":19313330,"author":"CharlesColeman","month":"2019-03","story":"The Oppression of the Supermajority","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explores how donor preferences bias charity","quote":"I highly recommend watching it, I thought it was elucidating.","text":"> But a system of philanthropy is inherently biased insofar as since donors can and do choose charitable causes that match their own preferences. Anand Giridharadas goes pretty deeply into that idea in this talk that he did at Google. I highly recommend watching it, I thought it was elucidating. [VIDEO] I want to read his book \"Winners Take All: The Elite Charade of Changing the World\" but I haven't gotten to it yet."},{"id":19221081,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-02","story":"Is Philanthropy Anti-Democratic?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why philanthropy and win-win solutions are anti-democratic","quote":"I strongly encourage everyone - especialy anyone employed at Google - to watch it.","text":"One of the best explanations why \"philanthropy\" and \"novel (disrupting), win-win solutions\" for systemic problems is highly anti-democratic (and inciting class warfare) is Anand Giridharadas's recent talk[1] at Google[2]. I strongly encourage everyone - especialy anyone employed at Google - to watch it. [1] [VIDEO] [2] Google is the perfect venue for this talk."},{"id":18292032,"author":"mistersquid","month":"2018-10","story":"Winners Take All: The Elite Charade of Changing the World [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explosive talk challenging Google to disclose political activity","quote":"the quality of the first half the video is superb.","text":"Giridharadas talk is explosive in terms of its ideas. From what I can tell, he's not pulling any punches even though he's been invited to speak on a Google campus (Boston). At 29:03 Giridharadas challenges his audience to demand that Google (and other Silicon Valley companies) generate an annual political disclosure to its employees, the very people in his audience. [0] As a side note, Giridharadas is very clear and measured in his articulation, and I found that speeding the video to 1.5 times normal rate to be a good delivery speed (YMMV). What Giridharadas says prior to the timecode I referenced is also provocative and politically risky. I can't yet comment on his entire talk, but the quality of the first half the video is superb. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27967295,"author":"brigandish","month":"2021-07","story":"Disinformation for hire, a shadow industry, is quietly booming","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent insights, including Google's role in hollowing out local news","quote":"which has many excellent insights","text":"There's a talk[1] by Anand Ghiridharadas about his book[2] - ironically, given at Google - which has many excellent insights, among them that local news has been hollowed out and destroyed by Google and the bigger fish are struggling, leading to this race to the bottom for clicks. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27565419,"author":"Shacklz","month":"2021-06","story":"Life as a public school teacher in the San Francisco Bay Area in 2021","stance":"rec","why":"Addresses why US public schools underperform","quote":"Anand Giridharadas addresses this in his infamous google-talk quite nicely I think","text":"Public schools work well throughout the majority of the developed world - the US seems to be a notable exception. Anand Giridharadas addresses this in his infamous google-talk quite nicely I think: [VIDEO] (I'm linking this talk on HN way too often, but it just seems perfectly fitting so very often)"},{"id":27570080,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-06","story":"Seeding by Ceding","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on philanthropy reinforcing the economic system","quote":"This video with Anand Giridharadas is also great","text":"\"These new philanthropists bring to charity an “entrepreneurial disposition”, Hay and Muller wrote in a 2014 paper, yet one that they suggest has been “diverting attention and resources away from the failings of contemporary manifestations of capitalism”, and may also be serving as a substitute for public spending withdrawn by the state. Essentially, what we are witnessing is the transfer of responsibility for public goods and services from democratic institutions to the wealthy, to be administered by an executive class. In the CEO society, the exercise of social responsibilities is no longer debated in terms of whether corporations should or shouldn’t be responsible for more than their own business interests. Instead, it is about how philanthropy can be used to reinforce a politico-economic system that enables such a small number of people to accumulate obscene amounts of wealth. Zuckerberg’s investment in solutions to the Bay Area housing crisis is an example of this broader trend.\" [1] Although with your \"in excruciating detail\" it sounds like you've been presented with the evidence before, yet you are somehow unwilling to admit the failings of the system. This last part is meant as an observation and not as a negative value judgement. This video with Anand Giridharadas is also great: [VIDEO] , as well as his book 'Winners Take All: The Elite Charade of Changing the World'. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":26602202,"author":"Shacklz","month":"2021-03","story":"Elite philanthropy mainly self-serving","stance":"rec","why":"Google talk on the book that ruffled feathers","quote":"got a bit of notoriety since he apparently ruffled a few feathers there.","text":"Anand Ghiridaradas' Book \"Winners Take All: The Elite Charade of Changing the World\" is very insightful in that regard. He held a google talk ( [VIDEO] ) about it that got a bit of notoriety since he apparently ruffled a few feathers there."},{"id":19357371,"author":"Shacklz","month":"2019-03","story":"GoFundMe CEO: ‘Gigantic Gaps’ in Health System Showing Up in Crowdfunding","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the topic, notable for being given at Google","quote":"For those interested, he held a Talk at Google about this topic","text":"For those interested, he held a Talk at Google about this topic: [VIDEO] I thought it rather interesting that he was able to hold this kind of talk at Google - after all, his audience there probably consists of people that are probably part of the \"winners\" he is talking about."},{"id":18994103,"author":"sleazy_b","month":"2019-01","story":"Down with philanthrocapitalism, says an entertaining polemic","stance":"rec","why":"Talk version of the book, pretty good","quote":"For those interested he gave a talk at Google in Cambridge that was pretty good.","text":"I read this book and, to be honest, did not enjoy it very much. It consists largely of profiles of people involved in this sort of philanthrocapitalism. The main theme seems to be that the ultra-wealthy have coopted the work of social institutions in such a way as to undermine efforts for truly revolutionary change, that is change that might threaten them. The premise is interesting but the content is pretty boring. I got tired of reading about people whom the author believes to be mislead or malicious or otherwise on the wrong path. For those interested he gave a talk at Google in Cambridge that was pretty good. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18784025,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-12","story":"Adding Up the Cost of Tax Breaks for Big Tech’s Data Centers (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how win-win framing narrows the conversation","quote":"Anand Giridharadas recently gave an a great talk[1] (at Google!)","text":"> “win win” (company gets subsidy, area gets jobs) Anand Giridharadas recently gave an a great talk[1] (at Google!) in which he explains how \"win win\" is a charade used to set the framing of the conversation. Framing a plan (like a subsidy) as something that benefits everyone has good optics, but it implicitly creates a framing that only considers that plan. (it's either this plan or \"you get nothing\"). Without the restricted framing, the choice isn't a binary \"subsidies or nothing\"; there are many ways that money could be spent. (e.g. maybe going without that business and investing in education or infrastructure would provide a greater benefit). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18347654,"author":"IBM","month":"2018-10","story":"An Alternative History of Silicon Valley Disruption","stance":"rec","why":"Argues tech thinks it is the pirates but is the navy","quote":"I think you'll like this talk by Anand Giridharadas","text":"I think you'll like this talk by Anand Giridharadas [1]. He makes the point that the tech industry still thinks they're the pirates and don't realize that they're the navy. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36792880,"author":"Shacklz","month":"2023-07","story":"Carnegie Mellon, University of Pittsburgh end use of legacy status in admissions","stance":"reference","why":"Dissects how wealth redistribution occurs via elite channels","quote":"see also his infamous google talk","text":"> This is, in effect, one of the most common ways wealth is redistributed in the US. Why is it so bad? Because the government could simply tax those with wealth more and use taxation as a means of redistribution. Like most western countries do. Anand Giridharadas dissects this topic rather convincingly in his \"Winners Take All\", see also his infamous google talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25474825,"author":"brigandish","month":"2020-12","story":"The YouTube ban is un-American, wrong, and will backfire","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for point on how winners' giving protects the system","quote":"","text":"> And finally, the word “censorship”. This is an accusatory word used by people who think it relates to their non-existent “freedom of speech”. The first amendment applies to the agreement between the US government and its citizens. It does not apply to your relationship with YouTube. > YouTube is a corporation that has an entirely separate agreement with its users, which does not include freedom of speech. Therefore, it’s not censorship — by definition. The first amendment and freedom of speech are not synonyms, quite obviously because there can be freedom of speech outside of the jurisdiction of the US government; and similarly, Youtube is a trans-national corporation with more than just US citizens using it. Even if it were wholly within US jurisdiction and Youtube only served US citizens, the first amendment is still not a synonym for freedom of speech. The word censor comes from a Latin word for a governmental position in ancient Rome but it does not follow that all censorship is therefore only possible or enacted by government. Not only is there no supporting logic for that, it's not evident in practice . Companies like Twitter[1], Facebook[2] and Google[3] remove things from their platforms for political convenience (or, as Anand Giridharadas points out[4], for any convenience): > When you look at the ways in which the winners of our age give back, help out, make a difference, they are often designed to protect the system - above all - that the winners stand on top of."},{"id":19622537,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2019-04","story":"The Dutch East India Co was richer than Apple, Google, Facebook combined (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"One of many links in a list on elite power","quote":"","text":"VOC (Dutch East India Company) was more powerful than most nations at the time. I asked this question on Quora once and got interesting answers [0]. VOC was an incredible enterprise, and there are a few today too, imo, they exist as conglomerates (Samsung, P&G, Amazon), political entities (CIA, CPC), monopolies (Maersk, Google), syndicates (DeBeers [1], NeoAristocrats [2], BigPharma, BigBank, BigSugar, BigOil), and cults (ISIS). These select few have an immeasurable and uncontrolled sway over the globe-- its environment [3], its inhabitants [4], its future [5], its past [6], its present [7]. The greed unrelenting [8], the game rigged [9][10]. -- [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK] [8] [LINK] [9] [LINK] [10] [LINK] edit: links"},{"id":19158664,"author":"ardy42","month":"2019-02","story":"For Dell’s Billionaire CEO, Taxing the Ultra-Rich Is a Joke","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument that billionaire philanthropy avoids hurting billionaires","quote":"billionaire philanthropy probably wont tackle the problems or solutions that will help society but hurt billionaires.","text":"> Just because some billionares use their money in good faith, how many of the >5.000.000$/year behave the same? Also billionaire philanthropy probably wont tackle the problems or solutions that will help society but hurt billionaires. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"t1MqJPHxy6g":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an illuminating, eye-opening long-form take on status, luck and how we judge the successful and unsuccessful.","q":{"x":"This doc is eye-opening","i":19562700,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":27674174,"author":"ArnoVW","month":"2021-06","story":"We moderns are status-drunk","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Status Anxiety documentary with gems like vicar versus TV preacher","quote":"I can highly recommend the book (and documentary)","text":"If you found this article interesting, I can highly recommend the book (and documentary) \"Status Anxiety\", by modern philosopher Alain de Botton. The video has a couple of gems, such as the comparisson between an english country-side vicar, and an american TV preacher. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20034553,"author":"netsharc","month":"2019-05","story":"Advertising as a source of dissatisfaction: cross-national evidence","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on status and recognition","quote":"This (2+ hours) documentary about status is quite eye opening","text":"Luxury goods are status symbols, we buy them because we crave status because deep down we crave recognition from others in society. So I wonder if urbanization/the trend towards more loneliness is the problem. If you have a tightly-knit community, you'd have people who appreciate you because of your character, sense of humor or good company; not because you drive a fancy car. And nowadays we don't really have real communities anymore, just polarization through the Internet, where people try to feel better about themselves by putting down others. This (2+ hours) documentary about status is quite eye opening: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19562700,"author":"netsharc","month":"2019-04","story":"A philosophy-quoting homeless man in China has fled internet fame","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why people seek status and fame","quote":"This doc is eye-opening","text":"This doc is eye-opening: [VIDEO] (although it's over 2 hours and I fell asleep the first time I watched it). but TL;DW: People are always looking for status, because status means others acknowledge and praise them. And today's celebrity culture means being famous is seemingly a quick way to gain status. With a better community you'd have friends who like you and acknowledge you because they got to know you and your character."},{"id":49207721,"author":"netsharc","month":"2026-08","story":"Obviously, DOGE Did Not Save Money","stance":"rec","why":"Segment on work ethic and judging the unsuccessful","quote":"A relevant segment on a 2h+ documentary","text":"If only the government thought like this, instead of blaming those family members of being \"lazy welfare queens\"... A relevant segment on a 2h+ documentary: [VIDEO] Or before that: [VIDEO] If you've absorbed the American teaching that \"if you just work hard you'll be successful\", then you'd have to believe the opposite: \"if you're not successful that means you're lazy\"."},{"id":45929515,"author":"netsharc","month":"2025-11","story":"Being poor vs. being broke","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary partly on hard work and luck, from 28m","quote":"This 2+ hour documentary partly talks about it, in particular from ~28m","text":"Too many people buy the American dream of \"If you just work hard enough, you'll be successful.\". If you believe this, then you'd have to believe the opposite: \"If you're poor and unsucessful, that means you didn't work hard enough, you must be lazy.\". And too many trust-fund kids or kids from rich parents who could afford to send them to expensive schools (or rich enough to live in a district with a well-funded school) dismiss their luck and believe \"I'm successful, that must mean I've been a diligent and smart worker.\". Also, beware of survival bias, most of people in here will have similar paths (born with smarts, good education, high-paying IT job, great success) and probably have similar beliefs about hard work and luck... This 2+ hour documentary partly talks about it, in particular from ~28m: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34445221,"author":"netsharc","month":"2023-01","story":"Money Can’t Buy Happiness. It Can’t Even Buy Status, a New Book Says","stance":"rec","why":"Long documentary on status and meritocracy","quote":"Interesting long-ass documentary about status","text":"I skimmed through the review, it seems it's skewering this book and the arguments it's trying to make. But curious, if other people see expensive cars as status, me showing up in a fancy car means me showing up with status. If I'm female and I show up with a Louis Vuitton bag, that also means showing up with status, but with a Michael Kors bag? As a female friend told me a few years ago \"Ewww, everyone buys that brand, it's now 'cheap'.\"... Interesting long-ass documentary about status: [VIDEO] , but also about the \"meritocracy\" and the US' idea of \"If you work hard you'll be successful\" which imply the flip of it: \"If you're poor, that means you're lazy\"."},{"id":32493719,"author":"netsharc","month":"2022-08","story":"General belief in a just world is associated with dishonest behavior (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Longer documentary on meritocracy beliefs","quote":"or even a longer documentary","text":"Well, the US is a fine example of your realization. People like to preach \"You just need work hard and you'll be successful.\". If you believe that, then on the flip side you'd have to believe, \"Those poor people are poor because they're lazy.\". To me that's why there's a lot of people screaming and yelling about \"Handouts for the poor!\". A good video about it: [LINK] , or even a longer documentary: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21629967,"author":"netsharc","month":"2019-11","story":"Greensboro Man Creates Dating App Where He's the Only Guy Allowed","stance":"rec","why":"Illuminating documentary about status","quote":"Alain de Botton has an illuminating documentary about status","text":"Hah, just like in that Black Mirror episode, where the main character avoids interacting with the waiter with a 3.x rating for fear of being judged by others as someone of lower status (score). Alain de Botton has an illuminating documentary about status (2h30m version: [VIDEO] , 5m version: [LINK] )"},{"id":14536552,"author":"netsharc","month":"2017-06","story":"Stop Pretending You’re Not Rich","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on meritocracy and status","quote":"I find Alain de Botton's essays enlightening, here's a 2+ hour documentary about it","text":"Meritocracy is an interesting \"religion\", which we unconsciously believe in, and one can probably argue is the basis of the Republican ideology. \"You're poor? That must mean you're lazy!\" I find Alain de Botton's essays enlightening, here's a 2+ hour documentary about it: [VIDEO] , and a 4 minute summary: [LINK]"},{"id":13045388,"author":"flycaliguy","month":"2016-11","story":"The Need to Read","stance":"rec","why":"Alain de Botton documentary on status","quote":"May I suggest you watch Alain de Botton's Status Anxiety next time?","text":"May I suggest you watch Alain de Botton's Status Anxiety next time? Might hit two birds with one stone. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10069852,"author":"shenanigoat","month":"2015-08","story":"Why we travel","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary condensing Status Anxiety book","quote":"For those who want to condense the reading experience, here is a 2.5 hr documentary version","text":"Agreed. I also really enjoyed Status Anxiety. For those who want to condense the reading experience, here is a 2.5 hr documentary version [VIDEO] ."},{"id":31558321,"author":"netsharc","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: Can you imagine a world where anybody could do any job?","stance":"reference","why":"Long documentary on status anxiety","quote":"After watching this (long) documentary about status anxiety[1]","text":"It'd be interesting if you had a brain tumor and could just ask your neighbor to operate it out - but I guess you'd still need to rent an operating room. I guess humans would be like general purpose computers that you can program to do anything (let's assume all of the computers are equally specced). You said \"entertainer\", so it makes me wonder if people will still be wowed by acting or singing performances if they could also do it as competently. Would humans have to be like Borg units in this scenario? But to return to the labor market, I guess no one get's paid 6 figures because they're \"one of the few\" that can architect the JSON for a webapp for some food delivery service, everyone can do that! People would still buy food because maybe not everyone wants to be a farmer, and people would still buy plane tickets because there are not enough resources to build everyone a plane (or an operating room). After watching this (long) documentary about status anxiety[1] (or shorter summary: [2]) I like to see things from that perspective. Would the labor market have financial advisors if we're all competent at managing our wealth, would we even be chasing wealth (which we mostly do because IMO it's a status symbol) if we're all equal? [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27222968,"author":"bellyfullofbac","month":"2021-05","story":"The End of Retirement (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Long documentary cited on work ethic and luck","quote":"Here's a 143 minute citation","text":"Here's a 143 minute citation: [VIDEO] Or a 6 minute one: [LINK] TL; DW: the flip side of the American/Protestant work ethic which says \"Work hard and you'll surely succeed\" is that then you'd have to logically view that the people who are failing/have failed in life are lazy. The reality is it's mostly luck, good or bad, that make you a billionaire or living in a van at 80. At least this logic is common among people who say \"We don't want a welfare state, that's basically state-approved theft of my money by welfare queens.\". On the topic of status, I can see why racists are threatened by a more equal society: it would mean the black family would be just well-off as them, and it would suck to not feel superior to \"those lazy people\". I guess this isn't much of a citation..."}],"r":[["LSxbc1IN9Gg",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["Q2aEzeMDHMA",1]]},"KkpqA8yG9T4":{"wy":"HN points to it as the grounded, approachable explanation of why REBCO magnets changed the outlook for fusion.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching Dr. Whyte's talks on YouTube, he discusses the challenges, design, and performance in an approachable way.","i":29687202,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29687202,"author":"maccam94","month":"2021-12","story":"After years of doubts, hopes grow that nuclear fusion is near","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Approachable talk on fusion challenges, design, and performance","quote":"I highly recommend watching Dr. Whyte's talks on YouTube, he discusses the challenges, design, and performance in an approachable way.","text":"Commonwealth Fusion Systems has addressed all of those issues: 1. The higher field strength from the new HTS magnets eliminates turbulence. 2. The ARC reactor will use a FLiBe molten salt blanket to capture energy from neutrons and breed tritium to be used in the fusion reaction. 3. The ARC reactor is designed to have a swappable 2cm thick vacuum chamber, which is the component subjected to the most neutron radiation (everything else is shielded by the molten salt blanket). These chambers are expected to last a year, and while they are moderately radioactive waste, the amount of material is relatively small and they should become safe in the order of a decade. I highly recommend watching Dr. Whyte's talks on YouTube, he discusses the challenges, design, and performance in an approachable way. There's a timestamp index in the comments on this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29094837,"author":"runnerup","month":"2021-11","story":"China to build 150 nuclear reactors- more than rest of world over last 35 years","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Walks through fusion performance curves, state of research, and small reactors","quote":"If you haven't watched this yet, it's definitely worth your time.","text":"> “People say nuclear is expensive in the West, but they forget to say it’s expensive because of interest rates,” Morin said. It's still a valid criticism if: - They could get those high interest rates from the return on other capital projects (nuclear less return than something else) - All externalities are included in costs We only have so much labor/material/energy available, and we do have to have some way of allocating them to the things with the most return. The big issue, obviously, is that tons and tons of negative externalities aren't priced in to most economic endeavors, that might make fission look a lot better. It might make it look a lot worse. Personally I'm hoping Elon/Mackenzie/Gates/Buffet/etc decide to throw a few dozen billion at fusion. I think its time truly is nigh. If you haven't watched this yet, it's definitely worth your time. Rather than being a super opinionated think-piece, it instead does a fantastic job walking the viewer through: - high level nuclear fusion engineering performance curves / the engineering design contraint space - the current state of nuclear fusion research (as of 5 years ago) and - breaks down why some new smaller reactors will likely begin construction soon and counter-intuitively, the most recently started ones may be completed before a fusion reactor that first broke ground 25 years ago and is still very much being actively built [VIDEO] Also worth noting that interesting things happen when you assume you can have \"large\" a"},{"id":25264106,"author":"cromwellian","month":"2020-12","story":"South Korea's fusion device KSTAR runs for 20 seconds at 100M degrees Celsius","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues fusion is now an engineering problem thanks to REBCO magnets","quote":"Watch Robert Whyte's presentation.","text":"Watch Robert Whyte's presentation. [VIDEO] The basic problems have all been solved, the science has been solved too. Tokamaks have already made net fusion power. The only difference between JET, which already succeeded, and ARC, is using Magnets with 2.6x the field strength. The B^4 power scaling, gives you about 50x the power, so ARC will generate 500 MW of power whereas JET (remember, already proven engineering), generated 10MW. So without a doubt, this is just an engineering problem at this point. A prototype device that didn't have to be maintained, could be built exactly like any other Tokamak, just with REBCO magnets, and you'd have the required fusion power. To get the required electric power, and add in maintenance, you need to at the FliBe liquid blanket, replacable toroid sections, and a heat exchanger, but again, these are known engineering problems with known solutions, akin to knowing that landing a rocket is possible, but figuring out the grid fins, heat shield, landing legs, and guidance software. Fusion devices on a small scale have been proving the plasma physics since the 90s. We don't have fusion yet simply because of people being conservative and sticking with traditional \"known to work\" magnets, which is why ITER is a huge, hulking monstrosity. As Dr Whyte says, cost scales by R, R can be reduced by a factor of 10 from ITER simply by using different magnets, so ARC will produce the same power as ITER, but significantly smaller and cheaper. The basic paper"},{"id":40762444,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2024-06","story":"HH70, the first high-temperature superconducting Tokamak achieves first plasma","stance":"rec","why":"Early lectures on high-field superconducting tokamak approach","quote":"I encourage anyone curious to look up videos on SPARC on YouTube. It’s very encouraging!","text":"I’ve been following them since they were giving promising lectures at MIT and I absolutely think they have the most solid approach! Tokamaks are well understood, they supposedly have the same plasma physics as ITER which has been heavily scrutinized and supported by work at JET, and their concept is simple - Tokamak but with very high field superconducting magnets using technology that wasn’t available when ITER was conceived, and apparently higher field strengths mean a smaller reactor for the same power gains. As a lay person the story is simple and that’s good! Then they demonstrated their magnets and got $2B in funding and now they’re deep in the construction phase for SPARC. I encourage anyone curious to look up videos on SPARC on YouTube. It’s very encouraging! It seems honestly very reasonable that they will see sustained net energy gain for their entire power plant before 2030 (tho SPARC is still a demonstrator not designed for continuous service, so “sustained” means like one minute). Here’s some videos: 8 years ago: [VIDEO] 2 years ago: [VIDEO] Latest update posted yesterday: [LINK]"},{"id":35968074,"author":"reaperman","month":"2023-05","story":"Renewables supplied 65% of new US utility-scale generating capacity in Q1 2023","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing breakdown of fusion physics with predictions that came true","quote":"it provides an amazing breakdown of the physics of fusion energy.","text":"In total a land area about the quarter of the size of California was contaminated with over 37 becquerel of radiation due to Chernobyl, including 10,000 farms in the UK alone. The solitary story was to get people to read the book. The debate is if fission is sustainable for the environment. I talked about contaminated agriculture and how much that was covered up and missing in the statistics — people still living in contaminated land and eating contaminated food today. “The huge numbers of people” is what I’m concerned about and why I advocate for wind and solar. I’m not in favor of the fossil fuel status quo. The radiation and pollution from coal ash and effects of global warming on agriculture are massive. But that’s not an argument for “fission is ecologically sustainable”, that’s an argument for “even though it’s not, we should still build dams and fission power plants anyways”. Which is a fine argument. It’s just a different one. A fission disaster or attack on a fission plant can take half of Californias agriculture out of the picture over night. You can’t say fission and wind have similar risk at the tail ends. Yeah, statistically it hasn’t happened, but the risk exists. Besides, new fission plants will take 20 years to build and by then we'll have actually clean baseload energy from fusion reactors. This video[0] is from 7 years ago, but it provides an amazing breakdown of the physics of fusion energy. The timeline and physics predicted in the lecture have come to pas"},{"id":26360904,"author":"hangonhn","month":"2021-03","story":"Fusion startup plans reactor with small but powerful superconducting magnets","stance":"rec","why":"MIT talk on a startup-style approach to the reactor","quote":"The MIT Club of Northern California posted a video a few years ago on this exact topic and reactor","text":"The MIT Club of Northern California posted a video a few years ago on this exact topic and reactor: [VIDEO] This thing came out of MIT, at least according to the video, and was really the collective efforts of a bunch of MIT grad students who made the breakthrough partially by taking a very Silicon Valley startup approach of using off-the-shelf parts, experimenting with new ideas, and starting small. I don't know if Professor Whyte framed it that way to appeal to the crowd or not."},{"id":24631317,"author":"DesiLurker","month":"2020-09","story":"Compact nuclear fusion reactor is 'very likely to work,' studies suggest","stance":"rec","why":"Original presentation on SPARC and new high-field magnets","quote":"he talks about the impact of these newer magnets at around 26min mark.","text":"yes thats true. ITER is obsolete already because it actually cannot use these high-temp-high-Tesla superconductors. at the moment ITERs best hope is that it 'proves' that fusion is viable & then do a full redesign for DEMO to do proper scaled operation. here is the original presentation by Dr Whyte of MIT behind this Sparc project: [VIDEO] he talks about the impact of these newer magnets at around 26min mark."},{"id":17472791,"author":"mastax","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: What talks or videos do you suggest to watch on a long flight?","stance":"rec","why":"Grounded talk on different approaches to scaling fusion","quote":"it's a very interesting and grounded talk about the different approaches to scaling fusion energy.","text":"\"Breakthrough in Nuclear Fusion?\" - Professor Dennis Whyte. [VIDEO] The title threw me off, but it's a very interesting and grounded talk about the different approaches to scaling fusion energy."},{"id":16685636,"author":"scottmsul","month":"2018-03","story":"Lockheed Martin has obtained a patent for a compact fusion reactor","stance":"rec","why":"Explains superconductor breakthrough enabling viable tokamak","quote":"The real deal is here, from MIT","text":"The real deal is here, from MIT: [VIDEO] Apparently there was a recent breakthrough in superconductors, which allows significantly more current in the inductor coils while still maintaining superconductivity. This in turn allows for much stronger magnetic fields, hence tighter confinement of the plasma and therefore more fusion. A standard tokamak with these new superconductors should produce more energy out than energy in, and be a viable source of energy."},{"id":16687439,"author":"JumpCrisscross","month":"2018-03","story":"Lockheed Martin has obtained a patent for a compact fusion reactor","stance":"rec","why":"Chart on fusion power growth and why better magnets change cost","quote":"Eye opener is the chart at 18:03","text":"Eye opener is the chart at 18:03: \"from 1970 to 1995, [fusion] power and energy increased much faster than computing power (Moore's Law)\" [1]. Magnetic-confinement fusion stalled, according to Dr. Whyte, because to get more power (and efficiency) we need stronger fields. To get stronger fields we needed bigger magnets. To support bigger magnets one needs bigger structures. Big magnets and big structures will absorb more magnetic and neutron flux; that increases maintenance frequency, complexity and cost. Better superconductors let us get stronger fields without bigger magnets. That changes the cost function. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15631428,"author":"maxharris","month":"2017-11","story":"For the love of Earth, stop traveling","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as a positive thing to put energy toward","quote":"why not put your energy toward something positive, like this?","text":"Instead of trying to reduce options for people, why not put your energy toward something positive, like this? [VIDEO]"},{"id":37149855,"author":"Gud","month":"2023-08","story":"Carl Sagan testifying before Congress in 1985 on climate change [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows CFS on track with prototype reactor","quote":"However they have been on track for a while now.","text":"Any solution to climate change will take decades to implement. There is no turning this around quickly. CFS are working on their first prototype reactor to be finished within a few years, but their factory to begin mass producing will take another decade. It is of course possible CFS will fail. However they have been on track for a while now. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31680940,"author":"DennisP","month":"2022-06","story":"Progress toward fusion energy breakeven measured against Lawson criterion","stance":"reference","why":"Head of MIT's fusion program contradicting a claim","quote":"here's the head of MIT's fusion program, claiming the exact opposite","text":"Can you provide a source for that? Because here's the head of MIT's fusion program, claiming the exact opposite: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30281963,"author":"dkbrk","month":"2022-02","story":"Major breakthrough on nuclear fusion energy","stance":"reference","why":"Whyte explains high fields tame plasma instabilities","quote":"Here's the section in Professor Whyte's talk","text":"I understand there's a bit more to it than that. Here's the section in Professor Whyte's talk: [VIDEO] > It's even more subtle than that, in fact this is really one of the things we've studies at MIT, is that there's other things that come in terms of benefits, particularly when you make the magnetic field very high, it basically starts to tame, just all of the whole suite of plasma instabilities that exist."},{"id":18707054,"author":"matt2000","month":"2018-12","story":"United States should prepare to build a prototype fusion power plant, panel says","stance":"reference","why":"More on making fusion projects smaller and cheaper","quote":"More on that here","text":"I strongly object to the language that's commonly used around fusion research. Much of it unfairly frames the discussion around what would be a world-changing technology if realized. For example, in this article: \"the controversial ITER project, a hugely expensive fusion reactor...\" There is no doubt this is an expensive project, estimated at ~$20B total cost ( [LINK] ) However, in terms of possible upsides or even compared to other projects it's _cheap_. Boston's \"Big Dig\" was $24B for example: [LINK] Which would you rather have? A 1.5m tunnel, or an energy source that would change the future of human civilization? I am being somewhat unfair here since the Big Dig was a case study of mismanagement and cost overruns, but as a society we should be willing to spend significant resources on world changing research instead of say $22.5B on three new boats: [LINK] All that being said, the way forward is probably to figure out ways to make fusion related projects smaller in scale and cost. Then multiple attempts can proceed in parallel without all the organizational and governmental overhead. More on that here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18386608,"author":"noetic_techy","month":"2018-11","story":"A faster, cheaper path to fusion energy","stance":"reference","why":"Source for hurdles: test reactor cost and FLiBe supply","quote":"I've seen the MIT lecture that presented this.","text":"I've seen the MIT lecture that presented this. Here are the main hurdles: 1) Estimated $40 billion USD need to build a test reactor. 2) Not enough FLiBe fluid (Low-Z fluid) on the planet for the reaction chamber. Would need large scale manufacturing. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16550610,"author":"sien","month":"2018-03","story":"Nuclear fusion on brink of being realised, say MIT scientists","stance":"reference","why":"Chart showing fusion progress faster than Moore's law","quote":"The increase in fusion magnetic energy has actually been faster than Moore's law.","text":"Sadly, that joke is at least 30 years old too. There are lots of companies in the game now. Tri Alpha Energy, General Fusion, Lockheed Martin and there are quite a few more. There is the Wendelstein 7X stellarator and ITER. The increase in fusion magnetic energy has actually been faster than Moore's law. See this video at 18:30 [VIDEO] The massive improvements in superconductive magnets are really making a huge impact. Check out ARC and SPARC from MIT. Humanity is going to get usable energy generated from fusion."},{"id":16389357,"author":"tim333","month":"2018-02","story":"ITER is a showcase  for the drawbacks of fusion energy","stance":"reference","why":"MIT video basis for favoring SPARC over ITER","quote":"I had similar thoughts after watching the MIT video","text":"I had similar thoughts after watching the MIT video ( [VIDEO] ). It would seem to make sense to scrap the $20bn ITER abd build MITs proposed $300m SPARC prototype instead [LINK] Quicker to make, cheaper, more modern tech and much closer to a possible commercial design."},{"id":15082732,"author":"visarga","month":"2017-08","story":"Nuclear fusion is on the brink of a milestone, but faces lack of funding [audio]","stance":"reference","why":"Source on REBCO tape enabling smaller, cheaper reactors","quote":"they say we have better superconductors now - the so called REBCO tape","text":"I watched a video about fusion where they say we have better superconductors now - the so called REBCO tape, that are cheap and most importantly - can generate stronger magnetic fields. So they can shrink the reactor 10x and make it cheaper and faster than the ITER project. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13091675,"author":"paulmd","month":"2016-12","story":"Is nuclear fusion poised to deliver?","stance":"reference","why":"MIT fusion director saying energy-positive fusion is close","quote":"MIT director","text":"Fusion being 20 years away was contingent on receiving an appropriate level of funding. Given the funding it's received (below the \"fusion never\" level), it's exactly on track. In fact, if anything it's ahead of \"never\" given the advances in superconducting materials we've made, since the director of MIT's fusion program seems to think that we're now only 5 years away from energy-positive fusion. [LINK] (source: [LINK] ) MIT director: [VIDEO] Manned missions to Mars are also perfectly feasible. Like fusion, we just don't devote enough resources to it. Pretty much anything space-related would be much easier with two simple steps: first, you build a launch loop to drop launch costs. It's all the capabilities of a space elevator but doesn't require any exotic materials. Launch costs are estimated at ~$3 per kg for a 20GW system, figuring $30B construction cost with 5-year payback if you can hit 100% utilization. And no that's not a typo, that's 5 years to profit at $3/kg - where's Elon Musk when you need him, right? :P [LINK] [LINK] Then, you drag an asteroid into orbit and mine it, so you have a bunch of material that's not at the bottom of a gravity well. Both of those steps are in a similar category to a manned mission to Mars - feasible, but would take some serious engineering and serious resources. But once you do them, you also open some serious doors. There is a massive quantity of literally any metal you want in an asteroid. One small rocky asteroid has about 10x the tot"}],"r":[["hx7BXih7zx8",2],["uxykI30fS54",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["EpRrD58Ah3Q",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["-s5szfPYKY4",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["tTBWfkE7BXU",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1],["Ja5Q75hf6QI",1],["OyBNmecVtdU",1],["1uflg7LDmzI",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Z7rd04KzLcg",1],["MQyxG4vTyZ8",1],["zUyH3XhpLTo",1]]},"bglWCuCMSWc":{"wy":"Recommended again and again in traffic-safety threads as an eye-opener on road design versus posted limits and driver behavior.","q":{"x":"it really opened my eyes on the relationship between rules and actual behavior.","i":34345350,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":40268915,"author":"gregmac","month":"2024-05","story":"Bollards: Why and What","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Design speed versus posted limits; the commenter's go-to video","quote":"This video is all I think of when this discussion comes up now","text":"Europe has a lot more roads with a lower design speed. Curves, narrow lanes, on-street parking, trees/poles/etc close to the road. These things cause people to drive slower, because it doesn't feel safe to go fast. In North America, roads are usually built in the complete opposite way, with long straight roads and wide lanes, so the design speed is actually quite high -- even if that wasn't the intent. People go fast, because it feels safe to go that speed, but isn't, because there are pedestrians and turns. We then \"fix\" that shit road design by having low speed limits. This video is all I think of when this discussion comes up now: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40482974,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2024-05","story":"California Senate Passes Bill Requiring Passive Speed Limiters","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great video on US street speed limits and deaths","quote":"Great video on this at","text":"Not much but it’s a start. Unfortunately the US treats every street and road like it’s a country road when it comes to speed limits, with a lot of deaths as a result. Great video on this at [VIDEO]"},{"id":34345350,"author":"noirdujour","month":"2023-01","story":"America’s roads and highways have a big, fat style guide","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Opened commenter's eyes on rules versus actual driving behavior","quote":"it really opened my eyes on the relationship between rules and actual behavior.","text":"> MUTCD designs streets similarly to rural highways, and this can create conditions where people speed when they need to be driving slowly, because the design of the street doesn't reflect the speed the sign says you need to be driving. Not Just Bikes covers this shortfall in a video on speed limits[1], and it really opened my eyes on the relationship between rules and actual behavior. Essentially, speed limit signs affect driving speeds slightly, but the street design is important. Wide, open, straight roads naturally prompt drivers to drive to highway speeds, even in more suburb areas. It's amazing how often I check the speedometer and find that I'm driving much more than the posted speed limit when following the flow of traffic. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30814535,"author":"gregmac","month":"2022-03","story":"New speed camera ‘detect’ vehicles braking as they approach","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Profoundly changed commenter's view of speed limits and road design","quote":"that profoundly affected my opinion on this stuff.","text":"I think my first intro to this was from a Not Just Bikes video on speed limits [1], and that profoundly affected my opinion on this stuff. I now think a lot of road design is just utterly broken, and it includes speed limits, what that channel (and other related) refer to as \"stroads\", and intersections (roundabouts vs traffic lights). That video in particular is highly relevant to the speed camera thing. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42699055,"author":"TomK32","month":"2025-01","story":"Making an intersection unsafe for pedestrians to save seconds for drivers","stance":"rec","why":"Road design shapes good or bad driving","quote":"The road design is what causes bad or good driving.","text":"The road design is what causes bad or good driving. The road you describe should narrow before the blind curve so the drivers would (often enough unconsciously) slow down before it. For the pedestrian crossing, small islands that separate the lanes and give pedestrians a safe space will help. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37911984,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2023-10","story":"Wales' 20mph speed limit: How has the first month gone?","stance":"rec","why":"Street design, not limits, determines driver speed","quote":"If you want drivers to go 20mph, you need to design the street so going any faster feels uncomfortable.","text":"As much as the comments from complaining drivers are frustrating, it reveals that the problem here is relying on speed limits and not street design. If you want drivers to go 20mph, you need to design the street so going any faster feels uncomfortable. [VIDEO] discuses this. Also, I notice that most of those drivers complaining don't address what it's like to have a small child who's a runner and is always trying to get loose of your grip and run in to the street."},{"id":33768362,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2022-11","story":"The Exceptionally American Problem of Rising Roadway Deaths","stance":"rec","why":"Channel covers road, pedestrian and bike safety extensively","quote":"Not Just Bikes on YouTube covers road/pedestrian/bike safety extensively.","text":"Not Just Bikes on YouTube covers road/pedestrian/bike safety extensively. [LINK] Specific videos: Crossing the street should be safer: [LINK] How to quickly build a cycling city: [LINK] Wasting money on car infrastructure: [LINK] The wrong way to set speed limits: [VIDEO] Safe cycling: [LINK] \"Stroads\" are dangerous: [LINK]"},{"id":33214330,"author":"YawningAngel","month":"2022-10","story":"Even after $100B, self-driving cars are going nowhere","stance":"rec","why":"Drivers follow road design cues, not posted limits","quote":"goes into detail on this","text":"That's not really how speed limits work though. People take their cues from road design and conditions, not what the speed limit actually is. Virtually changing speed limits is dangerous because it causes you to think you are altering the behaviour of traffic without actually doing so. [VIDEO] goes into detail on this"},{"id":32806500,"author":"occz","month":"2022-09","story":"Heavier cars are safer for their drivers, but far deadlier for everyone else","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant video on why people ignore speed limits","quote":"Relevant Not Just Bikes-video on the subject","text":"People don't follow speed limits, regardless of how literate they are. Relevant Not Just Bikes-video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32806588,"author":"ben-schaaf","month":"2022-09","story":"Heavier cars are safer for their drivers, but far deadlier for everyone else","stance":"rec","why":"Speed limits barely change speeds; road design does","quote":"Not Just Bikes has a great video covering this","text":"Not Just Bikes has a great video covering this: [VIDEO] TLDW: People don't obsessively check their speedometer and thus changing the speed limit largely doesn't affect the speed people drive at. To effectively change that you need to change the road design."},{"id":28991677,"author":"xputer","month":"2021-10","story":"Car accidents cause death, injury and trauma. Why do we shrug them off?","stance":"rec","why":"Road design should slow drivers naturally, not rely on signs","quote":"Our roads should be designed with human error in mind.","text":"Sorry but this is just so wrong. American road design is super predicated on personal responsibility. We design roads that are as wide as freeways and then put a little sign on the side that says 35mph speed limit. What we need is less focus on personal responsibility and accept that people make mistakes, we're all human after all. Our roads should be designed with human error in mind. The road design itself should make it natural for drivers to slow down near dangerous areas. See this video for example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29009011,"author":"xputer","month":"2021-10","story":"Car accidents cause death, injury and trauma. Why do we shrug them off?","stance":"rec","why":"Deliberately slowing cars through road design","quote":"It's about deliberately slowing cars down where they need to, through the road design.","text":"It's not about making the roads more narrow or winding per se. It's about deliberately slowing cars down where they need to, through the road design. See this video for example: [VIDEO] Besides that, roads in the US are generally poorly classified. They're often trying to both function as both high throughput roads and destination serving streets. Such hybrid roads are known as \"stroads\" and they're bad for everyone involved. Stroads are bad for moving cars fast due to the many conflict points (driveways, intersections, etc.) that force drivers to constantly slow down. They are also bad at serving destinations because the many conflict points make stroads very dangerous, especially if you're not in a car. See this video for example: [LINK]"},{"id":33603721,"author":"xracy","month":"2022-11","story":"Speed traps have no long-term effect on speeding: study","stance":"reference","why":"Narrower lanes slow traffic more than enforcement","quote":"The way to make traffic slower, is to make road lanes narrower.","text":"[VIDEO] This feels like doubling down on something that's not super effective. The way to make traffic slower, is to make road lanes narrower. You can also add curves/trees or overhangs to add shadows to the road, which breaks up the roadway and makes it harder to reason about from a distance."}],"r":[["oHlpmxLTxpw",2],["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["Ds-v2-qyCc8",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["Ot4qdCs54ZE",1],["YHLU0Uej1WA",1],["REni8Oi1QJQ",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["r-TuGAHR78w",1]]},"ZmNOAtZIgIk":{"wy":"HN commenters recommended it for years as a simple, exciting overview of the deep learning wave.","q":{"x":"he explains very simply how all this works for images and some examples about the audio case.","i":4146995,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":5528222,"author":"michael_nielsen","month":"2013-04","story":"Neural Network Visualisation in Clojure","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizes hierarchy of features learned by successive deep net layers.","quote":"Andrew Ng has a fantastic example of visualizing deep belief nets","text":"Andrew Ng has a fantastic example of visualizing deep belief nets: [VIDEO] The example starts at about 18:20. You really need to have watched the previous material to understand it, but the basic idea is that he's plotting the hierarchy of features learned by successive layers of a deep neural net (a sparse autoencoder, IIRC, it's been a while since I watched it)."},{"id":4829309,"author":"pilooch","month":"2012-11","story":"Scientists See Promise in Deep-Learning Programs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Most useful overview of unsupervised feature learning.","quote":"the one overview I've found the most useful is","text":"the one overview I've found the most useful is [VIDEO] (Bay Area Vision Meeting: Unsupervised Feature Learning and Deep Learning by Andrew Ng in April 2011)."},{"id":4403845,"author":"FrojoS","month":"2012-08","story":"Brains, Sex and Machine Learning (Geoffrey Hinton at Google Tech Talks)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"Here is a similar awesome by Andrew Ng","text":"Thanks for pointing this out. Here is a similar awesome by Andrew Ng [VIDEO] who teaches the original Coursea Machine Learning class."},{"id":4146995,"author":"brutuscat","month":"2012-06","story":"Microsoft Research make breakthrough in audio speech recognition","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Simple 40 minute explanation of deep learning for images and audio.","quote":"he explains very simply how all this works for images and some examples about the audio case.","text":"This seems very related to this [VIDEO] speak by Andrew Ng. It is a 40min speak, but he explains very simply how all this works for images and some examples about the audio case. It is incredible how using this deep learning techniques we can teach this \"neural networks\" to recognize such complicated patterns. It is like reverse engineering the brain's algorithms. BTW I took his Coursera's course about Machine Learning and it was great! I also recommend it A LOT to gather basic ML knowledge."},{"id":4163788,"author":"kevin1024","month":"2012-06","story":"Google & Stanford create a digital brain that learns to identify a human face","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Unsupervised feature learning outperforms hand-programmed models.","quote":"Check out this (awesome) talk","text":"Check out this (awesome) talk: [VIDEO] There are a number of areas in which unsupervised feature learning outperforms the hand-programmed models we have been fine-tuning for decades."},{"id":2503855,"author":"apu","month":"2011-05","story":"Neural Networks making a come-back?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Conveys the excitement and potential of unsupervised feature learning.","quote":"Here's a great talk by Andrew Ng of Stanford","text":"A good intro page with lots of links: [LINK] A lot of the newest work is under a few different names: \"deep learning\", \"convolutional deep networks\", \"unsupervised feature learning\", etc. Here's a great talk by Andrew Ng of Stanford, who's a recent convert to this area: [VIDEO] (Note that this is quite a one-sided view of things, but it does convey the excitement of deep-learning researchers and the potential of what might be possible.) Although I'm not in deep learning myself (I'm a computer vision researcher), here's a TL;DR as I understand it: rather than having people in specific domains such as computer vision or speech processing create their own features, the idea is to take raw inputs (pixels in the case of images) and train multi-layer neural net architectures that \"learn\" the relevant higher-level features in an unsupervised way (i.e., without labeled training data). Some of these seem to be pulling out interesting features and perform competitively on a few benchmarks in vision and other fields. I'm not sold on this yet, because it seems like the complexity of designing features has merely been traded in for the complexity of designing different learning architectures, but it's certainly becoming quite popular these days (mostly led by Geoff Hinton of Toronto, Yoshua Bengio of Montreal, and Yann LeCunn of NYU)."},{"id":4146957,"author":"robrenaud","month":"2012-06","story":"Building high-level features using large scale unsupervised learning","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable talk on Ng's state-of-the-art unsupervised learning results.","quote":"Here is a reasonably approachable talk he gave about it.","text":"Andrew Ng has set many state of the art results on various data sets using similar approaches as the one described in the paper. Here is a reasonably approachable talk he gave about it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3220133,"author":"dave_sullivan","month":"2011-11","story":"Stanford computer diagnoses breast cancer more accurately than human doctor","stance":"rec","why":"Current Stanford research on automatic feature learning.","quote":"Here's a video talking about current research into this at Stanford","text":"They kind of hint at it in the article, but sounds like automatic feature detection really helped here. That's really the future (or present): learning algos discovering better features automatically, significantly cutting down the labor involved in feature engineering. Pretty neat. Here's a video talking about current research into this at Stanford: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2665926,"author":"colincsl","month":"2011-06","story":"Andrew Ng: Machine Learning in Robotics","stance":"rec","why":"More in-depth presentation by Andrew Ng on the same subject.","quote":"Here is a more in-depth presentation he gave on the same subject at a Google Tech Talk","text":"Here is a more in-depth presentation he gave on the same subject at a Google Tech Talk: [VIDEO] (April 11, 2011)"},{"id":4435257,"author":"FrojoS","month":"2012-08","story":"Darpa Has Seen the Future of Computing ... And It's Analog","stance":"reference","why":"Example of breakthroughs in neural networks for pattern recognition.","quote":"there are a lot of breakthroughs in ANN's at the moment","text":"> By definition, a computer is a machine that processes and stores data as ones and zeroes. No, of course thats only true for digital computers. Otherwise, what would be the point of the headline? Not a great article but interesting news. I could imagine specialized computer for artificial neural networks (ANN) being commercially successful in the future. Not sure if this is what the people in this DARPA project are working on. As far as I can tell, there are a lot of breakthroughs in ANN's at the moment, especially in the realms of pattern (e.g. image) recognition: Ng: [VIDEO] Hinton: [LINK]"},{"id":4164107,"author":"otoburb","month":"2012-06","story":"Google & Stanford create a digital brain that learns to identify a human face","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Indirectly, yes. The Coursera course teaches K-means clustering as an example of an unsupervised learning algorithm, along with a technique to work with larger data sets in a more manageable way (e.g. PCA to reduce dimensionality). With the basic building blocks from the ML course you have a much better understanding of what Google and Stanford did. Andrew Ng is also the course instructor for the Coursera ML course[1]. I am presuming that supplemental readings and Youtube videos by Prof. Ng (e.g. [VIDEO] ) allows a sufficiently motivated individual to at least partially replicate the results in the OP. [1] Andrew Ng is also a co-founder of Coursera."}],"r":[["DleXA5ADG78",2],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["wjZofJX0v4M",1],["DkS1pkKpILY",1]]},"yiw6_JakZFc":{"wy":"Cited in climate threads to argue that individual action is not enough and that political pressure is needed.","q":{"x":"It's a Kurzgesagt video about why individual action is far from enough, and why we need systemic action.","i":28929903,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32770365,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-09","story":"Planting trees not always an effective way of binding carbon dioxide","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite channel's video on global warming","quote":"One of my favorite channels on YouTube is Kurzgesagt","text":"One of my favorite channels on YouTube is Kurzgesagt and they recently (looking at date, well, last year - still recent in the scale of things) had a video on global warming - Can YOU Fix Climate Change? [VIDEO] There's also Do we Need Nuclear Energy to Stop Climate Change? - [LINK] Is It Too Late To Stop Climate Change? Well, it's Complicated. - [LINK]"},{"id":30243272,"author":"LoveMortuus","month":"2022-02","story":"Satellite finds methane leaks from gas pipelines","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One person's lifetime emissions equal a second of global output","quote":"Kurzgesagt made a really good video about climate change","text":"Kurzgesagt made a really good video about climate change [0]. What stuck with me the most was that if I would eliminate 100% of my emissions in my entire life, I would save 1 second of global energy sector emissions. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28625812,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2021-09","story":"Amazon’s AI cameras are punishing drivers for mistakes they didn’t make","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains lesser-known carbon sources","quote":"This excellent cartoon video explains the seriousness of all the lesser known sources of carbon","text":"You are not representing the seriousness of this situation adequately (in my opinion). This excellent cartoon video explains the seriousness of all the lesser known sources of carbon: [VIDEO] Seems like Chairman Xi is serious about tackling Climate Change so he does not get deposed if the Gobi desert grows and eventually swallows up half of China."},{"id":39734690,"author":"instagib","month":"2024-03","story":"Liebreich: Net Zero Will Be Harder Than You Think – and Easier. Part II: Easier","stance":"rec","why":"Decent video covering concrete and other CO2 sources","quote":"here is a decent video and a follow up more positive one I found.","text":"If you don’t mind YouTube, here is a decent video and a follow up more positive one I found. It does mention concrete and many other forms of co2 production we haven’t figured out yet. 2022 Kurzgesagt in a nutshell. [VIDEO] 2023 less doomerism climate change update: [LINK]"},{"id":35238422,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-03","story":"IPCC climate crisis report delivers ‘final warning on 1.5C’","stance":"rec","why":"Pair with the optimistic follow-up video","quote":"Make sure that you watch the second one if you watch the first one.","text":"From Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell: Can YOU Fix Climate Change? [VIDEO] and We WILL Fix Climate Change! [LINK] Make sure that you watch the second one if you watch the first one."},{"id":30246564,"author":"jstx1","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Do you care about your carbon footprint?","stance":"rec","why":"Personal footprint is the wrong focus","quote":"Kurzgesagt also provided a very good view on this that I agree with","text":"No. > The use of household carbon footprint calculators originated when oil producer BP hired Ogilvy to create an \"effective propaganda\" campaign to shift responsibility of climate change-causing pollution away from the corporations and institutions that created a society where carbon emissions are unavoidable and onto personal lifestyle choices. [LINK] Kurzgesagt also provided a very good view on this that I agree with - [VIDEO] - \"Can YOU Fix Climate Change?\" (No*). The video explains it much better than I could in a comment but if you're concerned about your own contribution, you're focusing on the wrong thing."},{"id":29588746,"author":"anshumankmr","month":"2021-12","story":"A ‘false solution’? How crypto mining became the oil industry’s new hope","stance":"rec","why":"Pop science treatment of climate responsibility","quote":"","text":">Cryptocurrencies such as bitcoin, the most-popular decentralized digital currency, have a notoriously large carbon footprint (bitcoin mining alone consumes about half as much electricity in a year as all of the UK). So to leverage a cheap source of energy to run their bitcoin mining operations, Lochmiller and Cavness found themselves partnering with oil companies to repurpose a byproduct, primarily methane, that’s typically vented or burnt off in flares. Aren't crypto miners using GPUs to \"mine\" bitcoins? Doesn't that also mean every person who plays video games is also responsible for stressing the power generation system. And as GPUs become more powerful to give better frame rates and fancy tech like RTX, they also become more power hungry. Doesn't that also mean the massive data centers like the what Google is running for Stadia with massive number of GPUs is responsible for taxing the power generation system? Or people who train Deep Learning models with billion of parameters on GPUs? I do see people pointing fingers at Deep Learning and how it isn't \"carbon neutral\" but the other things are not really talked about. That being said, I am not people/companies who use GPUs aren't the cause of global warming or are somehow singlehandedly making it worse I believe that as a society we are kind of involved in this. Finger pointing will really keep going in circles. I am not the biggest fan of pop science as it often oversimplifies problems but this video by Kurzgesakt is very"},{"id":29111193,"author":"bellyfullofbac","month":"2021-11","story":"The Pentagon’s view of climate change and the risks of state collapse","stance":"rec","why":"Answer is political pressure, not individual carbon cutting","quote":"the answer can only be political pressure","text":"Kurzgesagt made a video about it, and they say, and I agree with them, the answer can only be political pressure: [VIDEO] They also mentioned the stat that if 1 person lived totally carbon neutral for 70 years, that would offset only 1 second of current global CO2 emissions, which I'm going to presume is sourced properly. I'm not very optimistic. Politicians follow the polls and go with what's popular, and if the people living in the rich nations have to pay more to fly to Asia for cheap holidays, and iPhones, etc, get more expensive because of carbon tax, the majority probably won't support the governments and will instead vote in populist idiots that will make things \"great again\". In the poorer countries, being told that the decades-old luxuries they've envied from richer nations will cost more because of government policies will also not be popular, never mind that the policies are meant to save the future."},{"id":28857148,"author":"jadams5","month":"2021-10","story":"Climate Clock","stance":"rec","why":"Answers what individuals can do about climate","quote":"Kurzgesagt: Can YOU Fix Climate Change?","text":"Kurzgesagt: Can YOU Fix Climate Change? [VIDEO] Link in response to people asking what everyone is doing, should be doing, and can be doing..."},{"id":28929903,"author":"oblio","month":"2021-10","story":"Carbon-neutrality is a tale: the race for renewables is burning Europe's forests","stance":"rec","why":"Individual action is far from enough; systemic action needed","quote":"It's a Kurzgesagt video about why individual action is far from enough, and why we need systemic action.","text":"That's nice, but the unfortunate thing is: [VIDEO] It's a Kurzgesagt video about why individual action is far from enough, and why we need systemic action. Do your part, but also pressure politicians, because that's where the main pain point is."},{"id":28680515,"author":"y4mi","month":"2021-09","story":"Europe’s energy crisis is about to go global as gas prices soar","stance":"rec","why":"Individual action is not enough; systemic change needed","quote":"","text":"Only in aggregation when everyone else changes their behaviour as well. Each individuals contribution is too tiny to have an impact, which is why we need a systematic change or legislation, so that its no longer a choice/personal responsibility to take action. Kurzgesagt's recent video (sponsored by bill gates notes)e xpressed the issue much better then I ever could, so i'll only link to it here. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48923641,"author":"netsharc","month":"2026-07","story":"The 3-degree limit could be exceeded as early as 2050","stance":"reference","why":"Fix is political change","quote":"I agree when Kurzgesagt argues, the fix is to push for political change","text":"Some argue that another form of climate denial is the denial that we're headed for certain doom. Of course it's an easy cop-out, to say \"well the planet's doomed anyway, let's book those flights to the Maldives! (Before the sea swallows them)\". Or to deny responsibility by saying \"Why should I change, look at those private jets!\". I agree when Kurzgesagt argues, the fix is to push for political change [VIDEO] . I like meat, petrol cars and flying vacations, but if they get heavily taxed to help save the planet, I'll sign up for that! I'll grumble that meat is such a luxury, but that's how it's supposed to be..."},{"id":37827022,"author":"netsharc","month":"2023-10","story":"Study finds the gulf stream is warming and shifting closer to shore","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in rebuttal of individual responsibility argument","quote":"","text":"God, what a tired argument to deflect responsibility. Not to say I'm holy, I've also given up trying to save the planet and am just going to enjoy my life until the climate kills me either directly or indirectly (lack of resources will lead to conflict, even in the West food and water might not be guaranteed), which I think will be in the next decade. What are the corporations doing anyway? They're selling crap to you and me, which we all addictively buy. iPhone 15, faster, shinier, newer, on sale now! RTX 4000 series, it needs a kilowatt power supply! Get Windows 11, the billions of laptops without TPM will be worthless when Windows 10 is EOLed in 2 years (Install Linux? But it's too hard to understand, I want my fucking convenience!). Wikipedia says there are 2640 billionaires in the world[1], if they all adopted the middle class lifestyle, would that save the planet? Hmm 2640 people flying on private jets vs the billions of planetary middle class with their cars and air-conditioned homes and taste for steak and jet-setting vacations. We're all \"environmentally friendly\" up to the line where beyond it it inconveniences us. For the billionaires flying commercial is inconvenient; for me, being vegan, using old tech, not allowing myself to ever see Japan again would be inconvenient. I admit that I like my luxuries, I'm not going to use the excuse that the billionaires have to do it first, even if they all turn green I'd still want my comforts, but I will support laws that forc"},{"id":35481092,"author":"oblio","month":"2023-04","story":"Europe drastically cut its energy consumption this winter","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as supporting rebuttal on personal responsibility","quote":"","text":"1. Source? 2. That's just dumb. Romania reduced its energy usage a ton since 1989 plus its per capita usage is super low, if someone needs to look in the mirror it's the US, Australia, Canada, Germany, China (China for the industrial pollution primarily), etc. 3. Also: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34581107,"author":"kiliantics","month":"2023-01","story":"Where Our Gasoline Comes From","stance":"reference","why":"Source for road construction CO2 claim","quote":"I believe I heard that in this kurzgesagt video","text":"You have to include more than just oil refining too: - there's the CO2 emitted during manufacture of the vehicle and maintaining the vehicle - the CO2 emitted to construct and maintain the roads the vehicle is used on (apparently this is about the same as the CO2 produced in making a car, per metre of road [1]) - since society is so massively oriented around vehicle use, you would probably need to apportion some significant amount of the CO2 emitted by the bureaucratic machine that allows for widespread vehicle use -- lawmakers, financiers, road engineers and regulators, police forces that enforce traffic codes -- this all costs a lot of energy and therefore carbon so it could be a non-negligible contribution - to much lesser order, the CO2 you yourself emit in order to operate the vehicle and maybe the CO2 emitted in building your garage where you store the vehicle [1] I believe I heard that in this kurzgesagt video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31640804,"author":"kiliantics","month":"2022-06","story":"Carbon dioxide now more than 50% higher than pre-industrial levels","stance":"reference","why":"Road construction carbon cost per metre versus a car","quote":"If this Kurzgesagt video is to be believed","text":"Exactly. If this Kurzgesagt video is to be believed, then the average road costs as much carbon to build PER METRE (!!) as it takes to produce one car: [VIDEO] Heavier vehicles like trucks (and EVs are heavier in general) cause much quicker degradation of roads. And doubling down on private vehicle use with EVs is going to increase demand for roads across the board. It's just so far from a useful solution to go down this route."},{"id":29362178,"author":"gh0std3v","month":"2021-11","story":"The Forest People","stance":"reference","why":"Climate change is multifaceted with no perfect solution","quote":"Kurzgesagt made a video explaining how climate change is a multifaceted problem","text":"> The expansion of the ‘Green New Deal’ and the rise of ‘renewable’ industrial technologies may be the death knell for these archaic and peaceful people. Make no mistake, these green initiatives – electric vehicles, wind turbines, solar batteries – these are actively destroying the last remaining strongholds of biodiversity on the planet. The future designs on the DRC include vast hydroelectric dams and intensive agriculture, stripping away the final refuges of the world. Now, more than ever, the Mbuti and other Pygmy peoples need our solidarity, an act which can be as simple as not buying that next iPhone This seems like a vast oversimplification by the author. Kurzgesagt made a video explaining how climate change is a multifaceted problem; that no solution will make everyone happy. I'm not trying to undermine the plight of the Mbuti people, but \"not buying that next iPhone\" is not going to solve the political, economic, and social strife that is present in the DRC. Also, the claim that green initiatives are \"destroying the last remaining strongholds of biodiversity on the planet\" overlooks the tradeoff in using green energy sources over fossil fuels. Link to video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28952890,"author":"ClumsyPilot","month":"2021-10","story":"Oil System Collapsing So Fast It May Derail Renewables","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim we cannot sustain this many cars","quote":"","text":"\"Public transit is not attractive because its not point-to-point, and you have no control over the other passengers on your ride. Autonomous minibusses with demand-driven routes, private subscriptions, and passenger reviews are the solution. \" They are not the solution, we cannot support this many cars even if they are all EV's. We also cannot support thia mich meat in our diet. The sooner people can accept that, the less pain it will be in the long run. [VIDEO] Also, how much do you have to hate public thanspor that you'd rather rise a totally unsafe and uncomfortable electric scooter than take a tram?"},{"id":28629983,"author":"xorfish","month":"2021-09","story":"Spain will ban selling fruit and vegetables in plastic containers starting 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Recent Kurzgesagt climate video","quote":"Kurzgesagt uploaded a video two days ago about climate change","text":"Kurzgesagt uploaded a video two days ago about climate change: [VIDEO] Fighting plastic packaging is one of the environmental issues that get an unreasonable amount of attention. It is more \"look we did something\" than \"look we actually make an impact\""},{"id":28656591,"author":"Cosmin_C","month":"2021-09","story":"Italy wants an exception to Europe's gas-engine ban for Ferrari, Lamborghini","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for industry emissions claim","quote":"Video reference","text":"Seriously, I would never have expected that somebody would unironically go on and compare VW with Ferrari. You literally have no idea what Ferarri is and what they do and where they do it and how they hire their employees. Their cars are almost exclusively hand-made and everybody who works at Ferrari is proud to do so, yes, even the dude mowing the grass - this is not cheap to maintain. There are people there whose skills can't be translated to building electric cars - which are really just oversized RC cars. There are people there who are experts in engines - and those will be laid off. In fact, everybody working on engines at Ferrari would be laid off. And those are a lot of people. The ICE is something that deserves to go on in something special like a Ferrari. This whole electric cars debacle makes me wish the planet would be blown up by aliens sooner before we all roll around in crappy BMW i3 lookalikes because there's nothing else available and there is no choice albeit industry is polluting several degrees of magnitude more than just ICE cars[1]. And certainly so in the case of a small italian sports cars manufacturer that yields true works of art, poetry on wheels. By comparison, VW is a tech giant who can afford to mass produce their stuff and will not suffer nor will they have to lay off a significant percentage of their staff. Video reference [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LxgMdjyw8uw",2],["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"vsMydMDi3rI":{"wy":"Praised as a charismatic, engaging talk with practical security lessons, like why credit cards protect you better than debit cards.","q":{"x":"One of the best google talks I have seen.","i":19485621,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":27049437,"author":"tchalla","month":"2021-05","story":"New book contends that Catch Me If You Can is mostly made up","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the best talks the commenter has heard.","quote":"his talk at Google is one of the best ones I've heard [1]","text":"Frank Abnagale's Comments on the movie and book (2002) [0]. I don't know if his story is true or not. But regardless of his story, his talk at Google is one of the best ones I've heard [1]. > I was interviewed by the co-writer only about four times. I believe he did a great job of telling the story, but he also over dramatized and exaggerated some of the story. That was his style and what the editor wanted. He always reminded me that he was just telling a story and not writing my biography. This is one of the reasons that from the very beginning, I insisted the publisher put a disclaimer in the book and tapes. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19485621,"author":"ai_ia","month":"2019-03","story":"Golden Rules for Making Money (1880)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interesting full lecture, among the best Google talks.","quote":"One of the best google talks I have seen.","text":"Offtopic, Frank Abnegale ( Catch me if you can fame ) also says the same thing in his [1] lecture at Google. The whole lecture is also pretty interesting. One of the best google talks I have seen. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19093105,"author":"dmcdm","month":"2019-02","story":"How A Young Woman Followed Two Hackers' Lies to Her Death","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Charismatic storytelling on how likability enables social engineering.","quote":"Great talk by the way, he's an incredibly charismatic and well spoken story teller","text":"He did a talk at Google where he asserted basically the same thing: [VIDEO] Great talk by the way, he's an incredibly charismatic and well spoken story teller, also a consultant for the FBI and a successful businessman - which makes you think about just how important likability and charisma are both to being a successful social engineer and being successful in-general. People will let you stray pretty far from reality if they like you - Holmes, Abagnale, and apparently Troy Woody (at least to this poor girl). Eventually reality catches up, one way or another."},{"id":16123677,"author":"willvarfar","month":"2018-01","story":"Elizabeth Warren's bill would fine the next Equifax for data breach","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Abagnale's life story plus his FBI view of the Equifax breach.","quote":"Its well worth watching the whole thing","text":"I bumped into this talk today: Frank Abagnale: \"Catch Me If You Can\" [VIDEO] He became an FBI agent and works with data breaches. So after entertainingly describing his life story he does mention the Equifax breach at the 37m mark. He paints a very negative picture of Equifax. Its well worth watching the whole thing, and not just skipping to his FBI Agent opinion of Equifax."},{"id":26611154,"author":"chris_f","month":"2021-03","story":"I tracked down my impostor","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by the real person behind Catch Me If You Can.","quote":"This is an interesting talk by Frank Abagnale (the real life person the story was based on) at Google.","text":"This is an interesting talk by Frank Abagnale (the real life person the story was based on) at Google. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20263676,"author":"fatline","month":"2019-06","story":"I just had over a thousand Euros stolen and Revolut is siding with the thief","stance":"rec","why":"Teaches why credit cards protect you better than debit cards.","quote":"This is among the most important learnings you can get from Frank Abagnale's talk at Google","text":"very sorry to hear that. Debit cards are convenient etc, but they don't offer much in terms of fraud protection. The general suggestion is to always use a credit card instead so that you don't expose your own money to theft, but the bank's. This is among the most important learnings you can get from Frank Abagnale's talk at Google ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":18220974,"author":"virde","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as a best talk, though not technical.","quote":"Technically not a tech talk but since it was at Talks at Google, this one","text":"Technically not a tech talk but since it was at Talks at Google, this one: Frank Abagnale: \"Catch Me If You Can\" | Talks at Google - YouTube [VIDEO]"},{"id":16128287,"author":"52-6F-62","month":"2018-01","story":"Elizabeth Warren's bill would fine the next Equifax for data breach","stance":"rec","why":"Abagnale comments on the Equifax breach around the 37 minute mark.","quote":"the entire video/talk is great","text":"* not in current use according to Equifax. I'm hesitant to take their word at face value, as they've tried to mitigate the entire situation via PR already and claim many users [who were affected] were not affected. However the site was still live, and exposed to the public web. It's only unrelated to the specific event in the United States, but clearly indicative of a pattern of negligence. I also wanted to add that the software in question that was compromised was a free and open source solution[0], not some top-dollar security program. And it was breached by relative simple XSS attack that even may have been carried out automatically. Beyond that it could just as likely have been a script kiddie who caught wind of the vulnerability on a message board somewhere and went hunting. I wouldn't call that highly sophisticated. XSS protection is the very baseline of internet data security... I'd also like to point to a video posted by another user upthread (willfarvar): [VIDEO] FBI's Frank Abagnale speaks to the Equifax breach at ~37m (as willfarvar says, though, the entire video/talk is great) --- [0] [LINK]"},{"id":28387132,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2021-09","story":"Ads Don't Work That Way (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"2017 Google security-month talk by Abagnale.","quote":"He did a Google talk in 2017","text":"He did a Google talk in 2017 [VIDEO] \"For Google's Security and Privacy Month, we are honored to present the real Frank Abagnale, Renowned Cybersecurity And Fraud Prevention Expert, Bestselling Author & Subject of Catch Me If You Can. His transformation from one of the world’s most notorious con men to an international cybersecurity expert trusted by the FBI has been mythologized in film and literature – but the takeaways he shares are the real deal.\" ..."},{"id":26950789,"author":"vngzs","month":"2021-04","story":"Experian’s credit freeze security is still a joke","stance":"reference","why":"Supports using credit cards to keep a buffer from your bank money.","quote":"","text":"If I buy coffee, I buy it on a credit card. Every daily purchase uses a credit card. Why? I want a buffer between me and the purchaser. I don't want them to be able to take money from my bank. [VIDEO] Now, if I shop online, I used to put it on a credit card. Now I generate a virtual debit card using an online service and pay with that. The logic is the same."},{"id":24899126,"author":"est31","month":"2020-10","story":"Tech companies are clamoring to give you credit cards","stance":"reference","why":"Explains credit versus debit exposure when a breach happens.","quote":"Here is an FBI employee explaining the difference between the two when a breach happens","text":"Here is an FBI employee explaining the difference between the two when a breach happens: [VIDEO] A more general article: [LINK]"},{"id":24520359,"author":"mhh__","month":"2020-09","story":"CEO of Cyber Fraud Startup NS8 Arrested by FBI, Facing Fraud Charges","stance":"reference","why":"Abagnale's FBI work and reformed status.","quote":"","text":"Frank Abagnale has worked for the FBI for 40 years now. He still has an Ego, but he has turned down several offers of pardons for his past crimes and is now very much on the light side of the force. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20863339,"author":"rgoulter","month":"2019-09","story":"Investigators who know you’ve faked your death","stance":"reference","why":"Abagnale says technology makes it easier for fraudsters to get details right.","quote":"technology makes it easier for the fraudster to come up with the right details","text":"FWIW, Frank Abagnale Jr (the guy depicted in the movie \"Catch Me If You Can\") was asked whether he thought it'd be harder for him to do what he did with technology as it is today. [VIDEO] He suggested that technology makes it easier for the fraudster to come up with the right details, whereas he often had to bluff when he was defrauding people."},{"id":16365141,"author":"yourapostasy","month":"2018-02","story":"The diabolical genius of the baby advice industry","stance":"reference","why":"Abagnale's account of his father and divorce, giving insight into his life.","quote":"","text":"Frank Abagnale's (\"Catch Me If You Can\" protagonist) take on divorce gives some insight into his life story [1], and is an interesting counterpoint. A transcript for those who hate watching videos. The transcript starts from the link and transcribes 132 seconds of his speech, up to the 23:57 mark. --- BGN --- I was one of those few children that got to grow up in the world with a daddy. Now, the world is full of fathers. But there are very few men worthy of being called daddy by their child. I had a daddy; loved his children more than he loved life itself. Steven Spielberg told Barbara Walters the more I've researched Frank's youth, without having met Frank, I couldn't help but put his father in the film through the likes of Christopher Walken. My father was a man who had four children---three boys and a daughter. Every night at bedtime, he'd walk into your room. He was 6'3\". He would drop down on one knee, kiss you on the cheek, pull the cover up, and he'd put his lip up on your earlobe. And he'd whisper deep into your ear, \"I love you. I love you very much.\" He never, ever , missed a night. As I grew older, I sometimes fell asleep before he got home. But I always woke up the next morning, knew he had been at my bedside. Years later, my older brother joined me in my room, temporarily; he was in the Marine Corps. He was 6'4\". He played semi-pro football for Buffalo. But my father would walk around to his bed, hug him, kiss him, whisper in his hear he loved him. When I was 16 "},{"id":35222020,"author":"olivermarks","month":"2023-03","story":"‘Catch Me If You Can’ conman Frank Abagnale Jr. lied about his lifetime of lies","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Abagale would make a perfect US politician, major league bullshitter who sticks to his stories for gain.","text":"Abagale would make a perfect US politician, major league bullshitter who sticks to his stories for gain. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1]]},"fD2briZ6fB0":{"wy":"Recommended as an empathetic, non-judgmental analysis of incel radicalization and how to counter it.","q":{"x":"Natalie Wynn's video^0 on incels always struck me as uniquely useful","i":38982534,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38982534,"author":"JieJie","month":"2024-01","story":"Incels need more mental health help, have \"fundamental thinking errors\"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explores incel genesis and radicalization while building empathy.","quote":"Natalie Wynn's video^0 on incels always struck me as uniquely useful","text":"Natalie Wynn's video^0 on incels always struck me as uniquely useful, not just for exploring their genesis and radicalization, but in generating empathy for those who suffer from it. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28313759,"author":"dijit","month":"2021-08","story":"Almost all of the top subreddits are moderated by the same people","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Empathetic low-judgement deconstruction of incel worldview, aiding deradicalisation.","quote":"Empathy is always at the root of deradicalisation, so I always plug her videos when I can.","text":"Radicalisation is actually very interesting and I (ashamedly) nearly succumbed to the same thing myself. There's a really good youtuber called \"Contrapoints\" that attempts to deconstruct with low judgement the kind of environment an Incel lives in and what the worldview is. Empathy is always at the root of deradicalisation, so I always plug her videos when I can. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":18344243,"author":"sascha_sl","month":"2018-10","story":"The Internet Will Be the Death of Us","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent video essay on the deeper issues of internet radicalization.","quote":"Here's an excellent video essay on it","text":"There's a bit more to it than you might think, and not in a good way. Here's an excellent video essay on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33069568,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2022-10","story":"Fake Men on Hinge","stance":"rec","why":"Good understanding of male online dating experience, notably around 17:00.","quote":"has a pretty good understanding of the male online dating experience (starting around 17:00)","text":"Ironically, this video focusing on \"incels\" has a pretty good understanding of the male online dating experience (starting around 17:00): [VIDEO] I agree that the various \"red pill\" subcultures have done a terrific job of sucking the air out of the room RE conversations about men's dating experience. It's a hard conversation to have with any amount of nuance in 2022."},{"id":22366218,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2020-02","story":"The Age of Instagram Face","stance":"rec","why":"Counter to reductive view of relationships based on Tinder and Instagram.","quote":"Basically, I suggest that you watch this","text":"> You can't have a happy society where the top 5% of beautiful men are paying people to schedule their calendar of tinder lays while other guys buy thot bathwater, or where the top 5% of women get all the attention and the rest wonder where their prince charming is who's been denied them from all this as they sadly pass 30 alone. This is so reductive of human relationships. You’re basing your entire worldview on what happens on Tinder and Instagram. That’s not the world, not even close. Basically, I suggest that you watch this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19891495,"author":"voltagex_","month":"2019-05","story":"For many young South Koreans, dating is too expensive, or too dangerous","stance":"rec","why":"Explains incel self-fulfilling dynamic well.","quote":"but explains it well.","text":"The \"incel\" population also manage to be a self fulfilling prophecy. It's a little dramatic, but [VIDEO] explains it well."},{"id":18594835,"author":"namlem","month":"2018-12","story":"Tumblr will ban all adult content on December 17th","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on toxic communities that boys fall into.","quote":"The youtuber ContraPoints made a great video about such communities","text":"Boys instead fall into other toxic communities, like incels. The youtuber ContraPoints made a great video about such communities: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18120592,"author":"hjek","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: Is Reddit censorship healthy?","stance":"rec","why":"Informed critique of the red pill community.","quote":"I'd warmly recommend watching ContraPoints","text":"It looks like Reddit hasn't censored TRP, just put up a warning. Reddit appears to be free software[0], so you can host your own if you're not OK with that. Haven't read what's on TRP, but if you'd like to listen to informed critique and considerations of it, I'd warmly recommend watching ContraPoints[1]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LdOe18KhtT4",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["8YUWDrLazCg",1],["qLjgXPDzeZo",1],["m7PVZixO35c",1],["Jzfpyo-q-RM",1],["h4Uu5eyN6VU",1],["4PHT-zBxKQQ",1],["dPHPNgIihR0",1],["EiZhdpLXZ8Q",1],["jQIHqkudgNY",1],["NOAgplgTxfc",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["FnEWBtCnFs8",1]]},"BCmz-fgp24E":{"wy":"HN users repeatedly post it to rebut the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks.","q":{"x":"Fifteen minutes pedalling in one direction is downtown Amsterdam, fifteen minutes in the other is farm land.","i":34600032,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39917675,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2024-04","story":"What the Suburb Haters Don't Understand","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (mistaken) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"> Everyone is different, but man... I'm the guy that likes to fire up the bbq and chat with neighbors in the backyard while the kids are who knows where (but safe) in the sprawl. I do this in \"the city\" right now (I BBQed some lamb on Sunday; my neighbour got a new motorcycle (Scrambler 1200XE) and I chatted with him about it). The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (mistaken) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks, which is certainly not the case: * [VIDEO] Here are some examples houses in an urban area with front yards, back yards, and garages (attached to lanes): * [LINK] * [LINK] A little less square footage: * [LINK] * [LINK] Plenty of that was build pre-WW2 in ways that didn't depend on cars: * [LINK] * [LINK] Suburban design is the worst of both worlds: lacking the density of the urban so infrastructure is inefficient and so is transportation, and basically bulldozing what makes more rural living nice (closeness to nature)."},{"id":38856573,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-01","story":"U.S. cities opt to ditch their off-street parking minimums","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (mistaken) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"> Why are we still trying to cram into small spaces? Define \"small\". Here are some examples houses in an urban area with front yards, back yards, and garages (attached to lanes): * [LINK] * [LINK] A little less square footage: * [LINK] * [LINK] The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (mistaken) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks, which is certainly not the case: * [VIDEO] Plenty of that was build pre-WW2 in ways that didn't depend on cars: * [LINK] * [LINK] Suburban design is the worst of both worlds: lacking the density of the urban so infrastructure is inefficient and so is transportation, and basically bulldozing what makes more rural living nice (closeness to nature)."},{"id":36598635,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-07","story":"(IEEE Spectrum) Your navigation app is making traffic unmanageable","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"> Public. Transit. Non-car centric development. The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks : * [VIDEO] Want a front yard, back yard, and garage (attached to a laneway)? Plenty of that was built pre-WW2: * [LINK] * [LINK] Examples (Streetview): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] See also: * [LINK] Fifteen minutes pedalling in one direction is downtown, fifteen minutes in the other is farm land."},{"id":36759898,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-07","story":"The force that shapes everything around us: Parking","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (mistaken) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"> Convince people to live in smaller houses and share walls with their neighbors, and the parking situation would change dramatically. Completely unnecessary. Want a front yard, back yard, and garage (attached to a laneway)? Plenty of that was build pre-WW2: * [LINK] * [LINK] The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (mistaken) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks : * [VIDEO] Examples (Streetview): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] See also: * [LINK] Fifteen minutes pedalling in one direction is downtown, fifteen minutes in the other is farm land."},{"id":36281620,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-06","story":"Electric cars are better for the environment, no matter the power source","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided/misinformed) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"> Nonsense. The inner city being a concrete hellscape isn't being helped by nicer parts on the outskirts. Why would the inner city need to be a \"concrete hellscape\"? You can have decent density without 'concrete hellscapes'. The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided/misinformed) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks : * [VIDEO] Want a front yard, back yard, and garage (attached to a laneway)? Plenty of that was built pre-WW2: * [LINK] * [LINK] Examples (Streetview): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":36469847,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-06","story":"Human-Scale Dutch Suburbs [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (mistaken) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"Want a front yard, back yard, and garage (attached to a laneway)? Plenty of that was build pre-WW2 in ways that didn't depend on cars: * [LINK] * [LINK] The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (mistaken) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks , which is certainly not the case: * [VIDEO] Examples (Streetview): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] See also: * [LINK] Fifteen minutes pedalling in one direction is downtown, fifteen minutes in the other is farm land."},{"id":35396789,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-04","story":"Maps show how parking lots “eat” U.S. cities","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"> Humans shouldn't be living in shoeboxes stacked 40 floors high. You have a limited view of 'urban living'. Want a front yard, back yard, and garage (attached to a laneway)? Plenty of that was built pre-WW2, and there's no reason why it couldn't be again: * [LINK] * [LINK] The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks : * [VIDEO] Examples (Streetview) of urban tree-lined streets which can support transit and where cycling is practical (and where a car is an option versus a necessity): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] See also: * [LINK] Fifteen minutes pedalling in one direction is downtown, fifteen minutes in the other is farm land."},{"id":35399239,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-04","story":"Maps show how parking lots “eat” U.S. cities","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"> You live in an apartment and trade away personal space and still have to take a train to work where you get little personal space. You have a limited view of 'urban living'. Want a front yard, back yard, and garage (attached to a laneway)? Plenty of that was built pre-WW2, and there's no reason why it couldn't be again: * [LINK] * [LINK] The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks : * [VIDEO] Examples (Streetview) of urban tree-lined streets which can support transit and where cycling is practical (and where a car is an option versus a necessity): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] See also: * [LINK] Fifteen minutes pedalling in one direction is downtown, fifteen minutes in the other is farm land."},{"id":34600032,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-01","story":"CA Cities to Lose All Zoning Powers in 2 Days","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks","quote":"The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks","text":"> Isn't it possible that people who live in beautiful neighborhoods with mature trees, nice big yards, and low traffic streets that are safe for kids to play around Basically how things were built pre-WW2? * [LINK] * [LINK] The Oh the Urbanity channel has a video on the (misguided) idea that \"urban living\" = Manhattan / Hong Kong apartment blocks : * [VIDEO] Examples (Streetview): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] See also: * [LINK] Fifteen minutes pedalling in one direction is downtown Amsterdam, fifteen minutes in the other is farm land."},{"id":32498580,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-08","story":"Why American cities are broke (2021) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Urban living is not just Manhattan","quote":"Urban living ≠ Manhattan","text":"> Suburbs provides housing. […] regardless of the density. At what cost, especially relative to other options. Lower density may end up costing more over the long term: * [LINK] Urban living ≠ Manhattan: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":38235930,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-11","story":"In praise of America's car addiction","stance":"reference","why":"Density does not equal Manhattan or Hong Kong","quote":"Also, \"density\" ≠ Manhattan/Hong Kong","text":"> Suburbs, as a consequence of their low density, must have bad or nonexistent public transport […] Please note the silent car-centric in front of \"suburb\" here. Suburbs used to be different pre-WW2: * [LINK] * [LINK] More generally it is sprawl —when residential is not with-in walking distance of commercial—that is the problem. You want to have cars be optional and not mandatory. Also, \"density\" ≠ Manhattan/Hong Kong: * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8wlld3Z9wRc",3],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",2],["QWWgr2rN45o",2],["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1]]},"3VQ382QG-y4":{"wy":"Repeatedly recommended as one of the most approachable introductions to lambda calculus and combinators.","q":{"x":"they are the most approachable videos on the topic I have ever seen.","i":41027895,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41027895,"author":"Zhyl","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: What resources do you recommend for learning Haskell?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Approachable videos on combinators and lambda calculus","quote":"they are the most approachable videos on the topic I have ever seen.","text":"I found these two videos on combinators and lambda calculus recently. They're only tangentially related to Haskell, but they are the most approachable videos on the topic I have ever seen. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":31336691,"author":"autophagian","month":"2022-05","story":"Logic","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Combinatory logic explained through birds, in JavaScript","quote":"I adore Gabriel Lebec's talk","text":"For anyone interested in the topic (combinatory logic as told through the medium of birds) I adore Gabriel Lebec's talk \"A Flock Of Functions: Combinators, Lambda Calculus & Church Encodings in JavaScript\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":24602777,"author":"triyambakam","month":"2020-09","story":"All you need is λ, part one: booleans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Intro to combinators and Church encodings with JavaScript equivalents","quote":"It's one of the best I've come across","text":"Just last night I found this excellent introduction to the Lambda Calculus by Gabriel Lebec [1]. It's one of the best I've come across and covers briefly history, building up combinators (using Smullyan's names) leading up to implementing Church encodings and gives Javascript equivalents (or approximations when not possible). The slides themselves are also very aesthetically pleasing, Gabriel has a good sense of colour and design [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39237151,"author":"zellyn","month":"2024-02","story":"A little closer to finding what became Moses Schönfinkel (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Good lambda calculus intro that strays into combinators","quote":"If you pick the right intro video, they'll stray into combinators","text":"I would start with lambda calculus. If you pick the right intro video, they'll stray into combinators: [VIDEO] is very good."},{"id":20697729,"author":"ayushgp","month":"2019-08","story":"Lisp is not based on the Lambda Calculus","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how a few basic functions build a whole language","quote":"I suggest you to watch this video if you want to understand how 3 basic functions can be used to create whole language","text":"I suggest you to watch this video if you want to understand how 3 basic functions can be used to create whole language [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OFgxAFdxYAQ",1],["O-2tpwW0kmU",1],["zduSFxRajkE",1]]},"BxQw4CdxLr8":{"wy":"HN fans love it for Knuth's humor and for how he makes open math problems feel approachable, and it is often cited for details like dancing links and counting rectangle subdivisions.","q":{"x":"it's always a pleasure for me to see Knuth's talks or read his work","i":15902403,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":35041352,"author":"svat","month":"2023-03","story":"I'm a Very Slow Thinker","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows Knuth's way of thinking.","quote":"I love the 2017 Christmas lecture","text":"I was going to post this on another thread (the Neal Stephenson one [LINK] ), but it also fits here: Donald Knuth does all his writing (even coding!) by hand with pencil on notepad, going through several rounds of editing before anything reaches a computer, and his stated reason is that he can type faster than he can think. (Somewhere a typing speed of 80 wpm is mentioned, which is not even all that fast.) So he says that writing by hand, being slower, perfectly matches the speed at which he can think. In other words, he describes himself as a slow thinker. (We can actually get some glimpses of the way he thinks from his public lectures, e.g. I love the 2017 Christmas lecture [VIDEO] or maybe—I haven't watched much—the 1985 problem-solving seminar course [LINK] .) And if Knuth describes himself as a slow thinker, it is a lesson to us all."},{"id":15902403,"author":"svat","month":"2017-12","story":"Donald Knuth's Christmas Lecture: A Conjecture That Had To Be True [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Knuth makes open math problems feel approachable.","quote":"it's always a pleasure for me to see Knuth's talks or read his work","text":"Indeed! He mentions something related at 5:00 in the video ( [VIDEO] ): > You know, people think that mathematicians have been working for hundreds of years and now there's tens of thousands or hundreds of thousands of mathematicians all spending every day working on problems so how could you possibly still find a simple down-to-earth problem that hasn't already been studied, you know, way too much? And the answer is: those problems aren't rare at all, they keep coming up several times a year, and this is an example where even this very basic problem of rectangle into rectangles has all kinds of, sort of, stones not yet turned. And the other thing you mentioned is one of the reasons it's always a pleasure for me to see Knuth's talks or read his work — he is one of the mathematicians (like Euler and unlike Gauss) who make it seem simple: we get the impression that we could do it too, if we spent enough time on it, that things are within reach for us, just barely. This is so inspiring. And then of course when you step back and look at his body of work, it's staggering! (Something like this talk may account for about half a page of Knuth's published output, and he has thousands of them…) That's additionally inspiring."},{"id":26850177,"author":"svat","month":"2021-04","story":"The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences","stance":"rec","why":"Knuth describes how OEIS lets you compute into literature.","quote":"The best description of the OEIS I've heard is by Donald Knuth in this fun talk","text":"The best description of the OEIS I've heard is by Donald Knuth in this fun talk ( [VIDEO] ) where at 19:50 he says how it lets you \"compute your way into the literature\": > For the last 30 years or more, there has been a wonderful tool for all kinds of problems of this form and it's been online for a long time: we have the Online Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences. And this is just the nicest thing since sliced bread for mathematics because you can compute your way into the literature. [audience laughter] If you want to know if anybody else has ever studied a problem, all you have to do is evaluate the first few cases of it and then you look it up and there it is. The hit rate is incredible, and all kinds of mathematicians have discovered each other through the OEIS. […] I donate to Wikipedia and the Internet Archive and the OEIS. (Aside: A good blog post about that lecture: [LINK] ) Indeed I've used it this way many times; the most recent example is that I became curious about how many polynomial functions there are mod n (after posting this comment: [LINK] ), and by computing the answers for n up to 10, I was able to look up up those numbers in the OEIS and find the general formula, and also the relevant papers. (Asked a question about it here: [LINK] but ended up answering it myself…) I don't think I'd have even known where to look (as I'm not a professional mathematician), if not for the OEIS. And this happens again and again."},{"id":19951269,"author":"svat","month":"2019-05","story":"I Got a Knuth Check for 0x$3.00","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long lecture with Knuth's humour.","quote":"you may like watching his (1-hour) Christmas lecture from 2017","text":"His first publication, as a high-school student, was in MAD magazine. His books are full of jokes, both highly technical ones and more trivial ones. :-) If you have some mathematical inclination and a couple of hours to spare, you may like watching his (1-hour) Christmas lecture from 2017: [VIDEO] — you may need to listen carefully (he's over 80 after all), but some of his humour comes through at moments."},{"id":18885646,"author":"svat","month":"2019-01","story":"Donald Knuth – The Joy of Asymptotics (2000) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Audience enjoyed and followed the lecture despite his age.","quote":"a lot of the audience did seem to follow along and enjoyed it.","text":"> unfortunately he's unable to present it well. His lectures are almost useless. I don't know; I've listened to dozens of hours of his videos and found them all useful and rather clear. I've heard comments like yours from others though, and I guess there are two points: * Of course a person at 80 will not speak the same way they did at 40. (I'm currently watching his lectures on Mathematical Writing, given when he was 49: [LINK] -- youtube has some even older videos on TeX etc but they're probably of less general interest.) Older people generally have more halting speech and the like, but this is perfectly natural. I suspect that if, for cultural or whatever reasons, you haven't spent a lot of time listening to old people and learning from them, you may just find it unfamiliar. Nevertheless, I was in the audience when Knuth gave his 2017 Christmas Lecture weeks before his 80th birthday, and a lot of the audience did seem to follow along and enjoyed it. (You can hear laughter in the expected places in the video: [VIDEO] -- so clearly not everyone feels the way you do.) * Every person has their own speech style, their way of translating thought into sounds. Like an unfamiliar accent, it may take a few minutes or hours to get used to it. (If you care enough about the content you will.) What you get from speech idiosyncrasies (their pauses, backtracking, mistakes, etc.) that you don't get from the written word is a window into someone's way of thinking, and I find in Knuth's lect"},{"id":25640567,"author":"jhncls","month":"2021-01","story":"The many ways of splitting a rectangle in many","stance":"reference","why":"Counting subdivisions of a rectangle into rectangles.","quote":"Don Knuth's 2017 Christmas lecture featured a way to count how many ways a rectangle can be subdivided into rectangles","text":"Don Knuth's 2017 Christmas lecture featured a way to count how many ways a rectangle can be subdivided into rectangles [0]. It got its own entry in the Online Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":18728366,"author":"svat","month":"2018-12","story":"The Yoda of Silicon Valley","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth jokes about triangle notepaper and uninvented mathematics.","quote":"see 16:05 of Knuth's 2017 Christmas lecture","text":"Ah one place I ran across this: see 16:05 of Knuth's 2017 Christmas lecture ( [VIDEO] ): > I got a nice piece of paper here to work with. They had a conference in Poland last year [BachoTeX 2016] and they sent me this nice pad of paper and I like it so much that I decided at my birthday party next month I gotta give out notepaper that's... [audio drowned by laughter] But actually the notepaper at my birthday party is going to be triangle... paper. Because I have a feeling that all kinds of mathematics has never been invented yet because people haven't been given triangle... [laughter]."},{"id":17386473,"author":"yantrams","month":"2018-06","story":"Dealing with Hard Problems (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Tight paving problem from the lecture.","quote":"tight paving/tiles\" problem from Knuth's Christmas Lecture","text":"This works well for me too :) Specifically when I am going to sleep. Recently, I spent a couple of days working on \"tight paving/tiles\" problem from Knuth's Christmas Lecture ( [VIDEO] ) and surprised myself when I managed to exhaustively enumerate all valid arrangements(around 40 I think) for some small configuration while lying on the bed at night, something that I never thought I could do without a pen and paper."},{"id":16036588,"author":"johnsonjo","month":"2017-12","story":"Ask HN: What was the best CS paper you read in 2017?","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth mentions dancing links in this lecture.","quote":"Knuth even talks about dancing links in his new 2017 Christmas Tree Lecture","text":"I found Knuth’s “Dancing Links” paper [1] very well written and a somewhat easy read (I had to reread certain parts a couple times). I had to write a sudoku solver for one of my classes and I read that dancing links and algorithm x was one way to do it [2]. I then read some things around the internet to apply dancing links to sudoku solving [3] [4]. If you read the Wikipedia entry on exact cover problems there is a section on sudoku as an exact cover problem [5] this is one thing necessary to understand in order to implement a sudoku solver with algorithm x and dancing links. Knuth even talks about dancing links in his new 2017 Christmas Tree Lecture [6] specifically here at 4:28 [7]. Basically he uses dancing links to solve for certain n in the problem he sets up in that lecture. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [VIDEO] [7] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TrC6ROeV4GI",1]]},"RWTic9btP38":{"wy":"Linked in threads on corporate tax incentives and subsidies as a detailed example of how they can trap a state.","q":{"x":"I found this insightful: Why Louisiana Stays Poor","i":39033729,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39033729,"author":"somethoughts","month":"2024-01","story":"Cancer Alley","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insightful look at resource-rich rulers and poverty.","quote":"I found this insightful: Why Louisiana Stays Poor","text":"I found this insightful: Why Louisiana Stays Poor [VIDEO] Definitely sounds very similar to CPG's \"Rules for Ruler\" video where he provides a description of rulers ruling in resource rich countries."},{"id":41173523,"author":"aqme28","month":"2024-08","story":"Twitter kills its San Francisco headquarters, will relocate to South Bay","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting video on corporate tax breaks focused on Louisiana.","quote":"this very interesting video on the subject focusing on Louisiana","text":"I'm really curious if there has been a comprehensive study on incentive corporate tax breaks like these. It has become my understanding that these are rarely worth it. Reminds me on this very interesting video on the subject focusing on Louisiana ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":37737915,"author":"thelastgallon","month":"2023-10","story":"Subsidies Awarded to Tesla Inc: at least $2,829,855,494 and Counting","stance":"rec","why":"Fossil fuel subsidies make one zip code richest and poorest.","quote":"Skip to 5:22 for the explanation.","text":"Why Louisiana Stays Poor: [VIDEO] This video is about fossil fuel subsidies in just one zip code which makes it the richest and poorest zip code at the same time. $2857 per capita subsidies via Industrial Tax Exemption Program. National average is $291 per capita, Texas $89 per capita. Skip to 5:22 for the explanation."},{"id":32706100,"author":"arrosenberg","month":"2022-09","story":"In Jackson, Mississippi, a water crisis of unparalleled scale","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how state funding flows away from majority-black cities.","quote":"there is a great video on how this works in Lousiana","text":"Mississippi was the most confederate of the Confederate states. Jefferson Davis was their Senator and then President of the Confederacy. After the civil war and the end of reconstruction, it was a hotbed of reactionary violence. There maybe a robust tax base, but you have to ignore the entire history of the state (which is majority white) and city (which is majority black and the descendants of slaves). I'm not as familiar with MS, but there is a great video on how this works in Lousiana - [VIDEO] Historically (back to reconstruction), the Federal government is the guarantor of Black rights in the South and they provide significant funds to the majority black/liberal cities to make up for the fact that the state governments do not provide. Generally the state government is funneling money out of these areas."},{"id":18671186,"author":"sschueller","month":"2018-12","story":"Apple to build campus in Austin and sites in Seattle, San Diego and Culver City","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how tax incentives ruin states like Louisiana.","quote":"it is very sad how these tax incentives are ruining entire states.","text":"I saw this video about LA a few days ago and it is very sad how these tax incentives are ruining entire states. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18672851,"author":"cenal","month":"2018-12","story":"Apple to build campus in Austin and sites in Seattle, San Diego and Culver City","stance":"rec","why":"Details how Louisiana is trapped by tax credit awards.","quote":"There is another great video that goes into detail","text":"There is another great video that goes into detail on his Louisiana is trapped in a tax incentive hell because of how they award these credits: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aXQ2lO3ieBA",1],["ICp2-EUKQAI",1],["u4L130DkdOw",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["42oQN7fy_eM",1]]},"o4-YnLpLgtk":{"wy":"The go-to demo whenever HN discusses what Lisp machines were like and what modern shells and editors lack.","q":{"x":"It makes working on VSCode today look like banging rocks together","i":41767000,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41767000,"author":"hcarvalhoalves","month":"2024-10","story":"The Rise of Worse Is Better (1991)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows interactive systems far ahead of modern editors.","quote":"It makes working on VSCode today look like banging rocks together","text":"See these demos for the kinds of interactive systems LISP enabled back in the day: [VIDEO] [LINK] It makes working on VSCode today look like banging rocks together, let alone 30 years ago."},{"id":37801117,"author":"yourapostasy","month":"2023-10","story":"Making Hard Things Easy","stance":"rec","why":"Brief overview of old Lisp Machine runtime visualization.","quote":"Check out demos of the old Lisp Machines, [1] is a brief overview demo","text":">My dream is to make everything visualizable at runtime. Check out demos of the old Lisp Machines, [1] is a brief overview demo, [2] links to a timestamp with a view of some simple diagramming, but I’ve seen TI-Symbolics beasts routinely display complex relationships in Lisp code on their massive (for the time) bitmapped screens. The limitation was the end user managing the visualization complexity. With open source llvm, clang and similar making available abstract syntax trees and even semantic analysis and type checking results, LLM’s assisting with decompiling binary blobs, and modern hardware (goggles, graphics cards, and so on), I sometimes wonder how close we can come to reproducing that aspect of the Lisp Machine experience on open source operating systems. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":26726886,"author":"rml","month":"2021-04","story":"What have we lost? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Short 15-minute demo by a Symbolics developer.","quote":"If you only have 15 minutes, he gives another demo here","text":"If you are into Lisp Machines, this talk on Symbolics by Kalman Reti (who worked there) is worth watching (1h): [LINK] If you only have 15 minutes, he gives another demo here: [VIDEO] He has another video where he does some actual hacking (smooth scrolling of sheet music for display while playing an instrument): [LINK]"},{"id":23242311,"author":"mikelevins","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Production Lisp in 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Expert demonstrating working with a Symbolics Lisp Machine.","quote":"showing some of what it's like to work with a Symbolics Lisp Machine.","text":"There's this recent video showing a little of what the Pharo Smalltalk environment is like: [LINK] The video was made by a Ruby programmer, rather than an experience Smalltalker, so it's more a newbie showing you cool things he's discovered, rather than an expert showing the power of the system. On the other hand, there's this one: [VIDEO] That one is an expert, Kalman Reti, showing some of what it's like to work with a Symbolics Lisp Machine. (It's not a real Lisp machine; they're all at least thirty years old now; this is an emulator running the old Symbolics OS on Linux). Go to YouTube and search for Rainer Joswig and you'll find a few video demos of Lisp Machines and MCL and so forth. Finally, here are a few images from about eighteen years ago, also from Rainer. They show various views of the Macintosh Common Lisp and SK8 environments. It's not an experienced user showing you around, but at least you can get some idea what the environments looked like: [LINK]"},{"id":20217573,"author":"zaarn","month":"2019-06","story":"Mezzano – An operating system written in Common Lisp","stance":"rec","why":"Fun demo of a Lisp machine as it was in the 80s/90s.","quote":"That shows off a LISP machine roughly as it was written in the 80s/90s.","text":"Since he did AI work in the 50's, probably a lot of his work was written in LISP or IPL, which had many of the concepts that LISP later used. Most of his high-profile inventions (Computer mice, Hypertext, Interactive Computers) were first commercialized on LISP machines in the 80s. LISP Machines also pioneered some other concepts such as windowing ystems, graphic rendering, modern networking, garbage collection, etc. There is a fun little demo on youtube here; [VIDEO] . That shows off a LISP machine roughly as it was written in the 80s/90s."},{"id":33523584,"author":"thrown_22","month":"2022-11","story":"Unix as a tool forge and how Emacs fits in Unix philosophy","stance":"reference","why":"Benchmark for what a real Lisp machine looks like.","quote":"is still _not_ a lisp machine","text":"I've ran chsh on top of a (virtual) Guix(SD) installation. It was rather nice but is still _not_ a lisp machine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30812551,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2022-03","story":"Why we need Lisp machines","stance":"reference","why":"Open Genera running in a VM, with editable running system.","quote":"Here's a demonstration of Symbolics Open Genera (TM) 2.0, demonstrated running in a virtual machine.","text":"The key thing about those type of systems was the ability to reach down into the system and edit the code of the system currently in operation. Here's a demonstration of Symbolics Open Genera (TM) 2.0, demonstrated running in a virtual machine. It is noted by the author of the video in the first minute or so that even in emulation, it is much faster than the original machines. - [VIDEO] Oberon also had a similar attribute, in that they kept the names of functions to operate on objects visible. The same was true of the Canon Cat, Hypercard, Ted Nelson's Xanadu project, Smalltalk, and a number of other early computing systems. The main feature common to all of these systems is that they all preserve context . In Genera, Oberon, Canon Cat, Hypercard, and SmallTalk the source was always available. (as far as I know). In Xanadu, the main functionality of the web was present, but it wouldn't allow the broken links (and lost context) that now plague the web. I think a future platform could take code in a number of languages, compile it to an abstract syntax tree, but preserve the context required to recreate the source. In fact, it's reasonable that you could import a routine in a language you aren't familiar with, and reverse the compilation to get it expressed in an equivalent (but less elegant) form in your language of choice, along with the original comments. There's nothing stopping an open source project from taking elements of these existing systems and moving forward from th"},{"id":30813067,"author":"bitwize","month":"2022-03","story":"Why we need Lisp machines","stance":"reference","why":"C JPEG decoder compiled and replacing Lisp one for speed.","quote":"Kalman Reti compiles a JPEG decoder written in C, and replaces the Lisp JPEG decoder that came with the system with it, yielding a performance boost","text":"You are aware that Lisp machines understood several different flavors of Lisp? The Symbolics ones understood Zetalisp and Common Lisp at least. Were they on the market today they could be convinced to run Clojure and Scheme as well. There are a few old-timers developing Common Lisp applications to run on modern hardware, using Symbolics hardware. In fact, Symbolics shipped compilers for other non-Lisp programming languages, including C and Ada. These interoperated smoothly with Lisp code, much more so than they do under Unix. In this demo, Kalman Reti compiles a JPEG decoder written in C, and replaces the Lisp JPEG decoder that came with the system with it, yielding a performance boost: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27053253,"author":"hprotagonist","month":"2021-05","story":"CLUI: Building a Graphical Command Line (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Clickable semantic data structures for building commands.","quote":"which also had semantically driven clickable data structures one could use to build up a command line invocation.","text":"The animations in this post remind me of nothing more than a Symbolics LISP Machine: [VIDEO] which also had semantically driven clickable data structures one could use to build up a command line invocation. In about 1982."},{"id":26962330,"author":"posobin","month":"2021-04","story":"Symbolics Lisp Machine demo (2013) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Past HN discussions of this video.","quote":"","text":"There are 30(!) comments on HN linking to this video, including discussions of Bret Victor's \"Learnable programming\", using Lisp in production from last year, comparisons with Smalltalk, a thread with examples of beautiful software, and more: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25300878,"author":"hhas01","month":"2020-12","story":"A terminal-based workflow for research, writing, and programming","stance":"reference","why":"1980s Lisp machine tech as better than modern terminals.","quote":"even 1980s tech was doing it better","text":"Doesn’t have the beautiful Display Postscript/LeX-esque typography that I want, and less said about Lots of Irritating Silly Parentheses the better, but even 1980s tech was doing it better: [VIDEO] I think there are lessons to be had from the likes of Smalltalk and Logo too. Highly expressive without insane levels of cryptic inconsistent punctuation rules and UX man-traps."},{"id":25025483,"author":"tsimionescu","month":"2020-11","story":"Programs are a prison: Rethinking the building blocks of computing interfaces","stance":"reference","why":"Genera as an example of a no-apps workflow.","quote":"something like the Genera LISP OS was probably much closer[0].","text":"The terminal is exactly an example of an app-centric workflow and why it is easier to build and/or easier to use than an API focused one. If you want to see a no-apps workflow, something like the Genera LISP OS was probably much closer[0]. People choose to use apps like ls, cat, less, echo, touch, find instead of using the FILE* object directly with readdir(), stat(), read(), write(), open(), creat() etc. All of the apps are designed to have human-readable output first, lots of options for controlling that output to make it as readable as possible etc. However, none of them exposes a rich model for its output that would make it possible to easily integrate it into more complex workflows. Instead, we rely on yet other apps, like sed, grep, xargs and essentially copy-pasting text between these apps (this is all that pipes really are, essentially). This becomes even more obvious when you use stuff like gcc or gdb, which have extremely rich and potentially useful layers of representation that they refuse to expose at all, even as APIs - only text is allowed in the interaction. Hell, the MS Office suite is a better example of a no-apps workflow, since each of the Office UIs has a deep understanding of the data produced by the others, and you can combine these in meaningful ways - much more so than terminal apps (for example, Word can show a portion of a spreadsheet without you having to guess at what contents it might have and how to parse it, like you would if you want to expose "},{"id":21964139,"author":"p_l","month":"2020-01","story":"Lisp Symbolics Macivory 3 with loaded software","stance":"reference","why":"Debug session fixing a C JPEG decoder on a Lisp machine.","quote":"It shows a debug session where C code running on the lisp machine","text":"It's JPEG, not PNG, but here you go, a demo by Kalman Reti (one of the Symbolics' developers): [VIDEO] It shows a debug session where C code running on the lisp machine (VLM in this case, unknown variant of the VLM though) is debugged to fix JPEG decompression error."},{"id":21770775,"author":"lispm","month":"2019-12","story":"A programmable programming language (2018) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a real Lisp REPL environment.","quote":"","text":"> No, other languages (most of them) don't have real REPLs. What they have, at best can be categorized as \"interactive shells\". > Try Racket, Clojure, Clojurescript, Fennel, Chez. Give Lisp an honest, heartfelt attempt to learn it. Compared to actual Lisps, some of these have mostly interactive shells. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15473231,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2017-10","story":"A few things I know about Lisp Machines","stance":"reference","why":"Symbolics Lisp Machine running in an emulator.","quote":"Not surprisingly, it looks a lot like Emacs (or vice-versa).","text":"Video of a Symbolics Lisp Machine (in Brad Parker's emulator).[1] Not surprisingly, it looks a lot like Emacs (or vice-versa). [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":11280053,"author":"vmorgulis","month":"2016-03","story":"Ask HN: What are some examples of beautiful software?","stance":"reference","why":"OpenGenera as an example of beautiful, consistent software.","quote":"everything displayed on the screen is typed and can be retrieved as an s-expression","text":"Oberon, TempleOS or OpenGenera. These projects are vertical, consistent and use only one programming language. In OpenGenera, everything displayed on the screen is typed and can be retrieved as an s-expression (like in a web browser): [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":10943981,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-01","story":"Announcing Wolfram Programming Lab","stance":"reference","why":"Lisp machine ideas that lived on in later systems.","quote":"","text":"Xerox controlled the whole stack, they invented it for their systems. So these workstations where an whole stack OS. You can read about their implementations on the links I provided before. Additionally, you can get the original Smalltalk-80 from from Stephane's web site. [LINK] This is a very ruft idea how it felt to use a Smalltalk workstation: [LINK] [LINK] The REPL was what is known in Smalltalk as transcript. Similarly the Interlisp-D workstation also had they own mixture of REPL and editing [LINK] Some of the ideas that lived on the Lisp Machines afterwards [LINK] [VIDEO] Mesa was a memory safe system programming language created at Xerox, as an evolution from Extended Algol to replace BCPL. Niklaus Wirth based his Modula-2 design on it. Shortly thereafter they updated Mesa into Cedar, which added support for RC with local GC for cycle collection. The system then got called Mesa/Cedar. It allowed the same interactive experience as the other workstations, but using a GC enabled systems programming language. The REPL provided nice features, like auto-suggestion when a typo would cause a compilation failure. It is also probably the first graphical debugger for a strong typed language. Any OS API could be used on the REPL by typing modulename.procedure , which could use other OS APIs to get its input from different sources. An idea Wirth adopted into Oberon. You can see how all three environments looked like here: [LINK] And yes, Powershell alongside .NET is probably the cl"},{"id":9674299,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2015-06","story":"Unix is not an acceptable Unix","stance":"reference","why":"Programs exchanging plain data rather than serialized text streams.","quote":"this lisp machine talk mention how 'programs' (I guess functions) exchanged plain old data rather than serialized ascii streams making things very fast","text":"I am really curious about how much parsing and formatting occupy *nix source code. (IIRC 30% of ls), and if it would be a good idea to decouple the way you mention it. my json-infused distro is still on my mind. ps: this lisp machine talk mention how 'programs' (I guess functions) exchanged plain old data rather than serialized ascii streams making things very fast. There are caveats of adress space sharing though but it's another nice hint we could investigate other ways. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8440168,"author":"Scuds","month":"2014-10","story":"Rich Command Shells","stance":"reference","why":"Emulated Symbolics Lisp machine showing what the system was like.","quote":"you can get the idea.","text":"This demo appears to be running on an emulator instead of an actual antique piece of hardware but you can get the idea. [VIDEO] the AI winter is something you don't hear too much about. [LINK]"},{"id":7777062,"author":"eschaton","month":"2014-05","story":"Pixel graphics in terminal","stance":"reference","why":"Symbolics Genera command line with interactive graphics and text.","quote":"The command line is contextually intelligent and presents interactive graphics and text.","text":"You mean like the command line on Lisp machines, such as this example from Symbolics Genera? [VIDEO] The command line is contextually intelligent and presents interactive graphics and text. Not a simulation of a teletype on a simulation of a character terminal."}],"r":[["2qsmF8HHskg",1],["HOuZyOKa91o",1],["OBfB2MJw3qg",1],["lyu7v7nWzfo",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["7PoajCqNKpg",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["kJXRyWQgOY4",1],["Pld8wTa16Jk",1],["Qam5BkXIEhQ",1]]},"NWSZ4c9yqW8":{"wy":"Recommended as a fascinating look at easy parallelism abstractions, though other commenters note the approach never beat sequential C by much.","q":{"x":"a promising language abstraction to do hard things with parallelism relatively easily","i":2477051,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":8647979,"author":"anaphor","month":"2014-11","story":"Guy Steele on Functional Code for Parallel Execution (2009) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Similar talk on nested data parallelism for hierarchical structures.","quote":"You might want to watch this (similar in some ways) talk by SPJ on Data Parallel Haskell","text":"Well nested data parallelism generally applies to hierarchical structures like trees, whereas flat data parallelism applies to things like arrays and whatnot. You might want to watch this (similar in some ways) talk by SPJ on Data Parallel Haskell [VIDEO]"},{"id":8278853,"author":"sgrove","month":"2014-09","story":"Rhine – A Lisp on LLVM","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how Haskell compilers map out contiguous memory.","quote":"it was interesting to see how they actually map out contiguous memory","text":"I believe Haskell compilers are pretty amenable to stuff like this - watching \"Data Parallel Haskell\", it was interesting to see how they actually map out contiguous memory, etc. [VIDEO] Sadly, it doesn't seem to be as tightly integrated as what you'd see in SBCL from a developers PoV, but I'm certainly intrigued by their ability to achieve some very specific implementation while separating it from the expression of the algorithms."},{"id":7543936,"author":"reitzensteinm","month":"2014-04","story":"Speed memory access by arranging data to take advantage of CPU caching","stance":"rec","why":"Makes high performance Haskell's eventual success look plausible.","quote":"I'd not make a bet against high performance Haskell's eventual success","text":"This is both a straw man (OP asked for limited help, not omnipotence), and predicting the future by drawing a line from the past. Functional compilers that outperform hand tuned C++ on many core machines may be possible, but very hard. Just because everyone has previously underestimated the difficulty does not mean it's an idea worthy of derision. I think it's much more likely that it's perpetually ten years away, until one day it's not (like the current, possibly temporary end to Moore's Law). Watching Simon Peyton Jones' talk on data parallel Haskell, I'd not make a bet against high performance Haskell's eventual success, especially as core counts continue to climb, and NUMA gets more NU. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2477051,"author":"Peaker","month":"2011-04","story":"Joel Spolsky: Can your programming language do this?","stance":"rec","why":"Data parallel Haskell as an abstraction for easy parallelism.","quote":"a promising language abstraction to do hard things with parallelism relatively easily","text":"Here's the main Haskell hero, Simon Peyton Jones giving a talk about data parallel Haskell, which I think is a promising language abstraction to do hard things with parallelism relatively easily: [VIDEO] As for techniques that can be used now: Haskell's `par` and `pseq` combinators allow adding parallelization to \"hard\" code after-the-fact pretty easily."},{"id":1431161,"author":"drhodes","month":"2010-06","story":"SeaMicro drops an atom bomb on the server industry","stance":"rec","why":"Parallelizing non-trivial data structures while avoiding tedious bookkeeping.","quote":"but worth a watch, imho","text":"While this isn't undeniable proof, it's interesting none the less, Simon Peyton Jones: Data Parallel Haskell. [VIDEO] He talks about how this scheme can parallelize non-trivial data structures, and how tedious book keeping can be avoided. I haven't used it yet in a project, so I can't speak of its efficacy - but worth a watch, imho."},{"id":7959662,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2014-06","story":"Teenage Haskell","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Your make some good points, but I don't quite buy them. Turbo Pascal for example was written by a single guy, Anders Hejlsberg, in about 2 years for several platforms in assembly language, and included an integrated (though primitive) text editor/IDE. Smalltalk was taken from ST-72 to ST-80 in 8 years, with small teams that also had to invent/implement complete VMs, operating systems, bitmap graphics engines, GUI framework(s) and sophisticated IDEs. You write that it would be nice to have an FP IDE so it could give you better feedback (or were you talking about an existing FP IDE). These guys built the live IDE (even better than types for direct feedback) in a fraction of the time. Does OPAL still compile to C? Does it still use Tk? Think about Ruby, Python, etc. I could go on (the VPRI stuff is also amazing), but I think you get the idea: it seems that not only can you be a productive member of society without FP, it sure looks like people can be a lot more productive without FP than with it. Java is not really a good example of anything. Certainly not of an OOPL: \"Java is the most distressing thing to happen to computing since MS-DOS.\" \"If the pros at Sun had had a chance to fix Java, the world would be a much more pleasant place. This is not secret knowledge. It’s just secret to this pop culture.\" — Alan Kay (who coined the term \"object oriented\") I don't quite get why you see LISP as an example for the power of FP, it is a multi-paradigm language with some functional elem"},{"id":4527138,"author":"thirsteh","month":"2012-09","story":"White Paper: Haskell vs. F# vs. Scala [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Data Parallel Haskell as a comparison point for language parallelism.","quote":"","text":"tl;dr: \"we remark that all 3 languages provide easy-to-use, high-level and efﬁcient support for parallelism. Haskell has an edge in its rich libraries and aggressive optimisation. Through laziness, top-level parallelism can be speciﬁed in a more modular way, minimising the need to modify components and avoiding any notion of explicit threads. Thus, Haskell is the language of choice for minimally intrusive parallelism. F# and Scala provide several mechanisms for parallelisation, some as high level as Haskell’s, some lower level with more potential for performance tuning. Thus, these languages provide a more direct route to controlling low level aspects and can use object-oriented and impure language features.\" It would be cool to see a similar contrast between Data Parallel Haskell ( [LINK] / [VIDEO] ) and others."},{"id":2725560,"author":"shriphani","month":"2011-07","story":"The Rise of \"Worse is Better\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Source for SPJ Koan","text":"Dude, you just DID NOT insult Haskell ! [Sort of relevant] Apparently SPJ was once asked by a customs official in America if an american citizen could do what he did. Someone with him announced that his work as the creator of ML made him irreplaceable. So you see, ML's got fanboys in high places. Even close to SPJ apparently. Source for SPJ Koan : [VIDEO] (around the 4 min mark)."},{"id":13695011,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2017-02","story":"Fooling the masses with performance results on parallel computers (2010)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Four cores to match sequential C","text":"One of my favorites of these is only showing the speedup...\"scales linearly\", without comparing to a non-parallel baseline. First time I saw this was with Clik, which reported linear scaling. I was impressed, until I found out that the parallel version was 6x slower on a single CPU than the sequential version. Similar with a SPJ talk on parallel Haskell (can't find it right now). Big claims about this being the One True Way, and then required four cores to match C sequential case. Hmmm. UPDATE: [VIDEO] around the 1h 10min mark. Four cores to match sequential C (previously had written six, was wrong)"},{"id":10331284,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2015-10","story":"Moore’s law may be running out of steam, but chip costs will continue to fall","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"after many years of development was still slower on 6 cores than C on a single core","text":"The \"FP for multi-core\" trope is a red-herring, at least so far. Yes, immutability makes parallelizing easier. It also makes things in general so much more expensive that we're still net-negative by a large margin. For example, Simon Peyton Jones gave a talk[1] about Data Parallel Haskell, which after many years of development was still slower on 6 cores than C on a single core. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8339516,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2014-09","story":"Programming without objects","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it is an absolutely fascinating talk, and the techniques look quite worthwhile","text":"LOL! My 1st year university course was 1989. When were you born? Interestingly, that is exactly what I see in the current generation of FP hypsters. Grandiose claims based on very limited experience. Aka: know so little, assume so much. But don't worry, it happens to the best. My favorite is still the Simon Peyton-Jones talk on Data Parallel Haskell, where he can't help himself, but just has to make the snide comment \"this is only possible in a language like Haskell\". Hand raised in the auditorium: The HPC community has been doing this for years. In FORTRAN. But carry on... (And of course, the results are dismal: 6 cores required to match sequential C! Considering that this sort of concurrency [there are others] is exclusively for better performance, that is a damning result). Despite these, er, issues, it is an absolutely fascinating talk, and the techniques look quite worthwhile. After all, they've been in use in the HPC community for some time :-) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kKySEUrP7LA",1],["YX40hbAHx3s",1],["7lm3K8zVOdY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["skos4B5x7qE",1],["a9xAKttWgP4",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["L_XJ_s5IsQc",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["0rSMt1pAlbE",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["N6xLMUifbxQ",1]]},"PhhC_N6Bm_s":{"wy":"HN people point lost, lonely or burned-out readers to it as a short, humane piece of perspective on how to live.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend a 23 minute investment in David Foster Wallace's","i":23453056,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":31440041,"author":"coip","month":"2022-05","story":"Tell HN: The loneliness of a pretty good developer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommended to a lonely, struggling developer as perspective.","quote":"I’d highly recommend you give it a sincere listen","text":"As someone far younger than you, yet at times infinitely jaded, through what have >0% odds of being at least related circumstance: If you haven’t come across DFWs This is Water, I’d highly recommend you give it a sincere listen: [VIDEO] What is your goal? Have to make hay while the sun shines, if you perform so we’ll see if you can find another opportunity to realize that gap you’re in? If that’s not fruitful, try and try again and at the end of the day, find resolve in knowing you’re doing what you can and, while you have that going wrt opportunities, give your thoughts and time to things around you that do inspire you and provide some degree of fulfillment. As someone in the field as a moderately tenured, and currently unemployed, “software engineer”: it is hard to unplug but god is it so necessary for being present and being able to see what else is going on you can nurture&enjoy. I’m a bit scared, given the macro picture rn, about being unemployed. But, at the end of the day, we are all in this ocean of chaos with 86400 seconds to allocate everyday however we choose. Bit of a lengthy rant, but feels good to capture some thoughts I’ve been having honestly, and hope you are most excellent!!! At least it’s almost the weekend haha."},{"id":23453056,"author":"jimwhite","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: I implemented the life I designed: perfect but I feel lost. What now?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A 23-minute speech worth the time for someone feeling lost.","quote":"I highly recommend a 23 minute investment in David Foster Wallace's","text":"Thanks to your exceptional focus and determination you've arrived early at what is widely called Midlife Crisis. The aphorism your story brings to my mind is \"Don't just do something, sit there!\". Not sure what will help you but I highly recommend a 23 minute investment in David Foster Wallace's \"This is Water\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":31448732,"author":"JeremyNT","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: Thoughts on being “boring”","stance":"rec","why":"Famous graduation speech expanding on life being what you make of it.","quote":"is in the famous graduation speech by David Foster Wallace","text":"There's a truism: life is what you make of it. The longer version, which might get you thinking a little more than the aphorism, is in the famous graduation speech by David Foster Wallace: \"This is Water\" [0] (you can also read a transcript [1]). [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":18644042,"author":"hprotagonist","month":"2018-12","story":"Why Aren’t Rich People Happy With the Money They Have?","stance":"rec","why":"Speech about default worship of money, power, and intellect.","quote":"If you worship money and things-if they are where you tap real meaning in life-then you will never have enough.","text":"I’m immediately reminded of the denouement of “This is Water”: [VIDEO] . It’s become/ perhaps always has been a little trite or on the nose, but it’s also not wrong. If you worship money and things-if they are where you tap real meaning in life-then you will never have enough. Never feel you have enough. It's the truth. Worship your own body and beauty and sexual allure and you will always feel ugly, and when time and age start showing, you will die a million deaths before they finally plant you. On one level, we all know this stuff already-it's been codified as myths, proverbs, clichés, bromides, epigrams, parables: the skeleton of every great story. The trick is keeping the truth up-front in daily consciousness. Worship power-you will feel weak and afraid, and you will need ever more power over others to keep the fear at bay. Worship your intellect, being seen as smart-you will end up feeling stupid, a fraud, always on the verge of being found out. And so on. Look, the insidious thing about these forms of worship is not that they're evil or sinful; it is that they are unconscious. They are default-settings. They're the kind of worship you just gradually slip into, day after day, getting more and more selective about what you see and how you measure value without ever being fully aware that that's what you're doing. And the world will not discourage you from operating on your default-settings, because the world of men and money and power hums along quite nicely on the fuel o"},{"id":18492840,"author":"_Microft","month":"2018-11","story":"A Brand New Interview with David Foster Wallace","stance":"rec","why":"Commencement speech by David Foster Wallace.","quote":"Nobody has mentioned","text":"Nobody has mentioned \"This is water\" from a commencement speech [1] at a college yet, so let me do that. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9244752,"author":"fny","month":"2015-03","story":"Ask HN: Do you meditate?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"For five years now, I've struggled against torrents of thought and anxiety. I meditated and prayed begging for some relief to no avail. About four months ago, I found Ashtanga yoga, and to say that it has changed my life is an understatement. Mentally, I have reawakened. I now think patiently and deliberately with a discipline and focus I've never experienced. A child-like enthusiasm about the world, despite its suffering and strife, relit within me: hope and faith abounds. Occasionally, of course, I still do struggle, but I struggle in peace and with confidence. The physical changes have been equally striking. I've always had poor posture, mild scoliosis, and a hip imbalance. Now, that's all gone: my posture is remarkable and effortless, and I carry a sense of self with every step. I also kick way more ass at CrossFit than ever. ;) Unlike most other yoga, Ashtanga intensely focuses on the breath as well, which has increased my aerobic capacity dramatically. I'm of the opinion that meditation alone lacks the mind-body dynamic of Ashtanga yoga. Ashtanga trains you to stay focused, keep calm, and breathe deeply despite the intense (and sometimes painful) physical practice. The physical aspect is also a beautiful reminder that mental and physical transformation takes patience and time. Struggles with strength, endurance, and flexibility serve as a tangible marker of one's progress and the work that remains. Gauging the progress of one's mind is insidiously difficult, particularl"}],"r":[["0fKBhvDjuy0",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DJtef410XaM",1]]},"F3ntGDm6hOs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an approachable way into C and low-level programming that gets to something visible quickly.","q":{"x":"I think Handmade Hero's[0] intro to C is good","i":22027182,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":8798900,"author":"stephenheron","month":"2014-12","story":"Ask HN: What new or hot technology do you recommend learning?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Building a game from scratch in C opened eyes to C's power.","quote":"The intro videos really opened my eyes to how powerful C is.","text":"I found this video series ( [VIDEO] ) very interesting. It is someone (Casey Muratori) building a game from scratch using C. The intro videos really opened my eyes to how powerful C is. Being able to directly access memory seems crazy for someone who is just used to dealing with languages that really remove you from the hardware."},{"id":33205823,"author":"jcalabro","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: Teaching Greybeard IT","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent intro to C on Windows, building up how computers work.","quote":"It starts with an excellent intro to C on Windows[1] and works its way up from there.","text":"It's not quite aimed at IT/System admin, but Handmade Hero[0] is an excellent resource for learning how a computer works from the ground up. It starts with an excellent intro to C on Windows[1] and works its way up from there. It's really long and involved, but I've found that internalizing the general ethos is quite valuable, even if you just watch a handful of episodes. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22027182,"author":"thomascgalvin","month":"2020-01","story":"We’re approaching the limits of computer power – we need new programmers","stance":"rec","why":"Good intro to C that gets to shiny colors on screen quickly.","quote":"I think Handmade Hero's[0] intro to C is good","text":"> I challenge you to find a C “boot camp.” C isn't a language that lends itself to bootcamp-stye learning. With Javascript, you can get something on-screen in a few minutes, and even if you make mistakes, you will normally see something . It's a more forgiving environment. With C, a small error prevents compilation at all, and it's going to be a relatively long time before you're ready to progress past the \"printing text to the console\" stage. C is flatly harder to learn, and unless you're the kind of person who likes mental challenge, it's less rewarding than Javascript. It isn't the kind of thing you tackle because you need hirable job skills by the end of the month. There are still some excellent C tutorials out there (for example, I think Handmade Hero's[0] intro to C is good, and Handmade Hero itself gets you to the \"shiny colors on the screen\" stage very quickly), but HH has a different mentality than a bootcamp. HH is about learning, exploring, breaking things, and figuring them out on the fly. A bootcamp is about gathering the minimum knowledge necessary to be productive as quickly as possible. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21736026,"author":"LucaSas","month":"2019-12","story":"How to write better game libraries","stance":"rec","why":"Good resource for learning C and low-level software.","quote":"As a good resource for learning C I would recommend Casey Muratori's tutorials","text":"As a good resource for learning C I would recommend Casey Muratori's tutorials: [VIDEO] If you are interested in learning more about writing complex software in C consider checking out HandmadeHero. Sadly there need to be more good resources on learning how to write good C and low level software. I am hoping my article can be a starting point for people who wish to learn about library design for example."},{"id":8605616,"author":"axotty","month":"2014-11","story":"Handmade Hero: C game from scratch","stance":"reference","why":"Intro video centered on Visual Studio; commenter asks about non-Windows support.","quote":"the preview/introduction video seems pretty centered around visual studio","text":"Wow. I can't even begin to express how much the premise behind this series resonates with me. I've been looking for something just like this. My only issue is that, although in C, the preview/introduction video seems pretty centered around visual studio[0]. In the FAQ you say that multi platform support for *nix systems will be added later. How exactly will this work for those of us who want to follow along, but don't have Windows? [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["r-TLSBdHe1A",1]]},"H46ON2FB30U":{"wy":"Recommended as the quickest way to see what TeXmacs can do, as a WYSIWYG alternative to LaTeX.","q":{"x":"See here a short video describing its features","i":34425000,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":29951272,"author":"amichail","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: What would be a better name for TeXmacs?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows TeXmacs capabilities","quote":"The software is amazing as you can see from this video","text":"I'm not a TeXmacs developer but I am a fan. The software is amazing as you can see from this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35347083,"author":"lejalv","month":"2023-03","story":"Tectonic – A modern, complete, self-contained Tex engine with Unicode support","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of programmable interactive typesetter","quote":"you may want to check out what it can do","text":"Not really what you asked for -- but there is an interactive modern typesetter that is programmable and works on trees. It's called TeXmacs - you may want to check out what it can do -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":34425000,"author":"mgubi","month":"2023-01","story":"A Programmable Markup Language for Typesetting [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Short overview of TeXmacs features","quote":"See here a short video describing its features","text":"Have you considered TeXmacs (www.texmacs.org)? See here a short video describing its features: [VIDEO] and the twitter feed for more examples: [LINK]"},{"id":29674100,"author":"amichail","month":"2021-12","story":"LaTeX Cookbook – Collection of LaTeX Recipes","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of WYSIWYG TeXmacs editor","quote":"Have you tried the WYSIWYG TeXmacs?","text":"Have you tried the WYSIWYG TeXmacs? (It's not based on TeX nor emacs but is inspired by both.) See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27573996,"author":"plafl","month":"2021-06","story":"Julia: Faster than Fortran, cleaner than Numpy","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of TeXmacs as integrated technical editor","quote":"The closest thing for me is using TeXmacs","text":"I'm sure there is so much room for improvement here. The closest thing for me is using TeXmacs: [VIDEO]"}]},"_bDXXWQxK38":{"wy":"It is the go-to accessible explainer whenever HN discusses Helion or alternative fusion designs.","q":{"x":"I recommend you watch this excellent video by Real Engineering","i":37676752,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37676752,"author":"TechTechTech","month":"2023-09","story":"Nucor and Helion to Develop 500 MW Fusion Power Plant by 2030","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent introduction to Helion.","quote":"I recommend you watch this excellent video by Real Engineering","text":"If you want to learn more about Helion, I recommend you watch this excellent video by Real Engineering [VIDEO]"},{"id":36969358,"author":"PuffinBlue","month":"2023-08","story":"Electronic Structure of LK-99","stance":"rec","why":"Describes Helion's approach avoiding tokamak and laser fusion issues.","quote":"It seems their approach removes many of the issues with Tokamak or Laser Pulsed fusion.","text":"Regarding fusion - have you seen Helion’s approach? [0] It seems their approach removes many of the issues with Tokamak or Laser Pulsed fusion. It also reduces most of the feedstock issues with the two part fuel breeding approach. Their approach seems easier to be optimistic about. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34584179,"author":"mpwoz","month":"2023-01","story":"Cargo airships could be big","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating overview of Helion's fusion approach.","quote":"This video was a fascinating watch","text":"There's a fusion startup called Helion near Seattle working on this, it'll be really exciting if it pans out at scale. This video was a fascinating watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34039469,"author":"jseliger","month":"2022-12","story":"Why fusion will never happen","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Helion's fusion approach, including D+D operation.","quote":"Video regarding Helion that sama just posted","text":"Video regarding Helion that sama just posted: [VIDEO] . Note that Helion says it can get D + D to work, which eliminates much of the OP's concern about tritium. The company is also hiring: [LINK] I do not personally have the ability or knowledge to independently evaluate claims, but the history of \"it will never work\" for something that does not outright violate the laws of physics as we understand them is notable too."},{"id":34069618,"author":"HarHarVeryFunny","month":"2022-12","story":"Nuclear physicist explains why fusion ignition is hailed as a major breakthrough","stance":"rec","why":"Presents Helion as a practical commercial fusion design.","quote":"I'm far more excited by the innovative Helion fusion design","text":"I'm far more excited by the innovative Helion fusion design, which unlike the NIF is intended for commercial use, and is in an advanced stage of development. NIF seems more like a way to conduct military related nuclear physiscs research rather than intended/suitable to ever be used for power generation. [VIDEO] Helion seems far more practical than the Tomahawk designs such as ITER, which seem they'll be too expensive to compete with solar/wind."},{"id":44770750,"author":"Animats","month":"2025-08","story":"Helion begins work on Washington nuclear fusion plant","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"They've been through a few generations of test machines. They have something called \"Polaris\". It was supposed to be finished around the end of 2024.[1] Their own site still talks of it as being under construction. Discussion on Reddit.[2] They previously built something called \"Trenta\".[3] That generates two balls of plasma and fires them at each other. There's no fusion or fusion fuel. It's a test rig for plasma generation and manipulation. That was running two years ago. \"Polaris\" is a scale-up of Trenta, with something to fuse, and with energy recovery. It's very unclear how far that project has progressed. If they were getting energy out, that would be big news. Helion is vague about how that's progressing. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44800283,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2025-08","story":"EPA Moves to Cancel $7B in Grants for Solar Energy","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Helion's pulsed compression fusion prototype from 2022.","quote":"Video from Real Engineering showing Helion's Pulsed Compression Fusion reactor prototype (2022).","text":"Or the fact that Microsoft believes they have cracked *fusion* power. Sun in a bottle. Microsoft is breaking ground in WA state building a new fusion reactor. [LINK] EDIT: Adding description to video link Video from Real Engineering showing Helion's Pulsed Compression Fusion reactor prototype (2022). The same kind of reactor Helion is building in WA state for Microsoft. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43964013,"author":"AntiEgo","month":"2025-05","story":"University of Texas-led team solves a big problem for fusion energy","stance":"reference","why":"Explains compressing plasma magnetically and harvesting energy directly, no steam.","quote":"Here is the video where I learned about it","text":"The steam reactor I guess you might be describing is tokamak, which i agree will be a dead end technology. There are interesting small fusion reactors that skip the steam step. They compress plasma magnetically, and when the fusion happens, the expanding plasma in turn expands the magnetic field, and the energy is harvested directly from the field. No steam and turbines. Here is the video where I learned about it: [VIDEO] Maybe any physicists in this thread could share insight on how feasible this is? Your main point stands of course: this is a moonshot project, and solar works TODAY!"},{"id":40826382,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-06","story":"Will we ever get fusion power?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Helion Energy has a contract to deliver Microsoft a fusion reactor by 2028.","text":"No, there are multiple projects and companies that are making good progress toward commercially practical fusion reactors. Helion Energy has a contract to deliver Microsoft a fusion reactor by 2028. It's only four years away so we don't need to speculate about it. Helion will either deliver or they won't: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] MIT's Commonwealth Fusion Systems has started construction of their small scale SPARC reactor. Once that has proven itself they will build a larger scale ARC reactor. They should get to first plasma in 2025: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":38323884,"author":"torginus","month":"2023-11","story":"Greg (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Helion's fusion works.","quote":"Here's a video on explaining how it works","text":"I just realized it's THAT Helion. Their fusion experiments are not without controversy. Here's a video on explaining how it works: [VIDEO] And here's a video explaining what's wrong with the scheme: [LINK] But the TLDW version is that environment required for fusing He3 with deuterium also leads to deuterium fusing with itself, a reaction that creates neutron radiation that irradiates its environment."},{"id":37403522,"author":"westurner","month":"2023-09","story":"The Federal Helium reserve is for sale","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how helium isotopes act as fusion fuel and byproduct.","quote":"Here's the part of the Real Engineering video where it is explained how 3He and 4He Helium isotopes are fusion byproducts","text":"Helion Energy [LINK] : > They are developing a magneto-inertial fusion technology to produce helium-3 and fusion power via aneutronic fusion, [2][3] which could produce low-cost clean electric energy using a fuel that is derived exclusively from water. [4] Here's the part of the Real Engineering video where it is explained how 3He and 4He Helium isotopes are fusion byproducts and fuel used to generate electricity with nuclear fusion: [VIDEO] (From \"When Will Fusion Energy Light Our Homes?\" (2023) [LINK] ) Doesn't this new nuclear fusion process also change the valuation of the Tritium in [Fukushima Daiichi,] heavy water? Why would you dump money into the ocean?"},{"id":36379555,"author":"zmgsabst","month":"2023-06","story":"Scientists create contained ball of turbulence in a tank","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"We have that part covered; though unclear that we could get four systems into close enough proximity without negatively impacting each other. [VIDEO] Still, if it allowed for more complete burning and/or less power input per axis…"},{"id":34615078,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-02","story":"Cargo airships could be big","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Helion's repeatable pulsed fusion reactor.","quote":"Helion have a 7th generation nuclear fusion reactor fusing once every ten seconds or so.","text":"Yes they do exist, link elsewhere in this thread, Helion have a 7th generation nuclear fusion reactor fusing once every ten seconds or so. [VIDEO] (It's not surplus of power, but it is repeatable fusion actually happening)."},{"id":34582798,"author":"westurner","month":"2023-01","story":"When Will Fusion Energy Light Our Homes?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Watched the Helion Learn Engineering video, too","text":"Watched the Helion Learn Engineering video, too: [VIDEO] Has that net-positive finding been reproduced yet in any other Tokamoks? How does this compare to Helion's (non-Tokamok, non-Stellerator fusion plasma confinement reactor) published stats for the Trenta and Polaris products? Could SYLOS or other CPA Chirped Pulse Amplification lasers be useful for this problem with or without the high heat of a preexisting plasma reaction to laser pulse next to? [LINK]"},{"id":34039784,"author":"zmgsabst","month":"2022-12","story":"Why fusion will never happen","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Helion's mostly aneutronic fusion approach.","quote":"Helion and similar methods side-step the issue by using (mostly) aneutronic fusion.","text":"Helion and similar methods side-step the issue by using (mostly) aneutronic fusion. [VIDEO] [LINK] Also, discussing solar and wind without discussing the base load/storage problem skews the discussion towards solar/wind. Steam plants don’t face those challenges in the way those two do; storage is as much a challenge for solar as steam is for nuclear."},{"id":34056214,"author":"hinkley","month":"2022-12","story":"Nuclear physicist explains why fusion ignition is hailed as a major breakthrough","stance":"reference","why":"Interview-style source on Helion's plasma collision and direct energy capture.","quote":"they are trying to smash two plasma balls together and then directly capture the magnetic flux created by the resulting fusion","text":"Too late to edit, but my source on the 2 months comment comes from an interview with Helion, who I think made the front page a couple of weeks ago, but in summary they are trying to smash two plasma balls together and then directly capture the magnetic flux created by the resulting fusion, rather than using a heat engine: [VIDEO] They talk about why ITER has no viable fuel source, and about how they (Helion) are going to have to run two configurations of fusion plants in order to source their own fuel. One that produces a 10% power surplus, undesirable neutrons and the fuel for their power plants. They didn’t say anything but I suspect they’ll end up coming up with a use for the neutrons, perhaps making fuels for other processes. Someone will likely eyeball that waste heat as well."}],"r":[["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["3wMKoSRbGVs",1],["qv5b1Xowxdk",1]]},"iDulhoQ2pro":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible, clear walkthrough of the transformer paper for people with some AI background.","q":{"x":"The Yannic kilcher review is quite good.","i":35978097,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34572457,"author":"bfung","month":"2023-01","story":"The Transformer Family","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed, simple explanation of the transformer paper","quote":"I find the channel amazing explaining super complex topics in simple enough terms for people who have some background in AI.","text":"YouTube channel that explains the paper in detail: [VIDEO] And subsequent follow-ups (ROME, editing transformer arch): [LINK] I find the channel amazing explaining super complex topics in simple enough terms for people who have some background in AI."},{"id":35978097,"author":"throwawaymaths","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: Can someone ELI5 Transformers and the “Attention is all we need” paper","stance":"rec","why":"Good review explaining transformers and attention","quote":"The Yannic kilcher review is quite good.","text":"The Yannic kilcher review is quite good. [VIDEO] I can't ELI5 but I can ELI-junior-dev. Tl;dw: Transformers work by basically being a differentiable lookup/hash table. First your input is tokenized and (N) tokens (this constitutes the attention frame) are encoded both based on token identity and position in the attention frame. Then there is an NxN matrix that is applied to your attention frame \"performing the lookup query\" over all other tokens in the attention frame, so every token gets a \"contextual semantic understanding\" that takes in both all the other stuff in the attention frame and it's relative position. Gpt is impressive because the N is really huge and it has many layers. A big N means you can potentially access information farther away. Each layer gives more opportunities to summarize and integrate long range information in a fractal process. Two key takeaways: - differentiable hash tables - encoding relative position using periodic functions NB: the attention frame tokens are actually K-vectors (so the frame is a KxN matrix) and the query matrix is an NxNxK tensor IIRC but it's easier to describe it this way"},{"id":29629126,"author":"visarga","month":"2021-12","story":"The potential of transformers in reinforcement learning","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible review of the Attention Is All You Need paper","quote":"For accessibility I recommend Yannic Kilcher video review of","text":"For accessibility I recommend Yannic Kilcher video review of \"Attention Is All You Need\" [VIDEO] Yannic has been making about 62 other transformer paper reviews since. You can find the usual suspects. [LINK]"},{"id":24895357,"author":"kanche","month":"2020-10","story":"Rethinking Attention with Performers","stance":"rec","why":"Video walkthrough of the Attention paper theory","quote":"for the theory bit you may find Yannic Kilcher's videos [0,1] helpful","text":"Everyone has a different way of approaching. I like video walk-throughs of papers. If you are the same, for the theory bit you may find Yannic Kilcher's videos [0,1] helpful. On implementation side, the best project out there is [2] with good documentation. Also spacy has transformer package [3] (I personally haven't tried it), so maybe it will be easier for you to jump in if you have prior experience with spacy. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":43959425,"author":"throwawaymaths","month":"2025-05","story":"Writing an LLM from scratch, part 13 – attention heads are dumb","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I'm not the originator of the hash table analogy. I got it from here","text":"> I love these word-salad exaltations of LLMs - people don't even know what they're writing half the time but it sure is provocative! Well FWIW I have done an implementation of Llama LLM (3 8-70b to be specific) in non-python so I do sort of know what I'm talking about. I'm not the originator of the hash table analogy. I got it from here: [VIDEO] Hell, the vectors that are generated are called K/V, so. Yeah it's a hash table. And the idea that first order logic and other facets of intelligence can just be cleverly arranged lookup tables comes from here: [LINK]"}],"r":[["2pWv7GOvuf0",1],["lhYGXYeMq_E",1],["-KxjVlaLBmk",1],["iOWamCtnwTc",1],["Gh-Tff7DdzU",1]]},"PZbqAMEwtOE":{"wy":"Commenters share it as a funny, memorable example of evasive depositions and of obstruction over public records requests.","q":{"x":"The New York Times did an amazing reenactment from transcripts of the legal deposition","i":29139584,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":45958360,"author":"vel0city","month":"2025-11","story":"Cities panic over having to release mass surveillance recordings","stance":"rec","why":"Funny dramatization of a transcript about copying fees.","quote":"NYTimes made a funny dramatization of a transcript from that case, pretty good.","text":"I'm reminded of the case of a recorder's office in Ohio charging $2 per page for copies of public documents. NYTimes made a funny dramatization of a transcript from that case, pretty good. What Is a Photocopier? [VIDEO]"},{"id":43441972,"author":"philsnow","month":"2025-03","story":"Sync Engines Are the Future","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"said dramatization is brilliant nonetheless","text":"> Over time, the meaning of the word 'Xerox' changed. More specifically, it gained a new meaning. For a long time, Xerox only referred to a company named in 1961. Some time in the late 60s, it started to be used as a verb, and as I was growing up in the 70s and 80s, the word 'Xerox' was overwhelmingly used in its verb form. [VIDEO] I don't think this dramatization (of a court proceedings from 2010) is related to Xerox's plight with losing their trademark, but said dramatization is brilliant nonetheless"},{"id":29139584,"author":"wodenokoto","month":"2021-11","story":"Dealing with the Evasive Witness","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing reenactment of a deposition about a photocopier.","quote":"The New York Times did an amazing reenactment from transcripts of the legal deposition","text":"There is a similar story about an office worker not knowing what a photocopy machine is. The New York Times did an amazing reenactment from transcripts of the legal deposition [VIDEO]"},{"id":24454492,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2020-09","story":"Revisiting the spectacular failure that was the Bill Gates deposition","stance":"rec","why":"Actors play out a real deposition transcript.","quote":"The NYT has an amazing video where they got actors to play out the transcript","text":"The NYT has an amazing video where they got actors to play out the transcript of one of these sorts of depositions. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19127286,"author":"azernik","month":"2019-02","story":"Attacking a Pay Wall That Hides Public Court Filings","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"this hilarious deposition was in a case about this exact issue (fees for copies of public records).","text":"Sidenote - this hilarious deposition was in a case about this exact issue (fees for copies of public records). [VIDEO]"},{"id":16665299,"author":"tmuir","month":"2018-03","story":"Facebook Use of Sensitive Data for Advertising in Europe [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here's a verbatim reenactment of a deposition in the case where a county employee puts on a charade","text":"Its probably safe to assume that the person you're recommending this to has absolutely no idea about the general concepts of software development, or the rough orders of magnitude of information that is reasonable to store and serve with modern infrastructure available to nearly any small business. Its reminiscent of the willful ignorance displayed by the Cuyahoga County Recorders Office, in Ohio. The office responded to FOIA requests by demanding $2 per photocopy, even though the documents were already stored on CDs. They hoped they could simply make freedom of information too expensive. Their legal argument was that the files could be semantically thought of as photocopies of the documents. Here's a verbatim reenactment of a deposition in the case where a county employee puts on a charade of not knowing that a photocopier is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30321722,"author":"isitmadeofglass","month":"2022-02","story":"Brainwashing is sorcery","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You could run that through a group of kindergartners and 30/30 would tell you he requested a lawyer. It’s only a person with a special kind of learned stupidity, or malice that can claim that they are not sure if it was a request for a lawyer. Reminds me of the “what is a photocopier” dialogue: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21291234,"author":"arjenschat","month":"2019-10","story":"Four kinds of documentation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Being THE term is the holy grail for consumer brands. You can only get huge when your brand is the synonym for a whole concept or industry. Like ColaCola, Uber, Facebook, Tweet etc etc. For example Mark's explanation of Facebook [1] or the Photocopier sketch [2] Edit: fixed link order [1] [LINK] (video) [2] [VIDEO] (video)"},{"id":14915634,"author":"MengerSponge","month":"2017-08","story":"Larry Page Deposition in Uber vs. Waymo","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"here's my favorite deposition related thing on the internet","text":"While we wait for someone with legal training to parse this out for us, here's my favorite deposition related thing on the internet: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uxC1fPE1QEE",1],["T647CGsuOVU",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1],["d-ruFmgTpqA",1],["FzF1KySHmUA",1],["YsmgPp_nlok",1],["GvtNyOzGogc",1]]},"f-wWBGo6a2w":{"wy":"HN fans say it offers value even to atheists by interpreting Bible stories as psychological and evolutionary archetypes, and some credit it with helping them through depression.","q":{"x":"You can be a hardline atheist and still get tons of value out of these lectures.","i":16195631,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":23251870,"author":"dagav","month":"2020-05","story":"Surviving Depression","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture series that helped turn depression around","quote":"I cannot overstate the profound effect they had on my life","text":"I highly recommend you listen to these lecture series. I cannot overstate the profound effect they had on my life, by giving me the tools I needed to turn my depression around and give me a sense of balance and control in my life. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":16195631,"author":"nicolashahn","month":"2018-01","story":"What’s So Dangerous About Jordan Peterson?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interprets Bible stories as archetypal patterns tied to psychology and evolution","quote":"You can be a hardline atheist and still get tons of value out of these lectures.","text":"For those interested in his non-gender identity content: His series on the Bible: [VIDEO] is generally regarded (by those I know, at least) as Jordan at his best. He goes through the first few stories in the Bible and tries to bring meaning to them, not from the fundamental Christian lens of \"this is absolute truth,\" but more like: \"Here are some archetypal patterns about the world and how to act in it that our ancestors have discovered over thousands of years and distilled into stories. Maybe they hold truth that isn't the 'word of God' truth, but observations about the world that have utility to us even as a modern, rational, scientific civilization. I'll back this up by tying it to studies on psychology, neuroscience, evolution, and animal behavior, and show that variations of the same archetypal stories appear in non-Judeo-Christian societies all over the world.\" You can be a hardline atheist and still get tons of value out of these lectures. I also found his 2017 Maps of Meaning lectures particularly interesting, and personally more relevant: [LINK] He does a similar thing with the story of Pinocchio instead of the Bible for the first few lectures, which might be more palatable for some. There's a lot of overlap with the Bible series as well."},{"id":27739831,"author":"alfor","month":"2021-07","story":"Dabrowski’s theory and existential depression (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains in evolutionary terms how Bible stories arose and what they mean","quote":"Take a look at Jordan Peterson biblical series, it explain in evolutionary terms how we invented theses stories","text":"There might not be meaning to the universe, but there is meaning in the human enterprise. We went from animals, to highly effective and conscious of ourselves and the universe. The human developpement keep pushing forward. I think that the main cause of meaninglessness is our rejection of our own culture ( mostly Christian). There is good reason to reject them as the bearded magician in the sky seem stupid to a modern science minded person, however if you look at those stories at a second degree there is a lot of parts that make sense. Theses stories were the bedrock of our civilisation and the spine of our consciousness. We need to update theses stories with what we now know instead of throwing them out because science make them look outdated. Take a look at Jordan Peterson biblical series, it explain in evolutionary terms how we invented theses stories, what they mean, what we can learn from them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40046014,"author":"tim333","month":"2024-04","story":"Ask HN: What movies changed your perception of reality or life?","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson is reasonably good on the Bible","quote":"on his home turf of the Bible he's pretty good.","text":"I watched a bit of that and a bit of the start of the biblical lectures ( [VIDEO] ) The Some More News is a bit unfair in that he's going on about something he doesn't understand - climate science. Whereas on his home turf of the Bible he's pretty good. I mean if you'd interviewed Einstein on say salsa dancing you might have thought he's an idiot. It's not a good way to judge."},{"id":18751044,"author":"squirrelicus","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Did you learn any life changing lessons this year?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I want to be extra clear: All the things I believe about religion are rooted in a belef in neurochemical reactions in meatbrains in a purely, exclusively, hard-materialistic perspective. I also want to state that I'm trying to see what you're written in the best possible light. I mean that. But I might fail, and for that I apologize in advance. I'm not trying to straw man, and if you think I am, know that's not my intention and feel free to clarify. > Could you list some of the truths of being human? Also give some examples of psychological realities of the human brain? Sure. This is going to take quite a bit of effort to unpack. But first, let me address the remainder of your post because I think it's important context. But my answer to this question is at the end. > The only ones I know of are biological in nature, as we are living, breathing, conscious, sentient organisms. I used to think us humans were all skeletons, but in reality we're just brains. And our brains, like all matter, are made out of atoms. The body parts are just the physical layer. Think about that next time you talk to someone face to face, you're two brains communicating with each other. Indeed. In fact, while the center of our perception might be our brains, what we are at a deeper level is worms. Coelemates, to be precise. We are incredibly sophisticated survival systems for a digestive tract. In any case, the things you said are probably true, and I do accept them as true with a high degree of confid"}],"r":[["I8Xc2_FtpHI",2],["ZggCipbiHwE",1],["5YV_iKnzDRg",1]]},"ffkIQrq-m34":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a sober critique of agile, though one commenter found it mixed.","q":{"x":"Agile!: The Good, the Hype and the Ugly by Bertrand Meyer is a must read.","i":26351003,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26351003,"author":"rramadass","month":"2021-03","story":"Ask HN: Do you think Agile/Scrum is beneficial for software delivery?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Meyer's critical analysis of agile's good and bad parts","quote":"Agile!: The Good, the Hype and the Ugly by Bertrand Meyer is a must read.","text":"Agile/Scrum in limited doses to aspects of Software Development is basically common sense. Applying it mechanically to the entire Product/Project Development life cycle is dumb. The \"Agile Manifesto\" is just well known platitudes. Agile!: The Good, the Hype and the Ugly by Bertrand Meyer is a must read. Summary: [LINK] Webcast: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18218023,"author":"DanPristupov","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Meyer's critique of agile methods","quote":"Here are some of my favorites","text":"Here are some of my favorites: How To Design A Good API and Why it Matters [LINK] Agile Methods The Good, the Hype and the Ugly [VIDEO]"},{"id":38898706,"author":"rramadass","month":"2024-01","story":"Niklaus Wirth, 1934-2024: Geek For Life","stance":"rec","why":"Meyer's critique of agile and upfront design","quote":"See also Bertrand Meyer, \"Agile! The Good, The Hype and The Ugly","text":"What you say has been well known and that is why techniques like Barry Boehm's \"Spiral Model\" - [LINK] were invented long ago. But \"Incremental Design\"/\"Refactoring\" as practiced by XP/Agile/Scrum crowd is not that at all. It is giving up on \"Upfront Design\" altogether because Requirements/Design is subject to change anyway. The fact that you will have unknowns should not be an excuse to not come up with a blueprint i.e. Design for the overall system. The focus should be on the long-term with decisions/allowances/techniques made for extension/maintainability/refactoring within the blueprint. That is the point of \"Upfront Design\". The XP/Agile/Scrum crowd have effectively thrown the \"baby out with the bathwater\" with the result that their methodology has degenerated to \"think short-term and make shit up as you go long-term\". See also Bertrand Meyer, \"Agile! The Good, The Hype and The Ugly\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23941663,"author":"theptip","month":"2020-07","story":"Cold Showers: For when people get too hyped up about things","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I found the presentation to be somewhat mixed.","text":"> Agile Methods: The Good, the Hype and the Ugly (Video) ( [VIDEO] ) Thoughts on this one? I found the presentation to be somewhat mixed. I found the initial comb through of the agile principles to be needlessly pedantic (\"'Simplicity... is essential' isn't a principle, it's an assertion!\"); anyone reading in good faith can extract the principle that's intended in each bullet of that manifesto. The critique of user stories (~35 mins in) was more interesting; it's something we've been bumping up against recently. I think the agile response would be \"if your features interact, you need a user story covering the interaction\", i.e. you need to write user stories for the cross-product of your features, if they are not orthogonal. I'm not really convinced that this is a fatal blow for user stories, and indeed in the telephony example it is pretty easy to see that you need a clarifying user story to say how the call group and DND features interact. But it does suggest that other approaches for specifying complex interactions might be better. Maybe it would be simpler to show a chart of the relative priorities or abstract interactions? E.g. thinking about Slack's notorious \"Should we send a notification\" flowchart ( [LINK] ), I think it's impossible (or at least unreasonably verbose) to describe this using solely user stories. I do wonder if that means it's impossible for users to understand how this set of features interact though? Regarding the purported opposition in agile to crea"}],"r":[["n8Dd5aVXLCc",1],["144uOfr4SYA",1],["x5Efg42-Qn0",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["GcJxRqTs5nk",1],["pQ0CvjAJXz4",1],["yErKTVdETpw",1],["f7CW7S0zxv4",1],["A-fxij3zM7g",1],["aAb7hSCtvGw",1]]},"lyqt6u5_sHA":{"wy":"HN links it in stellarator and fusion threads as an understandable look at the design and engineering challenges.","q":{"x":"This video explains the elements quite in an understandable way","i":13150383,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":14646977,"author":"nitrogen","month":"2017-06","story":"Artistically shaped magnets may make stellarators easier to manage than ITER","stance":"rec","why":"Overview video of the reactor.","quote":"Look for videos about the reactor on YouTube. Like this one.","text":"Look for videos about the reactor on YouTube. Like this one. [VIDEO] Also search HN for previous articles."},{"id":14647416,"author":"okket","month":"2017-06","story":"Artistically shaped magnets may make stellarators easier to manage than ITER","stance":"rec","why":"Engineering challenges and solutions in a 10-minute video.","quote":"The engineering challenges and their solutions are described in detail in this video","text":"The engineering challenges and their solutions are described in detail in this video: \"Wendelstein 7-X - from concept to reality\" (10m30s) [VIDEO]"},{"id":13150383,"author":"saganus","month":"2016-12","story":"Confirmation of the topology of the Wendelstein 7-X stellarator’s magnetic field","stance":"rec","why":"Understandable explanation of the reactor's elements.","quote":"This video explains the elements quite in an understandable way","text":"How the hell do you test such a monstrosity? I mean... I know that testing complex software projects is a pain in the ass. But... this? I mean... how do you even begin to plan where all the wires and other elements go and then test that it's a sound design? I'm guessing they do a lot of simulations and such, but what if something fails? how much do you have to backtrack to fix a potential bug? It just boggles the mind. I had no idea these things were so complex. Thanks for the links. Edit: This video explains the elements quite in an understandable way [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10712427,"author":"sawwit","month":"2015-12","story":"The first plasma: the Wendelstein 7-X fusion device is now in operation","stance":"rec","why":"Technical explanation of how Wendelstein 7-X works.","quote":"A more technical video explaining how it works","text":"Here is a time lapse video of the 9 year construction, quite fascinating to watch: [LINK] YouTube mirror: [LINK] A more technical video explaining how it works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10455431,"author":"amckenna","month":"2015-10","story":"First New U.S. Nuclear Reactor in Two Decades to Begin Fueling in Tennessee","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed look at the Wendelstein 7-X design.","quote":"And here is a great video showing the design in amazing detail","text":"Actually there are potentially some large breakthroughs in fusion power depending on how the new Wendelstein 7-X does when it is turned on later this year. Stellarators are pretty cool and if they prove viable could lead to a big shift in the field away from tokamaks which have stalled in efficacy improvements. Here is an article about it: [LINK] And here is a great video showing the design in amazing detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10491248,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2015-11","story":"Germany is turning on its monster stellarator","stance":"reference","why":"Wendelstein 7-X from concept to reality.","quote":"","text":"Wendelstein 7-X - from concept to reality: [VIDEO] Im Zeitraffer: Zusammenbau von Wendelstein 7-X: [LINK] (best @ 0.56x) Previously: [LINK] More: [LINK]"},{"id":10491975,"author":"justifier","month":"2015-11","story":"Germany is turning on its monster stellarator","stance":"reference","why":"Claims 2.5 Tesla magnetic field strength.","quote":"the first video claims a magnetic field strength of 2.5 Tesla","text":"the first video claims a magnetic field strength of 2.5 Tesla(o) roughly that of an MRI(i) can anyone speak to sort of considerations that lead to this value? 2.5T seems insignificant considering the events it will be confining how sensitive is the field to magnetic interactions outside of the device? (o) [VIDEO] (i) [LINK]"},{"id":10437772,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2015-10","story":"Wendelstein 7-X – a fusion reactor with a twist","stance":"reference","why":"Wendelstein 7-X from concept to construction.","quote":"","text":"Wendelstein 7-X - from concept to reality: [VIDEO] Im Zeitraffer: Zusammenbau von Wendelstein 7-X: [LINK] (best @ 0.56x)"}],"r":[["etx0k1nLn78",1],["aokNwKx7gM8",1],["ua5aOFi-DKs",1],["0Z4aF-qiziM",1],["gEKSpZPByD0",1],["SrU9YDoXE88",1],["U6qZPx4uD0g",1],["wtV5ev6813I",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1]]},"q7K0vRUKXKc":{"wy":"HN points to it as a staple pg talk, and it appears in lists of the best startup videos and tech talks.","q":{"x":"He outlines many ways why it's not merely ethically appropriate to be good","i":6302931,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":16840041,"author":"a_d","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Entry in a favorite tech talks list","quote":"Paul Graham - Startup school (2008)","text":"Bret Victor - Inventing on principle [LINK] Alan Kay - Power of Simplicity [LINK] Steve Jobs - Marketing (unveiling Think Different) [LINK] Robert Cialdini - On Influence [LINK] Peter Thiel - On Zero to One (Notes on Starts Ups or How to Build the Future) [LINK] Naval Ravikant on Reading, Happiness, Systems for Decision Making, Habits, Honesty and More (Farnam Street podcast) [LINK] Josh Wolfe - On \"This is who you are up against\" podcast [LINK] Richard Feynman - Fun to Imagine [LINK] Paul Graham - Startup school (2008) [VIDEO] James Burke - Connections (BBC documentary) <No link> Jim Al-Khalili tells the story of the Atom (BBC) [LINK] Elon Musk's 2003 Stanford University Entrepreneurial Thought Leaders Lecture [LINK] Ken Robinson - Do schools kill creativity? [LINK] Paul Stamets on mycology, bioremediation and fungi- Joe Rogan Experience [LINK]"},{"id":6302931,"author":"darushimo","month":"2013-08","story":"Dark Patterns - User Interfaces Designed to Trick People","stance":"rec","why":"Graham's 'Be Good' talk on why being good is strategically superior","quote":"He outlines many ways why it's not merely ethically appropriate to be good","text":"I think you're right here, but the qualifier \"all things being equal\" can't be taken lightly. Rarely if never are \"all things equal.\" I also don't think a shift in the market context is necessary. We already have a market place where people who feel tricked or duped will have a lower opinion of the company doing the duping. dark patterns have a bad edge to them too--very few of them, when noticed by the user, leave the user actually feeling good about how they were duped. This kinda reminds me of the talk PG gave at 2008 start up school titled \"Be Good.\" ( [LINK] or [VIDEO] ) He outlines many ways why it's not merely ethically appropriate to be good, but also a strategically superior approach to running a startup. Ease of use, simplicity, trustworthiness--these are important qualities of a biz's reputation, both internally and in public perception. And Dark patterns tend to harm that reputation. Edit: Addendum--obviously there are monopolies or monopoly-like situations where reputation isn't important."},{"id":2808897,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2011-07","story":"Ask HN: What are the best videos to watch about building startups?","stance":"rec","why":"Entry in a list of startup videos","quote":"(pg at Startup School '08)","text":"[LINK] - (Steve Blank / Eric Ries - part 1) [LINK] - (Steve Blank / Eric Ries - part 2) [LINK] - (Steve Blank / Eric Ries - part 3) [LINK] - (Steve Blank at the Startup Lessons Learned conference) [LINK] - (Steve Blank: The Democratization of Entrepreneurship) [LINK] - (Alexander Osterwalder speaking @ Google) [LINK] - (Eric Ries speaking @ Google) [VIDEO] - (pg at Startup School '08) [LINK] - (Peter Norvig at Startup School '08)"},{"id":1633321,"author":"JeanPierre","month":"2010-08","story":"Paul Graham and his \"no asshole\" rule (Interview)","stance":"rec","why":"A good Paul Graham talk","quote":"I really suggest to watch","text":"Never heard him? Not to derive this into some \"list of Paul Graham's speaks\", but I really suggest to watch [VIDEO] . A good speak, I think."},{"id":10058159,"author":"jordigg","month":"2015-08","story":"Octopart (YC W07) is Joining Altium","stance":"reference","why":"Graham talks about being good, not just not evil","quote":"how companies succeed not just by not being evil but by being good","text":"Yesterday I had PG's Startup School 08 video[1] on the background and he spoke about them just after being a cockroach and how companies succeed not just by not being evil but by being good. Really happy for them. Great team. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":1761266,"author":"terrym","month":"2010-10","story":"The Dream Is Over: Music Labels Have Killed Their Digital Future","stance":"reference","why":"Talk where Graham says YC funds what people apply with","quote":"","text":"I remember watching a video of a talk you gave at startup school 08 and near the end during the Q&A, you said something to the effect of \"we fund what people apply with, which usually ends up being some form of facebook or something to do with independent music\". It seems that if the labels have positioned themselves as unfavorable toward startups to the point of making an anti-platform, that there would be demand for an independent, label-free music buying/selling experience for startups to work with. Yet the only thing I think of that comes close was the old version of thesixtyone's marketplace. Are the startups that apply to ycombinator in this area trying to build something outside of the current music ecosystem and failing (and why)? Or is everyone just trying to work with the current system and finding that it isn't feasible/profitable? The talk: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LNjJTgXujno",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["ZTidn2dBYbY",1],["3wMKoSRbGVs",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1],["cidchWg74Y4",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["NdSD07U5uBs",1]]},"3EPTfOTC4Jw":{"wy":"Recommended as the detailed, entertaining history of why 3D Pinball vanished from Windows.","q":{"x":"If you have the time, I highly recommend checking out the NCommander's video talking about Space Cadet Pinball.","i":30441076,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30441076,"author":"registeredcorn","month":"2022-02","story":"The cats sitting on a fence in early builds of Windows 8","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed history of why Space Cadet Pinball left Windows.","quote":"If you have the time, I highly recommend checking out the NCommander's video talking about Space Cadet Pinball.","text":"If you have the time, I highly recommend checking out the NCommander's video talking about Space Cadet Pinball. [VIDEO] He references the same blog, and goes into some pretty incredible detail about the history of what happened to pinball coming pre-installed on Windows."},{"id":39813639,"author":"Kwpolska","month":"2024-03","story":"The Format Dialog in Windows NT","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Pinball issues on 64-bit Windows XP Itanium edition.","quote":"A great video explaining this is","text":"There were issues with Pinball on \"Windows XP 64-bit Edition\", or the Itanium version. A great video explaining this is [VIDEO]"},{"id":28859872,"author":"nazgulsenpai","month":"2021-10","story":"SpaceCadetPinball – Decompilation of 3D Pinball for Windows","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why 3D Pinball lacked 64-bit versions, including debugging story.","quote":"I learned a lot about 3D Pinball recently on this video [0].","text":"I learned a lot about 3D Pinball recently on this video [0]. It's specifically about the 64bit versions of 3D Pinball (or the lack thereof) and the reasons why. Part of that is debugging which relates to this. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28868470,"author":"leeter","month":"2021-10","story":"SpaceCadetPinball – Decompilation of 3D Pinball for Windows","stance":"rec","why":"YouTuber suffers through Itanium to test 64-bit pinball.","quote":"Warning contains a youtuber bashing their head firmly against the pain of Itanium to test this.","text":"Yes... [VIDEO] Warning contains a youtuber bashing their head firmly against the pain of Itanium to test this."}],"r":[["0rCUF-V1esM",1]]},"3TX3kV3TICU":{"wy":"HN users say it made Vim's completion system click for them.","q":{"x":"it \"clicked\" for me after watching this video","i":15507810,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":29113993,"author":"raman162","month":"2021-11","story":"Happy birthday Vim (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers ctags, jumplist and word, line and block completion.","quote":"I think the most important video I watched about vim was about text completion in a thoughtbot meetup","text":"I've only used vim for three years and it's been a fantastic experience. Every time I'm in a normal editor I long for vim. I think the most important video I watched about vim was about text completion in a thoughtbot meetup [0]. There I learned about ctags, the jumplist and ways to complete words, sentences, lines and blocks of code. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15507810,"author":"justinmk","month":"2017-10","story":"Vim after 15 Years","stance":"rec","why":"Made Vim's completion system click for the commenter.","quote":"it \"clicked\" for me after watching this video","text":"There's a lot of engineering in Vim as well. E.g., Vim's completion system is actually real cool once you learn it (it \"clicked\" for me after watching this video: [VIDEO] ). Vim regex, folding, and spellcheck also have useful features that you won't find anywhere else. More generally, I agree with you. If it were up to me I would prefer to use Emacs as a baseline. But it's not up to me, just like it wasn't up to Google to decide that Javascript wins. Ubiquity wins. Emacs lost. > for throwing everything and the kitchen sink into our editor. ... But with vim & neovim, people are building a complex operating environment That's not really true. The explicit goal of Nvim is to be embedded and focus on \"peer to peer\" nvim instances. That's the opposite of Emacs' constant reminder that there needs to be a single Emacs daemon controlling everything, and you \"should never leave Emacs\"."},{"id":14132086,"author":"AdmiralAsshat","month":"2017-04","story":"Switching to Sublime Text from Vim in 2017","stance":"rec","why":"Taught basic command completion in Vim.","quote":"This presentation taught me alot","text":"To go to the reverse route, I'm a SublimeText and Vim user that's debating going back to pure vim. Much of the functionality that I used ST for I found Vim could do naturally, with no plugins. This presentation taught me alot ( [VIDEO] ), such as basic command completion. There is some stuff that Sublime can still do that I don't think Vim can (multi-cursor editing comes to mind, and that's still really handy when I'm making edits to HTML). Other stuff can be done by both but I happen to like ST's better (the awesome GitHub package comes to mind). And finally, I did pay $70 for an ST license, so I imagine I'll keep using it for another few years or so. But given that I do alot of remote administration on RHEL/CentOS boxes where vim is the only thing I can safely assume to be installed, it doesn't hurt to stay fluent in vim for such a day."}]},"3WMuzhQXJoY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a thoughtful account from a feminist on men's issues and the cost of saying unpopular things.","q":{"x":"This is a good TEDx talk from a feminist","i":27594968,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":20570031,"author":"daenz","month":"2019-07","story":"When Having Friends Is More Alluring Than Being Right","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feminist filmmaker's TED talk on what she learned about MRAs.","quote":"It's a fascinating watch","text":"The Red Pill documentary by (feminist!) Cassie Jaye opened my eyes to what MRAs are all about. It's a fascinating watch, and I'd honestly be astonished at anyone who comes away from watching it thinking that they have no legitimacy. Her TED talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27594968,"author":"cassianoleal","month":"2021-06","story":"The incel lexicon: Deciphering the emergent cryptolect of a global community","stance":"rec","why":"Feminist filmmaker reports what she learned interviewing so-called enemies.","quote":"This is a good TEDx talk from a feminist","text":"Indeed. This is a good TEDx talk from a feminist who went to make a documentary about what she described as \"a misogynistic hate-group working against women's equality\" and about what she learned after a year interviewing \"the enemy\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":27596234,"author":"Popegaf","month":"2021-06","story":"The incel lexicon: Deciphering the emergent cryptolect of a global community","stance":"rec","why":"A feminist's view of the Men's Rights movement.","quote":"Here's what a feminist has to say about the Men's Rights movement","text":"And you should probably look into what the Men's Rights Activists are actually advocated for prior to passing judgement on a whole group. Be aware that news in the US is very polarized and that online communities are often converge to echo chambers. The comments you read online and the people you meet there are probably the most active there. IRL, (at least in Europe) people are much less extreme than they are online. Remember that online, people tend to be more crass. Don't read The New York Times or BBC at their word, just like you wouldn't take The Wallstreet Journal or The Washington Times at their word. Sentences like > Self-identified \"mens' rights activists\", by and large, advocate for mens' rights in much the same way as the Democratic Peoples' Republic of Korea is very, very democratic. Can easily be modified to replace \"mens' rights\" with \"feminists\" and \"feminism\" and be just as wrong. Here's what a feminist has to say about the Men's Rights movement [VIDEO]"},{"id":26068442,"author":"Geminidog","month":"2021-02","story":"I followed my dreams to get demoted to software developer","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter urges others to watch it on men's issues.","quote":"I think you need to watch the video below","text":"How is my statement biased? Men can suffer from unfair treatment as well. I never said anything to discount feminism, I simply stated one case where men are treated less well then women and I caveated that statement saying that this was based off of my own anecdotal experiences implying that your experience could be different. Do you think it's possible to live in a world where women are the only gender that is mistreated? Or do you think it's more realistic to live in a world where infractions are committed against both genders? This is off topic, but if my statement about this one case pissed you off because you're an ardent supporter of womens' rights I think you need to watch the video below and keep in mind... I literally never said anything AGAINST women's rights. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26614766,"author":"neonological","month":"2021-03","story":"Female Founder Secrets: Men Clamming Up","stance":"reference","why":"Activist feminist's career destroyed for saying something unpopular.","quote":"Below is an TED talk about the story of a activist feminist who had her entire activist career destroyed","text":"I went into this article thinking it was going to be an outrage piece about men being sexist towards women. Instead it's about men being afraid of being accused of being sexist. This is a very real issue. It's already gotten to the point where the people behind the movement are hysterically unreasonable and irrational. Literally even feminists who are integral parts of the movement itself aren't safe from their own vitriol. Below is an TED talk about the story of a activist feminist who had her entire activist career destroyed simply by saying something that the cancel culture disagreed with: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19550821,"author":"FakeComments","month":"2019-04","story":"New Siblings, Old Secrets","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Food for thought","text":"I sometimes wonder if we’ll see a genuine men’s rights movement around the question of false paternity, now that it’s trivial to document. There are many issues that disproportionately impact men — but that’s one that basically uniquely impacts men, and where women’s rights movements (such as feminism) have shown a distinct anti-male tendency. Food for thought: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16018057,"author":"mantas","month":"2017-12","story":"Men Resist Green Behavior as Un-Manly","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"A great example would be Cassie Jaye and her film","text":"The question is wether they want to make the world better or just push certain agendas. A great example would be Cassie Jaye and her film [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hNDgcjVGHIw",1],["ciStnd9Y2ak",1],["zGcKURD_osM",1]]},"RjfbvDXpFls":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a first-hand argument for building your own lean agent harness instead of relying on bloated, vendor-controlled ones.","q":{"x":"There is a really good video by the author of pi.dev","i":49168842,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49168842,"author":"sbysb","month":"2026-08","story":"Harness Engineering for Self-Improvement","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues for custom harnesses over bloated lab-controlled system prompts.","quote":"There is a really good video by the author of pi.dev","text":"There is a really good video by the author of pi.dev (which I have used to build some of my own harnesses): [VIDEO] The basis of the argument is that the labs are constantly pushing updates to their system prompts that are used in claude code or codex, which are exceptionally bloated and change the sand beneath your feet with every update. By rolling your own harness, as long as you keep up with the latest advances and changes in the ecosystem, you capture a lot of the 'control' that LLM-based development feels like it strips from you. Obvious disclaimer that I use pi.dev when I am aiming for consistency, not absolute quality. Custom harnesses on pi are what I ship, claude code is still my fallback if I need to make sure a PR is the highest absolute quality"},{"id":48976502,"author":"kzrdude","month":"2026-07","story":"Claude Code uses Bun written in Rust now","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on building pi amid AI slop.","quote":"is a good listen","text":"Mario Zechner's talk \"Building Pi in a World of Slop\" is a good listen, on the topic of adults in a \"world of slop\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":48320347,"author":"currywurst","month":"2026-05","story":"Zot now supports Claude Opus 4.8","stance":"rec","why":"Creator of pi explains why he built it.","quote":"the creator of pi will echo more or less your exact concerns as to why he developed it","text":"In 18min , Mario Zechner , the creator of pi will echo more or less your exact concerns as to why he developed it [VIDEO] Enjoy !"}],"r":[["T-TwcmT6Rcw",1],["2AXv49dDQJw",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["Jib2AmRb_rk",1]]},"j-kj2qwJa_E":{"wy":"HN people point to it as a very visual demonstration of what hot-reloading and interactive development can offer, and as an example of a great dev tool pitch.","q":{"x":"it's a very visual demonstration of what hot-reloading code and interactive development can bring","i":15038558,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15038558,"author":"JamesLeonis","month":"2017-08","story":"Ask HN: Lispers: Which dialect of Lisp do you use and why?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visual demo of hot-reloading and interactive development.","quote":"it's a very visual demonstration of what hot-reloading code and interactive development can bring","text":"If you're interested in Clojure, I _highly_ recommend /r/Clojure on Reddit. It's very active; several library devs, book authors, and core maintainers frequently post and comment. There's a weekly thread dedicated to new Clojure users ([Latest thread here][1]). The Clojure Slack[6] is even more active if you have a burning question. If you would like to dip your toes in the water, 4Clojure[2] is an online interactive Clojure Kata. There's also a (nearly) full REPL environment at Try Clojure[3] or repl.it[4]. The Clojure Cheatsheet[5] makes it easy to browse and discover the Clojure standard library, and IMHO is _so_ well organized! But the biggest part of Lisp is the interactive development. You really have to see it, and I love showing the [Bruce's Figwheel project presentation][7] because it's a very visual demonstration of what hot-reloading code and interactive development can bring. It also helps that he's doing Clojurescript in a live browser for web development. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [LINK] [6]: [LINK] [7]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27282078,"author":"lmilcin","month":"2021-05","story":"ClojureScript 1.10.866 Release","stance":"rec","why":"Where the commenter learned figwheel.","quote":"This is youtube video from which I learned about fighwheel","text":"Sure. I use fighwheel. [LINK] Actually, when I develop with clojure, I typically have both backend and frontend running at the same time and I am concurrently modifying both applications without restarting them. This is way, way more convenient than having to restart application and then to bring it to a state to run your test. This is youtube video from which I learned about fighwheel: [VIDEO] Have fun!"},{"id":14841749,"author":"freshhawk","month":"2017-07","story":"Show HN: Mesh IDE – a code editor that feels like a spreadsheet","stance":"rec","why":"Talks on interactive tooling with figwheel.","quote":"Bruce Hauman has some good talks about his tools, which are GUI application-centric and a joy to use","text":"I'm not sure if the parent did, but I followed what you were looking for and the answer is still \"try clojure\", perhaps more specifically it is \"Try the Clojure libraries figwheel and devcards to do web stuff\" but the push for immediate feedback and \"interactive programming\" is central for everyone. It's a community that took Bret Victor's Inventing on Principle talk to heart, already agreeing due to the REPL centric workflows that most people used. Bruce Hauman has some good talks about his tools, which are GUI application-centric and a joy to use [1][2][3] If you aren't doing web or native mobile GUI stuff then the default tools have immediate unit test/linting/spec compliance feedback triggered every time you save a file. Unless you mean you want a very simplified language, in which case you should look elsewhere. Clojure has definitely chosen the high power side of that trade-off, but this also allows some truly first class tools to be developed. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] <- this one in particular is original and outstanding in my opinion"},{"id":31782511,"author":"swyx","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best dev tool pitches of all time?","stance":"reference","why":"Figwheel pitch listed among favorite dev tool pitches.","quote":"Figwheel-Clojurescript","text":"i also asked this on twitter so just putting some replies here: - Doug Engelbart [LINK] - Twilio [LINK] - Stripe - \"7 lines of code\" - Netlify - [LINK] - Heroku - [LINK] and git push heroku master - Cloudflare - [LINK] - Node ( [LINK] ) and Deno ( [LINK] ) - Firebase (reportedly) - [LINK] smaller companies/less impactful pitches that i still like - Redux [LINK] - Stackblitz [LINK] - Comm [LINK] - Mongodb [LINK] - Let's Encrypt [LINK] - Figwheel-Clojurescript [VIDEO] - Svelte [LINK]"},{"id":15173148,"author":"resurge","month":"2017-09","story":"A Frameless Geodesic Dome (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk that mentions the author's dome.","quote":"I watched a video of the talk where this dome is mentioned last week.","text":"This is Bruce Hauman's dome (creator of the Clojure library Figwheel) I watched a video of the talk where this dome is mentioned last week. [VIDEO] I was hoping there'd be some more info about the way he lives in general. Seeing from the outside picture he also seems to grow his own food."},{"id":13380557,"author":"TheSmoke","month":"2017-01","story":"A deep dive into ClojureScript reagent","stance":"reference","why":"Figwheel presentation cited in a workflow write-up.","quote":"you get figwheel!","text":"here is my experience: - creating a project from scratch is easy: lein new reagent-frontend my-project. it creates a project from reagent-frontend template. you can build your app with lein cljsbuild auto or build a production build with lein release. - build time significantly drops, even with advanced optimizations, comparing to webpack. - both component and app state management becomes a lot easier thanks to reagent atoms. - not much of a big deal but hiccup templates make inline styling easier. - you get figwheel! [0] [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OINa46HeWg8",1],["CK62I-4cuSY",1],["KZjFVdU8VLI",1],["ovD0aLdRUs0",1],["d-7o9xYp7eE",1]]},"wVp5koAOu9M":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend the channel's alternative-living videos, and this one for its look at a net-zero desert home.","q":{"x":"this fun video about a net zero desert house","i":30277013,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48741713,"author":"culi","month":"2026-07","story":"Forestiere Underground Gardens","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Channel full of fascinating videos on alternative homes.","quote":"Almost all the videos on this channel are fascinating btw.","text":"Almost all the videos on this channel are fascinating btw. Greenhouses enveloping entire houses/villages[0][1] in northern Europe to earthships[2] to someone growing citrus in Nebraska using trenches[3] to entire villages underground in Australia[4] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] I'd also recommend the channels FLORB and Happen Films though they're admittedly a little corny. [LINK] [LINK] Crime Pays But Botany Doesn't recently did a tour of an interesting alt home as well. [LINK]"},{"id":30277013,"author":"somethoughts","month":"2022-02","story":"Separating urine from sewage could mitigate some environmental problems","stance":"rec","why":"Fun look at net zero desert house with water cycle.","quote":"this fun video about a net zero desert house","text":"Kinda of reminds me of this fun video about a net zero desert house(/commune?). They proposed/demostrated a cycle that is basically Shower -> indoor plants -> toilet -> outdoor plants New Earthships capture more energy, water & food at lower cost [VIDEO]"},{"id":24068955,"author":"205guy","month":"2020-08","story":"Arcosanti","stance":"rec","why":"Earthship community tour in Taos, among channel's alternative living videos.","quote":"Earthship community in Taos New Mexico","text":"Kirsten Dirksen Seems to be finding and documenting them on her YouTube channel: [LINK] Many are just individuals in alternative living situations or creative housing, but some are communities or -in-the-making: Earthship community in Taos New Mexico: [VIDEO] Back-to-the-traditional-village in Portugal: [LINK]"},{"id":33163672,"author":"cookiengineer","month":"2022-10","story":"Geothermal may beat batteries for energy storage","stance":"reference","why":"Earthship water reuse and passive cooling systems.","quote":"","text":"This reminds me a lot of the Earthship community in the middle of the desert, which always focusses on smart ways of reusing water for different purposes, and by not wasting water as much as possible. The houses usually have a clean/grey/blackwater + a rainwormbox system to process fecals and reuse it for growing plants. Their air conditioning system is basically just a pipe in the ground where the hot air flows through, cools down, and automatically gets pushed through the house when they open the windows on the roof. I was always wondering why there are no systems converting the unused electricity in potential energy by moving water to a higher ground. And more importantly: Why there are no water storages on the roof. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["S0dSm_ClcSw",1],["86gyW0vUmVs",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["ZD_3_gsgsnk",1],["aXQ2lO3ieBA",1],["ICp2-EUKQAI",1],["u4L130DkdOw",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["RWTic9btP38",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["fCbAafKLqC8",1],["EWqThb9Z1jk",1]]},"CUjt36SD3h8":{"wy":"HN commenters link it to correct the widespread misconception about why light slows in a medium.","q":{"x":"See this video for an explanation of why light travels slower through a medium with an index of refraction greater than one.","i":34213495,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38600589,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2023-12","story":"What is a zero refractive index material?","stance":"rec","why":"Fermilab videos on refractive index.","quote":"Fermilab has done several videos on this topic as well that I found highly informative","text":"For anyone who's interested, Fermilab has done several videos on this topic as well that I found highly informative: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":34213495,"author":"djmips","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Concepts that clicked only years after you first encountered them?","stance":"rec","why":"Why light is slower in media with higher refractive index.","quote":"See this video for an explanation of why light travels slower through a medium with an index of refraction greater than one.","text":"I had thought because light was absorbed and re-emitted but that and scattering ideas are incorrect. See this video for an explanation of why light travels slower through a medium with an index of refraction greater than one. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25590195,"author":"gugagore","month":"2020-12","story":"Well, Actually – Physics Answers","stance":"rec","why":"Corrects the misconception about light slowing in media.","quote":"For a video to address this misconception of the speed of light being constant","text":"For a video to address this misconception of the speed of light being constant, and it just appears to slow down due to ping-pong, I recommend \"Why does light slow down in water?\" by Fermilab: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25590260,"author":"vecter","month":"2020-12","story":"Well, Actually – Physics Answers","stance":"reference","why":"Source refuting the absorb and re-emit explanation.","quote":"This is wrong. Two sources [0][1].","text":"> Well, actually, the speed of light is constant, it just takes time to ping-pong around a lattice/gas. Photons hit particles, get absorbed, and re-emitted, which causes an effective slowdown at a larger scale. A gas is just vacuum with particles floating around in it. When photons travel between those particles, they are traveling at c. The effective slowdown is determined by the lattice/gas, and we denote this slowdown with the refractive index... This is wrong. Two sources [0][1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["rEoc0YoALt0",1],["3MghYhf-GhU",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["fKSSY1gzCEs",1],["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["Ds-v2-qyCc8",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1]]},"Lq-Y7crQo44":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining demonstration of how powerful algorithmic gerrymandering can be.","q":{"x":"For a great (and entertaining!) look into how you can use simulations to generate these types of maps","i":36239567,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":44780896,"author":"Spivak","month":"2025-08","story":"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how powerful gerrymandering can be.","quote":"Required watching to put things into perspective of how powerful gerrymandering can be","text":"Required watching to put things into perspective of how powerful gerrymandering can be [VIDEO] There was a proposed (and failed) ballot initiative in Ohio that tried to address this in the only way you actually can. The tl;dr is that there is no such thing as a fair map and so the state would be required to gerrymander the districts to match the state-wide election results. Basically proportional representation with extra steps, but brings the \"secret metric\" in map making to the forefront and requires it be a specific value so you can't tune it to your favor."},{"id":36239567,"author":"opdahl","month":"2023-06","story":"Math Has Changed the Shape of Gerrymandering","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining look at generating district maps with simulations.","quote":"For a great (and entertaining!) look into how you can use simulations to generate these types of maps","text":"For a great (and entertaining!) look into how you can use simulations to generate these types of maps you should check out this video by the superb channel AlphaPhoenix: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36035404,"author":"j5155","month":"2023-05","story":"What if you did the exact opposite?","stance":"reference","why":"Related example of automated gerrymandering with randomness.","quote":"reminds me of AlphaPhoenix’s automated gerrymandering project","text":"This aspect of adding randomness to avoid local randomness reminds me of AlphaPhoenix’s automated gerrymandering project: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NVAmnV65_zw",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"PezlFNTGWv4":{"wy":"HN cites it in discussions about how media should report mass shootings, as an expert-backed argument for not glorifying perpetrators.","q":{"x":"Basically at the end the forensic psychiatrist says that giving this intense superficial coverage about the gunman only encourages more copycat killers.","i":4289232,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":36239411,"author":"chx","month":"2023-06","story":"Charlie Brooker Got ChatGPT to Write a Black Mirror Episode","stance":"rec","why":"Brooker on how mass shootings are reported.","quote":"Charlie Brooker is fantastic","text":"Charlie Brooker is fantastic: On how news is reported [LINK] On how mass shooting is reported and about the saturation [VIDEO]"},{"id":6897029,"author":"chestnut-tree","month":"2013-12","story":"News is bad for you","stance":"rec","why":"Clips show the formulaic way news is reported.","quote":"These clips from Charlie Brooker (British TV presenter) give a good sense of the formulaic way news is reported","text":"I'm from the UK and I'd go so far as to say the majority of the British press are toxic. They have an incredibly inflated view of themselves and they couldn't care less about what's in the public interest (just look at how they've responded to the recommendations of the Leveson Inquiry which investigated the repulsive ethics and culture of the British press). The UK press empahtically follow their own self-interests and agendas (and that includes broadsheets like The Guardian). There will always be individual reporters who rise above the abysmally low standard of reporting - but they are a tiny minority. TV news is better in my opinion - they at least aspire to some measure of impartiality. These clips from Charlie Brooker (British TV presenter) give a good sense of the formulaic way news is reported (strong language in the videos). [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4289232,"author":"swang","month":"2012-07","story":"Accused Movie Killer James Holmes 'Refused to Program in an Object-Oriented Way'","stance":"rec","why":"Psychiatrist says intense coverage of gunmen encourages copycat killers.","quote":"Basically at the end the forensic psychiatrist says that giving this intense superficial coverage about the gunman only encourages more copycat killers.","text":"I want to share this video from Charlie Booker. [VIDEO] Basically at the end the forensic psychiatrist says that giving this intense superficial coverage about the gunman only encourages more copycat killers. I don't know what compelled me to click the link even after watching the video and telling myself to stop following more coverage, but that may be all the more reason why it needs to stop. I honestly don't care what his intentions are, and I think the biggest punishment to him would be if everyone just forgot his name and he be left to pass the rest of his life in jail sitting in relative obscurity."},{"id":31739835,"author":"Zhyl","month":"2022-06","story":"Murder-Suicides by Pilots Are Vexing Airlines as Deaths Mount","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This kind of thing always makes me think of the well-known Charlie Brooker (creator of Black Mirror) clip","text":"This kind of thing always makes me think of the well-known Charlie Brooker (creator of Black Mirror) clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20610596,"author":"mhh__","month":"2019-08","story":"Terminating Service for 8Chan","stance":"reference","why":"Brooker foresaw that coverage signals attention to potential shooters.","quote":"As pointed out by an ever prescient Charlie Brooker almost ten fucking years ago.","text":"> you signal a green light to all the other potential shooters that this is how you get attention. As pointed out by an ever prescient Charlie Brooker almost ten fucking years ago. ( [VIDEO] ). On a similar subject, The Samaritans publish very specific and thorough guidelines as to how to report and publish suicides ( [LINK] ) which are still ignored by many even if unknowingly."},{"id":20070324,"author":"finnthehuman","month":"2019-06","story":"US newspapers run more photos of school shooting suspects than victims (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows responsible shooting coverage is well known.","quote":"It's not a secret how to responsibly cover shootings in the news, and hasn't been for 30 years.","text":"Showing victims vs suspects is a con. It's not a secret how to responsibly cover shootings in the news, and hasn't been for 30 years. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19400944,"author":"nabla9","month":"2019-03","story":"Facebook, YouTube Blindsided by Mosque Shooter's Live Video","stance":"reference","why":"Guidance on how to report mass murders.","quote":"How to report mass murders - Charlie Brooker","text":"For the same reason they remove porn. You can still enjoy the murder porn by watching 24/7 hour endless media masturbation. How to report mass murders - Charlie Brooker [VIDEO]"},{"id":13109411,"author":"bargl","month":"2016-12","story":"Tell HN: Political Detox Week – No politics on HN for one week","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates problems with 24-hour news coverage.","quote":"I believe part of the issue is pointed to in this video.","text":"I empathize with your view, but I very much disagree with you. 1) It's an experiment, there should never be any disagreement with an experiment unless the experiment itself can cause harm to someone/something. The results of this experiment should be evaluated closely, and if they make new guidelines for HN I'm fully onboard. But it's an experiment. 2) The person who shot the gun did not read HN, I may be wrong but I feel this is a fair assumption. I'm not saying no one here could shoot a gun in public, but they wouldn't come here as anything but a complete troll and shoot a gun based on some crap story like that. If I'm wrong in this then the experiment is terrible and stop it now. But I very much doubt it. 3) The issue is that news in general has degraded. This degradation of journalism has led to many of the issues we experience today. If experimenting on HN can lead to some sort of anecdotal evidence that Politics = bad for communities I'm all for it. I believe part of the issue is pointed to in this video. [VIDEO] 24 Hours news is the issue. 24 Hours politics is the issue. Taking a purge is a great idea. 4) HN should not be where you get your news about politics."},{"id":11077844,"author":"whitegrape","month":"2016-02","story":"How Silicon Valley is dealing with mental illness","stance":"reference","why":"Expert argues media coverage drives copycat shootings.","quote":"(Skip to 1:30 if you want an argument from authority.)","text":"This is mostly true, but I don't think the evidence is there for videogames. What you might find evidence for w.r.t videogames is that they'll change your behavioral responses under stress, for example having played many shooter games, and given a real gun, then put in a stressful situation, you're more likely to pull the trigger on another human than otherwise. For mass shooting, absolutely true: [VIDEO] (Skip to 1:30 if you want an argument from authority.) I suspect it's true for suicides as well. People underestimate copycat / monkey-see-monkey-do influences."},{"id":10674253,"author":"Terr_","month":"2015-12","story":"Show HN: Don't Name the Shooter","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for correlation between media obsession and follow-up attacks.","quote":"There's a huge correlation between media-obsession after a shooting-tragedy and another attack occurring soon afterwards.","text":"> I get the \"sexinesss\" of denying the person the attention they wanted, but it doesn't help society. I think you have it totally backwards. If anything, it's \"sexy\" for news agencies to barf out everything they can know or speculate about the person. It lets them grab eyeballs, ratings, and fill airtime and pages. In contrast, removing the \"notoriety payoff\" is the sober , serious , and \"wise\" approach for society. There's a huge correlation between media-obsession after a shooting-tragedy and another attack occurring soon afterwards. [0] > We need to delve No, \"we\", the media-consuming audience, emphatically do NOT need to do any delving. 99.9% of us will never have expertise, and even if we did would be unable to act upon it. The people who do (sociologists, psychologists, etc.) are going to be able to get at the data anyway, they don't need to get their primary research from the 24-hour flashing-klaxon news-blast. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10388881,"author":"cuberator","month":"2015-10","story":"On the manifestos killers leave behind","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that media attention drives shootings.","quote":"i believe that its the media attention that is the biggest culprit.","text":"Very well written and interesting. While some people put the blame on firearms (lets not turn this into a debate on gun control) i believe that its the media attention that is the biggest culprit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8166254,"author":"ixwt","month":"2014-08","story":"Suicide Contagion and the Reporting of Suicide (1994)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of media sensationalism around shootings.","quote":"News media doing anything to get a story to grab viewers.","text":"Most people don't. This seems to be quite prevalent in most societies. News media doing anything to get a story to grab viewers. Another example is school shootings: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5581081,"author":"losethos","month":"2013-04","story":"Razer will not cancel 90%-off test coupons","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"In 9th grade when I moved to Goodyear, I was a year ahead in Math, so they put me one the 10-12 grade campus, taking all year ahead classes. I took Calculus my junior year. I did not have credit hours to graduate high school in 3 years, so I just exhausted free high school classes. I took short hand, accounting and electronic becausew I had already taken physics my junior year. Free courses. Then, I had a full scholarship to ASU -- all my tuition and a merit scholarship for having a 1440 SAT. I also had a Unidynamics scholarship. I gopt a master's in electrical engineering. We mocked psychology and business students. [LINK] Are we having fun yet? God says... 21:20 And Ahab said to Elijah, Hast thou found me, O mine enemy? And he answered, I have found thee: because thou hast sold thyself to work evil in the sight of the LORD. 21:21 Behold, I will bring evil upon thee, and will take away thy posterity, and will cut off from Ahab him that pisseth against the wall, and him that is shut up and left in Israel, 21:22 And will make thine house like the house of Jeroboam the son of Nebat, and like the house of Baasha the son of Ahijah, for the provocation wherewith thou hast provoked me to anger, and made Israel to sin. 21:23 And of Jezebel also spake the LORD, saying, The dogs shall eat Jezebel by the wall of Jezreel. 21:24 Him that dieth of Ahab in the city the dogs shall eat; and him that dieth in the field shall the fowls of the air eat. 21:25 But there was none like unto Ahab, w"},{"id":4922808,"author":"wlll","month":"2012-12","story":"Mass shooting at Sandy Hook Elementary School in Newtown, Connecticut","stance":"reference","why":"Argues for preventing widespread news coverage of shooters.","quote":"Prevent widespread news coverage","text":"Option 3. Prevent widespread news coverage: [VIDEO] Though that seems about as likely as \"1.Disarm everyone\"."}],"r":[["FF-tKLISfPE",1],["aHun58mz3vI",1]]},"jGWFg7EDnyY":{"wy":"HN commenters credit it for inspiring their own satellite-reception hobby and praise the channel's high-quality content.","q":{"x":"The Thought Emporium makes really high quality content.","i":31141113,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31141113,"author":"notsound","month":"2022-04","story":"How to pull images from satellites in orbit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"High quality content across varied projects from the channel","quote":"The Thought Emporium makes really high quality content.","text":"The sequel to this video: [VIDEO] The Thought Emporium makes really high quality content. He does a lot of things, most recently covering things in metal, but in the past they have made videos on gene editing, growing opals, and making holograms. It’s a lot of different topics, all presented very well."},{"id":18585417,"author":"c0nducktr","month":"2018-12","story":"Receiving NOAA Weather Satellite Images with $10 USB SDR Device (2014) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspired a hobby of receiving GOES satellite imagery","quote":"got inspired after watching this video","text":"I'm in the process of setting up an antenna for receiving data from the GOES-15 weather satellite. I'm just starting out but got inspired after watching this video (no affiliation with the channel). [VIDEO] People have been able to get some stunning pictures of the earth from those satellites. Examples: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] If you're interested, check out r/rtlsdr, which is where I first was introduced to the hobby. It's full of helpful people."},{"id":18619309,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2018-12","story":"Serial Port SDR","stance":"rec","why":"Series on using HackRF for radio telescope and satellite reception","quote":"A YouTube channel called The Thought Emporium has a series about using a sdr called the HackRF","text":"A YouTube channel called The Thought Emporium has a series about using a sdr called the HackRF to make a radio telescope and a satellite receiver for a few weather sats. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35821006,"author":"barbazoo","month":"2023-05","story":"The First Image from EUMETSAT’s Newest Satellite","stance":"reference","why":"Prompted a question about satellite downlink encryption","quote":"","text":"I was just watching [VIDEO] and I wonder if some satellites encrypt the content before sending it to earth."}],"r":[["8PmM6SUn7Es",1],["3AtBE9BOvvk",1],["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["GsyhGHUEt-k",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["Fy0aCDmgnxg",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["WZskjLq040I",1],["aVwxzDHniEw",1],["goePYJ74Ydg",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1]]},"yy8jQgmhbAU":{"wy":"HN cites it as a concrete, worked example of data-oriented design and as an alternative to OOP in performance-sensitive code.","q":{"x":"a more recent video on it from CppCon 2018 where the presenter goes over a specific worked example.","i":21676176,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":24265721,"author":"nikki93","month":"2020-08","story":"Unity Software Inc S-1","stance":"rec","why":"Data-oriented design applied to a web animation engine.","quote":"Interesting talk re: data oriented design for a webby animation engine here actually","text":"Glad to see some agreement there. I'm interested in building an ECS-based approach for a UI system at some point. The Yoga layout engine (implements flexbox -- [LINK] ) seems to actually be pretty amenable to just incorporating as a `Layout` or `Flex` component, then you could have components for platform-specific renderings (eg. `iOSTextInput`, `AndroidTextInput`, `DOMTextInput` in wasm, ...) that could read from cross-platform common components (`TextInput`). A core aspect would also be a `Hierarchy` component that just has a parent entity id and a list of child entity ids (that it keeps in sync properly). Then bind things to JS and allow you to code it from React, while still being able to eg. run an animation thread for some specific animations that just queries and reads/writes the `Layout` and `AnimationSpec`s etc. Interesting talk re: data oriented design for a webby animation engine here actually: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21676176,"author":"michael_j_ward","month":"2019-12","story":"Practical Examples in Data Oriented Design","stance":"rec","why":"Worked example of data-oriented design from CppCon 2018.","quote":"a more recent video on it from CppCon 2018 where the presenter goes over a specific worked example.","text":"Just adding a more recent video on it from CppCon 2018 where the presenter goes over a specific worked example. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18340845,"author":"phaedrus","month":"2018-10","story":"C++17 constexpr Compile-time Ray Tracer (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on data-oriented design and where polymorphism still fits.","quote":"the rest of the video is worth watching, too","text":"That's a very good point. It might be question of high versus low level. From a video I watched today (the rest of the video is worth watching, too, but the following stand-alone quote is apropos): \"Polymorphism and interfaces have to be kept under control. I don't believe that their role is in a very low level system like interpolating a couple of numbers in the animations, but they have their place in high level systems, in client facing APIs. They are amazing if you do a type of plug in and you really need some type of dynamic polymorphism.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":27660585,"author":"jfpardy","month":"2021-06","story":"Data-oriented design or why you might shoot yourself in the foot with OOP (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"One of several CppCon talks on data-oriented design.","quote":"","text":"There's been a few talks: CppCon 2014: Mike Acton \"Data-Oriented Design and C++\": [LINK] CppCon 2018: Stoyan Nikolov “OOP Is Dead, Long Live Data-oriented Design”: [VIDEO] Building a Data-Oriented Future - Mike Acton [2019]: [LINK] And a blog/book has been created: [LINK]"},{"id":24173357,"author":"nikki93","month":"2020-08","story":"Kosmonaut: web browser from scratch in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by a developer of a game UI rendering engine.","quote":"talk by one of the devs of that at","text":"Some UI rendering engines for games do something like this, eg. [LINK] -- talk by one of the devs of that at [VIDEO]"},{"id":22135383,"author":"valand","month":"2020-01","story":"Ask HN: What change in your programming technique has been most transformative?","stance":"reference","why":"Data-oriented design as a transformative programming technique.","quote":"","text":"Static typings + type inference Parse, don't validate [LINK] All libs/products should be pure functions, with input output documented, making libs/products predictable Use in-app event sourcing to reduce the need for global states states DoD [VIDEO] In non bare-metal languages, this will be useful for readability For errors, return instead of throw"},{"id":21753833,"author":"valand","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: How to improve code quality while maintaining decent velocity?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Having your PR rejected in a healthy environment means that your PR is radically far from the reviewer's expectation. Having your PR rejected in a toxic environment can mean a lot of things, and a company sought after doesn't mean that it MUST be not toxic. First, you have to make sure if both of YOU and YOU ENVIRONMENT is healthy. If both are healthy, proceed reading. > The challenge here is to deliver good quality code, while maintaining a decent speed of delivery. You made it sound like producing good quality code = slowness. FALSE. Seeming slow, good quality code boosts your team's velocity. BUT keep in mind: \"quality\" is \"the standard of something as measured against other things of a similar kind; the degree of excellence of something\". Your team has its standards, either is a derivative of other globally acknowledged standards such as [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] , etc, or self-made standards. You go follow them, make them happy! Coding and design skill has a different definition than code quality. It is what punches up to the sky: the overall velocity, the success of the team, the balance of growth and culture retainment of the company, the development experience, the users' thumbs ups, amazements, and \"this app is life changing\" comments, the number of milestone celebration in All You Can Eat restaurants each years, the number of people rising up from junior to senior each years, etcetera etcetera. Not only you make your team happy with the quality code based on their st"},{"id":20390315,"author":"drainyard","month":"2019-07","story":"Data-Oriented Design (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's plenty of good talks and literature on the subject and why it is applicable to game development, but also more generally. This talk by Mike Acton (Formerly Insomniac now Unity): [LINK] I recommend this talk all the time, since it is the one that got me convinced to look into DoD seriously. Also: More Mike Acton (now at Unity): [LINK] Stoyan Nikolov “OOP Is Dead, Long Live Data-oriented Design”: [VIDEO] Overwatch Gameplay Architecture and Netcode (More specifically about ECS): [LINK] The main argument is that you work with data so you should care about data. All a program does is take some input, do something with it, and give you an output. If you structure your program around this mindset, a lot of problems become much simpler. Concurrency is one thing that becomes much simpler to reason about now that you understand how your data is managed and moved around. Realisations that most of the time you have multiple of something and very rarely do you have a single thing. So having a lot of small objects of the same type each doing some work within their deep call-stack, rather than running through an array of them and doing the work you need to do. I disagree that encapsulation is a brilliant idea _in general_, because it promotes hiding data, and hiding data is not inherently good. There's obviously cases, where there is internally critical, but since all your state is simply tables of data, your program is just choosing to represent that data in some way, which can mak"},{"id":20210012,"author":"DeathArrow","month":"2019-06","story":"Classes vs. Data Structures","stance":"reference","why":"Data-oriented design as an alternative to OOP.","quote":"People are starting to use data oriented design instead of OOP.","text":"People are starting to use data oriented design instead of OOP. Data oriented design doesn't hide state, is generally faster and easier to comprehend as it doesn't abstract too much. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":19999666,"author":"leni536","month":"2019-05","story":"Compress objects, not cache lines: an object-based compressed memory hierarchy","stance":"reference","why":"Using arrays of primitives instead of objects.","quote":"An other cure for that is to use arrays of primitives a lot more","text":"In the article they mention that most programs are not dominated by arrays but objects. An other cure for that is to use arrays of primitives a lot more: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["W3aieHjyNvw",1]]},"7Vc8sxhy2I4":{"wy":"HN cites it as a firsthand account of how dysfunctional large IT projects can be, with about 50 contractors and no one in charge.","q":{"x":"I saw Mikey Dickerson give a great talk about his experience leading the completion of healthcare.gov","i":23898002,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23898002,"author":"beckler","month":"2020-07","story":"Companies made millions building unemployment websites that didn’t work","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk on leading healthcare.gov completion.","quote":"I saw Mikey Dickerson give a great talk about his experience leading the completion of healthcare.gov","text":"I saw Mikey Dickerson give a great talk about his experience leading the completion of healthcare.gov, and it sounds like you only saw a slice of it. This is the talk I attended: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12036265,"author":"beckler","month":"2016-07","story":"How a team of young people helped rebuild healthcare.gov (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how insane the initial recovery situation was.","quote":"I think it shows just how insane the situation was.","text":"I saw Mikey Dickerson talk about his experience in the initial recovery phase of healthcare.gov at Velocity in 2014. I think it shows just how insane the situation was. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11199722,"author":"FLUX-YOU","month":"2016-02","story":"Tech workers are increasingly looking to leave Silicon Valley","stance":"reference","why":"Describes dysfunction and wastage fixing healthcare.gov.","quote":"Wastage is immense in IT from the sounds of it in this talk","text":">How do you switch from one strain to another? End up around people that already have that strain, get their attention, and become friends so you can learn and they can teach. You unfortunately just can't teach yourself everything. It sounds like another form of pedigree selection. Or maybe dividing the field up into \"us vs. them\". Pretty understandable sentiment if you get pushed into fixing Healthcare.gov like he describes in one of his talks and working with guys arguing about tickets not existing because they're on different ticket systems and people with zero motivation to build a decent product. Wastage is immense in IT from the sounds of it in this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10247356,"author":"newjersey","month":"2015-09","story":"How Healthcare.gov Botched $600M Worth of Contracts","stance":"reference","why":"About 50 contractor firms and nobody in charge.","quote":"Mikey Dickerson talked about how there were ~50 contractor firms and nobody seemed to be in charge.","text":"Part of the issue is everyone who is present at every meeting gets billed for the time at the meeting for the project. Mikey Dickerson talked about how there were ~50 contractor firms and nobody seemed to be in charge. This is good for all contractors because if nobody's in charge then nobody gets in trouble. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10064092,"author":"finkin1","month":"2015-08","story":"The US digital service","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about the insane work hours fixing healthcare.gov.","quote":"the work hours were pretty insane to fix healthcare.gov","text":"According to this talk, the work hours were pretty insane to fix healthcare.gov. From the 2014 Velocity Conference in New York City, Mikey Dickerson's keynote: \"One Year After healthcare.gov: Where Are We Now? [VIDEO]"}]},"EhqZ0RU95d4":{"wy":"Recommended in Ask HN career threads as motivational advice to use spare hours to start building.","q":{"x":"One of the best inspirational videos that got me through that time","i":1396076,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":1396076,"author":"Cmccann7","month":"2010-06","story":"Ask HN: What to do about a boring job ?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Loud but inspirational push to use spare hours creating value","quote":"One of the best inspirational videos that got me through that time","text":"I was actually in this exact situation about ~8 months ago. One of the best inspirational videos that got me through that time was [VIDEO] . Caution: Gary is a little loud and obnoxious but he makes good points. Even if you have a 8-5 day job, work the 8pm-2am night shift and stop complaining. Take @edw519's advice and make a list of 10 things you can do to create value and start doing them. Even if you have to be somewhere for 8+ hours that sucks use that time to your advantage. There is no excuse for not making it happen, use that free time you do have to make meaning and create value."},{"id":874776,"author":"symptic","month":"2009-10","story":"ASK HN: I'm lost -- need direction","stance":"rec","why":"It's never too late; start small with passion and just do it","quote":"I think you could benefit greatly from Gary Vaynerchuk's ideas in this keynote","text":"I think you could benefit greatly from Gary Vaynerchuk's ideas in this keynote: [VIDEO] The general overview of what he says: it's never too late, you don't have to jump neck deep into it at first (i.e.: quit a job and take funding), you need more passion than Gandhi, and just fucking do it."},{"id":12592171,"author":"vblord","month":"2016-09","story":"Ask HN: What to do with 3K visitors a month?","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote on having a side project while working a day job","quote":"Here's that keynote where Gary talks about having a side project and working 9-5.","text":"That's a great dream and a great goal. Unfortunate there isn't a magic way to make 100k from a website without building a good product. I wish there was a way. As for the 100 visitors saying \"Hi, is anyone around here?\" The chicken and the egg problem is a very common problem with social sites. The most common way I've seen this solved is by the owners of the site \"faking\" responses or filling in their site with fake content to look like it is active. They do this just until the site takes off. But it sounds like your project is not that large yet and needs to be built up larger. I'd say for you to keep at it or start another project that you can get more traction in. For what it's worth... In my experience, only working a couple hours a week on a side project won't get you anywhere fast. I usually spend around 15 on my side projects a week. Otherwise, nothing will get done on them. You just need to make the time. As Gary Vaynerchuk puts it... go to work, go home, kiss the dog, and between 9PM and 1AM you need to crush it. I wish you the best and hope you can make something happen. Edit: Here's that keynote where Gary talks about having a side project and working 9-5. [VIDEO]"},{"id":874527,"author":"jnaut","month":"2009-10","story":"ASK HN: I'm lost -- need direction","stance":"reference","why":"Gary Vaynerchuk's advice to work a day job and build on the side","quote":"As Gary[ ] says","text":"First things first: There are too many checklists out there for telling you what it takes to be an entrepreneur, I am not talking about them. Do you believe beyond criticism, reason and logic that you have what it takes to be an entreprenreneur?? If the answer is yes, read on. Be sure about it or you will regret this. I think a good idea will be to take it slow. A phased migration over a time period of year+. phase1: For a few months at the start, maintain a status quo at current job just keep working right, but change your spending, living and saving habits. Meanwhile prepare your family for the change, slowly. You have lived too long under the cozy umbrella of constant and guaranteed paycheck at the end of every month, that has to change over to a not so persistent income and amounting only to live well and keep the kids just happy. Yes, as someone pointed out, convince and take along your spouse through this transition. Encourage her to take up a job if she is not already doing so. Save money. phase2: Save more money :-) Then move over to part time, at the same organization if they respect your work or some other organization. You can look at freelancing also, but that a different game, you will have to build that image slowly. Consider yourself lucky if you get good gigs at the start, which is not usually the case. Meanwhile, during this 2nd phase transition, start developing a small fun/serious project or for open source in your part time. As Gary[ [VIDEO] ] says: work 9"}]},"NTJQTc-TqpU":{"wy":"Cited as the legendary extreme SimCity build, and recommended as fascinating viewing with the creator's commentary.","q":{"x":"Don't miss the video","i":32235204,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":16935159,"author":"int_handler","month":"2018-04","story":"Magnasanti: Large and Terrifying SimCity (2010)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Creator discusses Magnasanti, inspired by Kowloon Walled City","quote":"(which I really recommend watching by the way)","text":"In his video he made for Magnasanti [0] (which I really recommend watching by the way), Oscala did mention that an earlier version of the city was in fact inspired by the Kowloon Walled City. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32235204,"author":"thematrixturtle","month":"2022-07","story":"Magnasanti – The largest and most terrible city of SimCity","stance":"rec","why":"Video of the Magnasanti city worth watching","quote":"Don't miss the video","text":"This appears to be a low-quality partial copy of a more detailed blog post from 2010, now gone but still living on via archive.is: [LINK] Don't miss the video: [VIDEO] Discussed previously on HN: [LINK] (620 comments)"},{"id":36040826,"author":"meerita","month":"2023-05","story":"SimCity 4, the greatest citybuilder of all time, was released 20 years ago","stance":"reference","why":"Legendary six-million-resident SimCity build","quote":"the most legendary build: MAGNASANTI","text":"I love SimCity 2000. I remember back then the most legendary build: MAGNASANTI (6 million residents, 2.523.010 commerce and 644.173 industry. 9172 million total population) [VIDEO]"},{"id":3675931,"author":"smalter","month":"2012-03","story":"Electronic Arts bringing back ‘SimCity’ franchise after 10-year absence ","stance":"reference","why":"Example of extreme SimCity cities without streets","quote":"the insane simcities that this guy built, , didn't have any streets.","text":"In my experience, what you wrote is true. But the insane simcities that this guy built, [VIDEO] , didn't have any streets."},{"id":3592471,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2012-02","story":"City Layout Design: A programmer's perspective","stance":"reference","why":"Magnasanti, an extreme SimCity city design","quote":"a guy designed the ultimate city, Magnasanti, in Simcity 4.","text":"Somewhat relevant, a guy designed the ultimate city, Magnasanti, in Simcity 4. Here's his video [1] and an interview [2]. Choice quote from the interview: Vice: I think most people’s natural inclination after watching your video would be awe, followed by fear. Vincent: I am in agreement. That was part of the intent of the video, for those that know, play, and love the game. Introversion, an indie game company in the UK who created hacker favorites like Uplink has unfortunately shelved their game project played in a fully procedurally generated city, from the major neighbourhoods down to the individual floors of the buildings. You could get a demo of the city generator from the recent Humble Bundle. There are still some fascinating videos to find on their blog [3] or on youtube [4] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [edit]: better link for the Subversion video"},{"id":38151966,"author":"petermcneeley","month":"2023-11","story":"Leaf blowers are a scourge. Why is it hard to get rid of them?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"What is this article about? If it is getting rid of noisy gas leafblowers im sure we are all on board. If it is about getting rid of beautiful suburbia and replacing it with Magnasanti then I think only a tiny minority want that. MAGNASANTI [VIDEO]"},{"id":37411650,"author":"ethbr1","month":"2023-09","story":"Is this duplo train track under too much tension?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant","text":"There has to be an internet law somewhere that any fun hobby can be obsessively following into madness. Relevant: [VIDEO]"}]},"S4gU2Tsv6hY":{"wy":"HN recommends it for anyone interested in legal systems, governance, or history.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend for anyone interested in legal systems or history in general","i":23979628,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23979628,"author":"andrewxdiamond","month":"2020-07","story":"Iroquois Nationals, barred from World Games, gave gift of lacrosse to the world","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Haudenosaunee legal system and its origins","quote":"Highly recommend for anyone interested in legal systems or history in general","text":"The Haudenosaunee (incorrectly referred to as the Iroquois) have a fascinating and complex legal system. This video explained the system and how it came to be. Highly recommend for anyone interested in legal systems or history in general [VIDEO]"},{"id":17528769,"author":"bane","month":"2018-07","story":"Legal Systems Very Different from Ours","stance":"rec","why":"Intricate governing system of the Iroquois Confederacy","quote":"there's a great video on the governing system of the Iroquois Confederacy, which is surprisingly intricate.","text":"Interesting, there's a great video on the governing system of the Iroquois Confederacy, which is surprisingly intricate. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43272980,"author":"JumpCrisscross","month":"2025-03","story":"Macron to open debate on extending French nuclear protection to European allies","stance":"reference","why":"Iroquois confederacy as governance model","quote":"","text":"> in the delusion of a united Europe, in which Germany is willing to subordinate itself to France The Haudenosaunee a/k/a Iroquois had a unique confederacy consisting of two dominant and a few lesser powers [1][2]. And the Roman Republic had two consuls, including on campaign. Maybe a nuclear command inspired by both is the answer: any launch requires codes from each of Paris, Berlin and the head of state of the country actually launching. (Practically, the Germans are sort of irrelevant given they not only have no nuclear weapons, they’re also dismantling their civil nuclear expertise. I’m assuming they grow up and reärm. Otherwise, centralising nuclear power in Paris doesn’t depend on Germany agreement.) [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["q0RX59G2jaA",1],["ouAG4vXFORc",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["iGbC5j4pG9w",1],["ZXJWO2FQ16c",1],["UiS27feX8o0",1]]},"dw_mRaIHb-M":{"wy":"Pointed to as a comedic but clear explanation of power dynamics in discussions of race and diversity in tech.","q":{"x":"the very best explanation is long-form stand-up comedy. If you don't understand, you really should watch this.","i":9380627,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9380627,"author":"spacemanmatt","month":"2015-04","story":"100 Days as a Black Man in Silicon Valley","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long-form stand-up explaining power imbalance in racist statements.","quote":"the very best explanation is long-form stand-up comedy. If you don't understand, you really should watch this.","text":"There are a lot of ways to explain that power imbalances are critical context to racist statements. Yes, when black people say racist things in America, it's racist, but when white people do it, it's racism. But the very best explanation is long-form stand-up comedy. If you don't understand, you really should watch this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9380194,"author":"smtddr","month":"2015-04","story":"100 Days as a Black Man in Silicon Valley","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates assuming the current state is neutral when judging help for groups.","quote":"Basically, it talks about the underlying assumption that the current state of things is neutral for everyone","text":"First, I downvoted your comment for the excessive negativity/abuse in it. You could have made your point just as clear without them. Second, I'm going to take your comment to point out something I've noticed when talking about sexism/racism. I'm going to call this theory of mine \"Calibration\". When I hear people say stuff like \"What about letting [underrepresented group] choose what they want to do with their lives?\" , I've come to believe that most are not trolls and they're probably not actually actively racist[1]. It's a difference in calibration. Perhaps you believe by default these kids are at a neutral(zero) level and have the full power of choice right now. But, what I see is that society actually puts them at like a negative 5. You see these efforts as forcing them to go into tech, a +5 if you will. I see it as just bring them up to 0 from the -5 society put them at so they have the same sense of free choice you & I do. This vid I think is also based on the calibration idea when it comes to \"Reverse racism\" [VIDEO] . Basically, it talks about the underlying assumption that the current state of things is neutral for everyone so any effort to improve standings for a group is unfair to the groups not getting help. It's like how every year on Google+, when the LifeAtGoogle account posts something like Black Appreciation day during Black history month, someone always asks \"When is White appreciation day?\" . Or when people talk about BlackGirlsCode.com, someone says \"I bet "},{"id":6983124,"author":"jdunck","month":"2013-12","story":"Girls Who Code","stance":"rec","why":"Comedic take on why criticism of a dominant group isn't racism.","quote":"If you would like to know what racism is and why criticism of a dominant group on the basis of race isn't in","text":"That people are reading this subthread and finding common cause with this right here... Do you realize how ridiculous you are? If you would like to know what racism is and why criticism of a dominant group on the basis of race isn't in, here's one take: [VIDEO] But you'd have to listen to get it, and you'd definitely have to give up your bullshit self-serving circular \"logic\"."},{"id":11483351,"author":"mgbmtl","month":"2016-04","story":"Stephen Fry hits out at ‘infantile’ culture of trigger words and safe spaces","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It is far from being a level playing field. The message varies greatly depending on the sender, receiver and context, right? I think it's quite OK to call out your fellow stereotype when they're being dicks. And while we're at it, since this thread is going down into \"this is reverse racism\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["E4tYpXVTjxA",1],["ShEez0JkOFw",1]]},"-DVyjdw4t9I":{"wy":"HN points to it for Guido's own views on the GIL, Python internals and using Copilot, especially in Python-related threads.","q":{"x":"I recommend this interview to get a slightly better glimpse of the guy","i":33998759,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36904586,"author":"fernandotakai","month":"2023-07","story":"Commander Keen's Adaptive Tile Scrolling","stance":"rec","why":"Lex Fridman interview with Guido van Rossum.","quote":"i would also recommend another two of his interviews","text":"link for people that want to watch it: [LINK] i would also recommend another two of his interviews: Todd Howard [LINK] Guido van Rossun [VIDEO]"},{"id":33998759,"author":"pen2l","month":"2022-12","story":"Python 3.11 Delivers","stance":"rec","why":"Interview giving a better sense of Guido's views.","quote":"I recommend this interview to get a slightly better glimpse of the guy","text":"This is baseless conjecture and completely without merit. Guido was intimately involved with this speed-up work. I've disagreed with Guido over things but the things you're saying are just weird. I recommend this interview to get a slightly better glimpse of the guy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43095103,"author":"vonneumannstan","month":"2025-02","story":"The Generative AI Con","stance":"reference","why":"Guido describes using Copilot daily as code review.","quote":"My biggest adjustment with using Copilot was that instead of writing code, my posture shifted to reviewing code.","text":"Yeah unironically. Guido van Rossum - \"I use it every day. My biggest adjustment with using Copilot was that instead of writing code, my posture shifted to reviewing code.\" [VIDEO] Here's Jeff Dean saying 25% of the characters in new PRs at Google are AI Generated. [LINK] Andrej Karpathy - \"I basically can't imagine going back to \"unassisted\" coding at this point\" [LINK] Andrew Ng - \"I run multiple models on my laptop — Mistral, Llama, Zefa. And I use ChatGPT quite often. \" [LINK] Simon Willison [LINK] I mean I can keep going. I doubt you would compare yourself to these people."},{"id":38599464,"author":"mobiuscog","month":"2023-12","story":"How Many Lines of C It Takes to Execute a and B in Python?","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamped discussion of Python internals with Guido.","quote":"There was some high-level discussion of this in the chat Guido van Rossum had with Lex Fridman (timestamped)","text":"There was some high-level discussion of this in the chat Guido van Rossum had with Lex Fridman (timestamped): [VIDEO]"},{"id":36913871,"author":"frfl","month":"2023-07","story":"The Python Steering Council intent to approve PEP 703: making the GIL optional","stance":"reference","why":"Guido discusses the GIL's status.","quote":"Guido van Rossum did mention the status of the GIL in one of the Lex Friedman episodes","text":"If I remember Guido van Rossum did mention the status of the GIL in one of the Lex Friedman episodes [1] he was on (it's been a while, so I may be misremembering). Surprised to see a big decision like this happen so quickly. Did Meta's announcement play a big role in this [2]? [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["4XpnKHJAok8",1],["I845O57ZSy4",1],["L6A1Lt0kvMA",1]]},"-hxX_Q5CnaA":{"wy":"HN commenters like his candid, authentic delivery and the lessons on customer obsession and thinking long term.","q":{"x":"I always remember Jeff Bezos' 3 lessons: obsess over customers, invent, think long term.","i":3233169,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":3233169,"author":"gabaix","month":"2011-11","story":"Jeff Bezos Owns the Web in More Ways Than You Think","stance":"rec","why":"Bezos explains customer obsession, invention and long-term thinking.","quote":"I always remember Jeff Bezos' 3 lessons: obsess over customers, invent, think long term.","text":"I always remember Jeff Bezos' 3 lessons: obsess over customers, invent, think long term. [VIDEO] I love the way he unfolds his ideas simply and disrupts markets, one at a time."},{"id":3049571,"author":"jonknee","month":"2011-09","story":"Amazon Unveils $199 Kindle Fire Tablet","stance":"rec","why":"Authentic, candid Bezos presentation sharing mistakes and lessons.","quote":"He's awkward at moments, but authentic throughout. I like his style.","text":"> Bezos looked awkward and nervous Sounds about like every time I've heard him speak. He probably let out some really loud laughs too. Remember his welcome video to Zappos employees? It was a paper based presentation (giant sheets!) where among other things he explained mistakes he and Amazon made and then shared \"everything I know\". He's awkward at moments, but authentic throughout. I like his style. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18573565,"author":"jfoster","month":"2018-11","story":"Al Lowe reveals his Sierra source code collection","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as related to early Amazon bug story.","quote":"Sounds a bit like one of the early bugs on Amazon.com","text":"> There was a casino that let you bet negative amounts at the blackjack table, then lose on purpose to make lots of money! Sounds a bit like one of the early bugs on Amazon.com: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XuKjBIBBAL8",1],["uDr4L6BzpP8",1]]},"9tocssf3w80":{"wy":"Linked in political threads as a short, accessible companion to Snyder's book and as a reminder not to obey in advance.","q":{"x":"I also recommend the short Youtube series Snyder recorded before the release of the graphic edition","i":43504610,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43504610,"author":"neo4llm","month":"2025-03","story":"On Tyranny [book]","stance":"rec","why":"Short video series by Snyder accompanying the graphic edition of On Tyranny.","quote":"I also recommend the short Youtube series Snyder recorded before the release of the graphic edition","text":"Could not agree more! I also recommend the short Youtube series Snyder recorded before the release of the graphic edition: Lesson 1: [VIDEO] Full Playlist: [LINK]"},{"id":43504624,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2025-03","story":"On Tyranny [book]","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent Snyder talks on positive versus negative freedom.","quote":"I have watched him give excellent talks on youtube, which reference it.","text":"He has a new book On Freedom , too. I haven't read it yet, but I have watched him give excellent talks on youtube, which reference it. I believe his core thesis in On Freedom is that America has focused on negative freedom, when what really matters is positive freedom. \"Negative freedom\" is the idea that no government should interfere with your life. Authoritarian regimes arbitrarily exercise power, and that results in people being prevented from doing things or forced to do things. Maybe that's restrictions on speech or protest. Focusing on \"negative freedom\" turns people against their governments, which creates a sort of power vacuum anyone with resources is happy to fill. Negative freedom is good, nobody should want the government interfering with their lives, but focusing solely on negative freedom leads to authoritarianism. We can see this effect right now with \"free speech absolutists\" functionally becoming oligarchs. He says what people should focus on instead is \"positive freedom\" -- The idea that you have the freedom to do what you want. If you don't have health, money, or education, you can't really be free. There is no freedom in a hospital bed. So healthcare creates positive freedom. Building housing lets you spend more money on things you want to do instead of rent, that's positive freedom. Without education, you are more easily victimized by those who wish to manipulate you and you are less capable of producing things of value. So education is freeing. He also h"},{"id":35510530,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: Tech work that strengthens liberal democracy","stance":"rec","why":"Snyder narrates On Tyranny in video form.","quote":"he also narrated it in video form","text":"I would go spend a couple of days on Yale history professor Timothy Snyder reading his books and then watch him on YouTube. Harvard law professor Lawrence Lessig is also a major academic in this area. Their respective relevant books are \"The Road to Unfreedom\" and \"They Don't Represent Us.\" Timothy Snyder also wrote \"On Tyranny,\" which is a very quick read which is directly applicable to the question you are asking, he also narrated it in video form: [VIDEO] Timothy Snyder is more about understanding and fighting the forces of tyranny while Lawrence Lessig focuses more on the mechanics of our democracy and the consequences of our current implementation. I promise you will love Timothy Snyder. My personal take is \"democracy\" the word has been hijacked and it's probably time to stop using it casually. The core idea and foundation of liberal democracy and what you feel is being eroded and needs strengthening is rule of law . Russia tried to privatize and democratize but failed because they did not have rule of law. Rule of law is where all the good stuff comes from. So what you really want an answer for is \"how do I use my technical skills to promote rule of law?\" Supporting rule of law means supporting a fact based world, supporting institutions that do investigative research, and promoting empathy for the oppressed. Supporting rule of law means empowering the un-empowered by, for example, supporting unions. Snyder would say: So choose an institution you care about – a court, a"},{"id":42784025,"author":"_DeadFred_","month":"2025-01","story":"Ask HN: Is this too political for Hacker News? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"mistaking my not obeying in advance","text":"My bad. Doesn't look like I can delete my submission. I apologize for coming off gotcha, gloaty, or sneering I thought I was being pretty blunt. I was genuinely surprised yesterday and realized I have a huge misunderstanding about how things operate here. On the Elon thread dang stated that users flag articles and implied that user flagging was the overriding arbiter of authority on HN and linked [LINK] (implying flagging/community consensus is above the mods) which is how I came to my 'not very accurate description of how HN works'. Yesterdays response further confused me as the community discussion in the flagged article seemed to lean more towards unflagging than support for the flag remaining. Thanks for clarifying that I still misunderstand. I will ask others still in the bay area for more insight directly and refrain from talking/asking about HN on HN. Honest question what is pre-sneering? Maybe you are mistaking my shock at events yesterday and the position taken here for sneering? I have to admit I am extremely shocked and disillusioned. But I'm definitely not sneering. I think you are mistaking my not obeying in advance ( [VIDEO] ) and asking a very blunt question for some sort of juvenile internet snark. I think responding negatively and how I assumed most people would to outright Nazi behavior and defense/deflection/headburrying of it is different than run of the mill internet snark/sneering. I guess keeping with that post about being post-authenticity yesterday ma"},{"id":42334415,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-12","story":"LA Times Owner Personally Reviews Opinion Headlines to Avoid Offending Elon Musk","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Do not obey in advance","text":"Gutless. That's how it happens in authoritarian regimes. People start obeying in advance. Do not obey in advance: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["PJy8vTu66tE",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["9WcHsT97suU",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["Eghl19elKk8",1],["d0nERTFo-Sk",1],["GTQnarzmTOc",1]]},"OB-bdWKwXsU":{"wy":"Cited as a good talk on the thinking behind C++11 and on using the type system to prevent bugs; some other mentions quote it critically.","q":{"x":"Very good talk about the ideas behind C++ 11","i":5513491,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5513491,"author":"munk801","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Ideas behind C++11","quote":"Very good talk about the ideas behind C++ 11","text":"GoingNative 2012 - Bjarne Stroustrup talks about C++ 11. Very good talk about the ideas behind C++ 11 [VIDEO]"},{"id":4048958,"author":"agentultra","month":"2012-05","story":"A Dangerous Obsession with Primitives","stance":"rec","why":"Stroustrup on unit-conversion bugs and using types to prevent them","quote":"There's a salient point in that talk where he describes a mission that failed due to a human error.","text":"This sounds like the kind of problem that puts really expensive robots into craters. [VIDEO] There's a salient point in that talk where he describes a mission that failed due to a human error. Someone forgot to convert an internal representation of a unit in some algorithm which led to a miscalculation and a loss of some millions of dollars. Apparently the error made it through all of the code reviews and tests that were written. Bjarne then shows some pretty simple code that might have avoided such a disaster by specifying your types up front and letting the compiler do the work of converting them. Seems like a pretty simple concept and I wonder why it's so often missed."},{"id":45374444,"author":"quuxplusone","month":"2025-09","story":"My new Git utility `what-changed-twice` needs a new name","stance":"reference","why":"Stroustrup's talk simplifying code with STL find_if and rotate","quote":"the code that is presented is the 5-line replacement using the STL's `find_if` and `rotate`.","text":"The talk: [VIDEO] Sadly the 25-line original code isn't presented; the code that is presented is the 5-line replacement using the STL's `find_if` and `rotate`. Bjarne sketches the idea that those five lines can be further condensed into two lines with the non-STL `gather` algorithm: auto dest = std::find_if(v.begin(), v.end(), contains(p)); stdx::gather(v.begin(), dest, v.end(), [](const auto& elt) { return &elt == &*source; }); But this is overkill — replacing an O(distance(source,dest)) non-allocating rotate with an O(v.size()) potentially-allocating stable_partition — and more importantly it re-complicates the code. Now, I think part of his point is that `stable_partition` is \"simpler\" than `gather` only because it's in the STL. If we add `gather` to the STL too and everyone learns what it means, then there's no objection to using `gather` for \"simplification\" like this: it would be a straightforward simplification in almost the same way that `std::equal_range(first, last, x)` is a straightforward simplification of `std::make_pair(std::lower_bound(first, last, x), std::upper_bound(first, last, x))`. The \"almost\" is that actually there is an algorithmic advantage to `std::equal_range`: when you're looking for the upper bound, you don't have to consider any of the elements to the left of the lower bound you already found. You get a (very slight) performance boost by using the combined `equal_range` algorithm. `gather`, on the other hand, has no such advantage; and (as we've "},{"id":19998276,"author":"harry8","month":"2019-05","story":"Near-duplicate features of C++ (2017)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"So here's Bjarne being \"Uber-Macho\" to the delight of the conference crowd.","text":"What I mean is anything that is showing off, has a lack of humility and implicitly disparages others in a way that is totally unneeded while producing lower-quality, less-maintainable, less robust code. Being clever for the sake of appearing clever. That kind of thing. if(condition) I_am_tough_so_I_do_not_brace(); The un-braced block is a classic \"macho\" pose in code. leaving out a '{' and a '}', two characters, has no benefit in any way, shape or form. It is decrying \"I will never make a mistake with this loose razor blade if you do maintaining it is because you suck\" There is literally no reason to do it ever, under any circumstances. Yet I have, and we do. We can change. Now watch the arguments presented when you bring un-braced blocks up. They're insanely ridiculous. (vertical space, need static analysis, etc.) If you were betting your retirement savings on no bug in the line of code now or in the future, you'd be very nervous about it sure. And then you would brace it. Unbraced blocks are a minor thing that mostly don't matter. I wouldn't fight too hard over it. It's just that it's so very, very clear that one way is bugs for zero benefit while the other isn't. Meh. So here's Bjarne being \"Uber-Macho\" to the delight of the conference crowd. Note the quote: \"I think I understand what you're saying and I think you happen to be wrong.\" (crowd whoops in glee). [VIDEO] This is a pretty ugly way of saying \"I disagree and this is why...\" But the crowd loves it. That attitude ge"},{"id":15372135,"author":"harry8","month":"2017-09","story":"Thoughts on generative C++ [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I understand what you are trying to say and I think that you are wrong.\" --Bjarne Stroustrup","text":"\"I understand what you are trying to say and I think that you are wrong.\" --Bjarne Stroustrup, to a chorus of razzes, jeers and laughter at a question in going native a few years back. [VIDEO] about 1:09:30 But hey don't take my word for it, my reputation is nothing and I'm as anonymous as a wrong number whether I like it that way or not. How about Ken Thompson? His doesn't seem to be similar, if you like an argument from authority - and I think such argument has a place if not used exclusively. \"I would try out the [C++] language [at AT&T] as it was being developed and make comments on it. It was part of the work atmosphere there. And you'd write something and then the next day it wouldn't work because the language changed. It was very unstable for a very long period of time. At some point, I said, no, no more. In an interview I said exactly that, that I didn't use it because it wouldn't stay still for two days in a row. When Stroustrup read the interview he came screaming into my room about how I was undermining him and what I said mattered and I said it was a bad language.\" Ken Thompson; cited in Seibel, Peter (2009). Coders At Work. p. 475. Alexandrescou openly criticises Alasdair Meredith at cpp con in a way that isn't really friendly or respectful. He also finishes all his talks with \"Destroy!\" as an open acknowledgement of the defensiveness he feels about the naked hostility that exists in the C++ world. I like his stuff and the bulk of his attitude, I hate the context"}],"r":[["Ee3EtYb8d1o",1],["Wp-WiNXH6hI",1],["-4Yp3j_jk8Q",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["QyJZzq0v7Z4",1],["0Cu6EbELZ6I",1],["HnOix9TFy1A",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1]]},"Wo2m3jaJixU":{"wy":"Recommended whenever local-first software, sync engines and CRDT alternatives come up, as a practical production account.","q":{"x":"This talk by Karri about Linear's \"sync engine\" is also a good watch","i":38292108,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38292108,"author":"saqadri","month":"2023-11","story":"You don't need a CRDT to build a collaborative experience","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about Linear's sync engine by Karri","quote":"This talk by Karri about Linear's \"sync engine\" is also a good watch","text":"You may not need CRDT per-se, but building a collaborative experience is so difficult. I worked on collaborative systems for a bit, and also have read a bit about how Figma and Notion do it (this is a good read: [LINK] ) -- it's still super hard to get right. This talk by Karri about Linear's \"sync engine\" is also a good watch: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":37749057,"author":"YousefED","month":"2023-10","story":"Local-first software: You own your data, in spite of the cloud (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Linear's sync engine using local-first techniques","quote":"Another interesting video I recommend on this topic is about Linear's \"Sync Engine","text":"This article has been quite the inspiration for many projects and progress on this front. I think we're seeing more and more developments around CRDTs and local-first frameworks / applications. I'm working on a few projects in this area: - [LINK] - Notion meets Notebook-style live programming for TypeScript / React - [LINK] - a rich text editor built on TipTap / Prosemirror that supports Yjs for local-first collaboration - [LINK] - a wrapper around Yjs for easier development In my experience so far, some things get more complicated when building a local-first application, and some things get a lot easier. What gets easier is that once you've modeled and implemented the data-layer (which does require you to rethink / unlearn a few principles), you don't need to worry about data-fetching, errors etc. as much as in a regular \"API-based\" app. Another interesting video I recommend on this topic is about Linear's \"Sync Engine\" which employs some of the local-first techniques as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43434387,"author":"probabletrain","month":"2025-03","story":"Sync Engines Are the Future","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Linear syncs all data for large orgs","quote":"there's a talk on that here","text":"> Previous sync engines really want you to sync all data Linear had to do all sorts of shenanigans to be able to sync all data, for orgs with lots of it – there's a talk on that here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40787858,"author":"dgroshev","month":"2024-06","story":"Local First, Forever","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"(this should probably be a post) CRDTs and local first are ideas that is perpetually in the hype cycle for the last decade or so, starting around the time Riak CRDTs became a thing and continuing all the way to today. Niki's post is a perfect illustration: CRDTs offer this \"magical\" experience that seems perfectly good until you try building a product with them. Then it becomes a nightmare of tradeoffs: - state-based or operation-based? do you trim state? how? - is it truly a CRDT (no conflicts possible), a structure with explicit conflict detection, or last-writer-wins/arbitrary choice in a trench coat that will always lose data? Case in point: Automerge uses pseudo-random conflict resolution, so Niki's solution will drop data if it's added without a direct causal link between the edits. To learn this, you have to go to \"under the hood\" section in Automerge docs and read about merge rules very attentively. It might be acceptable for a particular use case, but very few people would even read that far! - what is the worst case complexity? Case in point: yjs offers an interface that looks very much like JSON, but \"arrays\" are actually linked lists underneath, which makes it easy to accidentally become quadratic. - how do you surface conflicts and/or lost data to the user in the interface? What are the user expectations? - how do you add/remove replication nodes? What if they're offline at the time of removal? What if they come online after getting removed? - what's user experie"},{"id":36521435,"author":"notamy","month":"2023-06","story":"Scaling Linear's Sync Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Notes the post is just an embedded video without text version","quote":"there doesn't seem to be an accompanying text-only version.","text":"Heads-up: This is a video ( [VIDEO] ) embedded in a blog post; there doesn't seem to be an accompanying text-only version."}]},"uhbDfi7Ee7k":{"wy":"HN brings it up in threads on cooling, window heat gain and house design as a convincing case for awnings.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections just did a video on awnings.","i":40986769,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42162712,"author":"hunter2_","month":"2024-11","story":"Show HN: I built a(nother) house optimized for LAN parties","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant to solar heat gain through windows","quote":"Check out","text":"It's almost certainly insolation (sol, not sul). UV/IR rejecting film on the windows might help, given mild enough winters that blocking it year round is fine. Check out [VIDEO] If you can find someone willing to do it, dumping the heat (pumped out as air conditioning) directly into the pool would be quite efficient relative to heating the pool separately. Have it dump to the ambient outdoor air only as overflow when the pool's thermostat is satisfied (upper 80s or whatever)."},{"id":40986769,"author":"elric","month":"2024-07","story":"New technology could reduce the amount of energy for air conditioning","stance":"rec","why":"Shows awnings are effective at cooling homes","quote":"Technology Connections just did a video on awnings.","text":"> Passive cooling measures (e.g., window shutters) should not be overlooked as they cost nothing to run. Technology Connections just did a video on awnings. I'm not quite as puppy eyed about them as TC, but they are pretty darn effective. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43927298,"author":"MattSayar","month":"2025-05","story":"Heat stress mitigation by trees and shelters at bus stops","stance":"reference","why":"Awnings reduce indoor temperatures","quote":"Reminds me of a Technology Connections video describing how helpful awnings are in reducing temperatures inside your house","text":"Reminds me of a Technology Connections video describing how helpful awnings are in reducing temperatures inside your house [0]. Are there any modern-looking awnings that wouldn't look out of place in my neighborhood? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41854344,"author":"AlexErrant","month":"2024-10","story":"Why don't we use awnings anymore (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Video with the same message as the blog","quote":"(And it had more or less the same message as this blog.)","text":"The \"Technology Connections\" youtube channel recently discussed awnings too. (And it had more or less the same message as this blog.) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NOAgplgTxfc",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1]]},"vTbILK0fxDY":{"wy":"Offered as a starter for people wanting to understand China's demographic crisis and how it threatens economic growth.","q":{"x":"A starter on this is the three part polymatter series.","i":28813078,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28813078,"author":"anm89","month":"2021-10","story":"China's Xi vows 'reunification' with Taiwan","stance":"rec","why":"Starter on China's demographic problem and falsified census data.","quote":"A starter on this is the three part polymatter series.","text":"I've recently been really digging in on China, and the more I read / learn, the less threatening (not evil, the CCP is certainly plenty evil) I perceive them. A starter on this is the three part polymatter series. 1) China has a monster of a demographics problem that they are clearly lying about with census data: [VIDEO] [LINK] 2) It's pretty well established at this point that Chinese property markets have serious issues which threaten systemic issues: [LINK] [LINK] 3) Their resource situation, especially water issues are serious: [LINK] 4) They have terrible Geography from a military perspective and are not nearly as militarily strong as they project [LINK] 5) And the biggest one. Chinese citizens are increasingly not prosperous enough to be ok with it. Also the gender imbalance is so severe that there are guaranteed to be 10s of millions of angry single men over the next decades, almost certain to increase social discord. [LINK] [LINK] They have also seriously mishandled their relationship with Australia who they really needed which was just long term cemented with the AUUKUS alliance. Another interesting channel which I found because she was talking about evergrande default over three years ago: [LINK] Video on frequent structural disasters [LINK] I think over the next 30 years, the US tripping over it's own feet is a bigger risk than anything China related"},{"id":26739818,"author":"TheAdamAndChe","month":"2021-04","story":"How Western Civilisation Could Collapse","stance":"rec","why":"Explains China's demographic crisis from the one-child policy and economic consequences.","quote":"discussing a demographic crisis in China caused from their one-child policy","text":"Of relevance is this video[1] discussing a demographic crisis in China caused from their one-child policy that will lead to significant economic issues in the coming decades, much like how Japan is having issues. There's a good chance this will limit their growth. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27755739,"author":"novok","month":"2021-07","story":"A theory that “elite overproduction” leads to political instability (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows China's demographic challenges are slowing growth.","quote":"China has a lot of challenges coming up in the future that is slowing that growth now","text":"China has a lot of challenges coming up in the future that is slowing that growth now: [VIDEO] It's making the CCP jittery, which is a theory behind the increasing authoritarianism in the country now."}],"r":[["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["3qHkcs3kG44",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["GZTr1-Gp74U",1]]},"xnqTKD8uD64":{"wy":"HN people list it among the go-to talks for learning or refreshing modern C++ style, and cite it for Sutter's guidance on function parameter passing.","q":{"x":"","i":16536336,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16536336,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: Best way to learn modern C++?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among Sutter talks for learning modern C++.","quote":"Back to the Basics! Essentials of Modern C++ Style","text":"- Tour of C++ ( [LINK] ) - Principles and Practice Using C++ ( [LINK] ) - From Mathematics to Generic Programming ( [LINK] ) - The Scott Meyers books Some of the Bjarne Stroustrup videos, \"Learning and Teaching Modern C++\" - [LINK] Some of the Herb Sutter videos, \"Writing Good C++14... By Default\" - [LINK] \"Back to the Basics! Essentials of Modern C++ Style\" - [VIDEO] Some of the Kate Gregory videos, \"Stop Teaching C\" - [LINK] \"10 Core Guidelines You Need to Start Using Now\" - [LINK] \"It's Complicated\" - [LINK]"},{"id":8788573,"author":"jerv","month":"2014-12","story":"Ask HN: How to refresh on modern features of C++?","stance":"rec","why":"Herb Sutter's talk on modern C++.","quote":"Read it! And watch the youtube video in [1].","text":"Herb Sutter, one of the most prolific C++ experts out there, recently gave a talk on Modern C++ [1]. In the beginning of his talk, he states that every C++ developer should read a \"Tour of C++\" [2] by Bjarne Stroustrup, the original designer of C++. I recently read it and it's great. The book has less than 200p, but still covers the most important parts of C++11, albeit not in great detail (the reader is pointed to \"The C++ Programming Language\" by the same author for that). Read it! And watch the youtube video in [1]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27168489,"author":"oconnor663","month":"2021-05","story":"Things you can’t do in Rust (and what to do instead)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of shared_ptr aliasing pitfall in callback code.","quote":"you have to be careful never to dereference a shared_ptr that might be aliased","text":"ASan, UBSan, etc. are absolutely life-changing, no doubt about it. But there's a pretty big difference between catching things statically, and catching them at runtime. If you have an issue that, say, only triggers on odd-numbered Tuesdays on Windows 7, your tests suite is probably going to miss it, and ASan isn't going to be able to help you. Here's an example from one of Herb Sutter's talks, which I refer to frequently. He describes an issue that can come up with shared_ptr in reentrant/callbacky code, and the punchline is that you have to be careful never to dereference a shared_ptr that might be aliased: [VIDEO] I also just wrote a giant example of my own :) in reply to your toplevel comment. [LINK] . TSan will be pretty good at catching some of the simple variants of this, like where you totally forget to take a lock. But it's going to have a hard time with cases where you \"stash\" a reference longer than you should, because later uses of the reference might appear safe for a while yet become unsafe down the road with unrelated code or timing changes. Another issue with ASan and UBSan, is that every application has to run them (and maintain its own test suite comprehensive enough to make them useful). With static checks, a memory-safe library is memory-safe for everyone , even for applications that aren't particularly careful with their tests. The library author is able to express their safety requirements through the language, and all callers have to respect those requir"},{"id":21666050,"author":"legends2k","month":"2019-11","story":"Cheat Sheet: C++ Function Parameter Choices [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Sutter explains optimal function parameter passing cases.","quote":"where he explains the various cases","text":"In our 60+ team of C++ programmers, a constant theme that keeps coming up in code reviews is how a function optimally takes its parameters. There's been enough overthinking and I came across Herb Sutter's CppCon 2014 Essentials of Modern C++ Style [1] where he explains the various cases. I made a cheat sheet (1-page PDF) for quick reference of the options and when to go for them. Comments and suggestions welcome! GitHub project [2]. # Credits - Herb Sutter, other C++ experts and the community - Emacs[3] and Org-mode[4] creators - CheatSheet by Musa Al-hassy # Links [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["NH1Tta7purM",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["XkDEzfpdcSg",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["lgivCGdmFrw",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1]]},"AHs2Ugxo7-8":{"wy":"HN people link it as the original, technically solid presentation on thorium reactors whenever the topic comes up.","q":{"x":"Brilliant fairly technical presentation on Thorium.","i":7572019,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7572019,"author":"everyone","month":"2014-04","story":"Thorium reactors: Asgard’s fire","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Technical presentation that convinced the commenter on thorium.","quote":"Brilliant fairly technical presentation on Thorium.","text":"Brilliant fairly technical presentation on Thorium. [VIDEO] This more or less sold me on the idea years ago."},{"id":2724102,"author":"moreati","month":"2011-07","story":"Thorium nuclear reactors: a possible solution of the energy crisis? (video)","stance":"reference","why":"Original talk on thorium reactors.","quote":"Here are the original videos","text":"Here are the original videos: The Liquid Fluoride Thorium Reactor: What Fusion Wanted To Be [VIDEO] Aim High: Using Thorium Energy to Address Environmental Problems [LINK] Lessons for the Liquid-Fluoride Thorium Reactor (from history) [LINK]"},{"id":2272360,"author":"tambourine_man","month":"2011-02","story":"TerraPower and Bill Gates Have a Radical Vision For the Reactors of Tomorrow","stance":"reference","why":"Google tech talk on the liquid fluoride thorium reactor.","quote":"How does that compare to thorium based reactors?","text":"How does that compare to thorium based reactors? The Liquid Fluoride Thorium Reactor: What Fusion Wanted To Be [VIDEO]"}]},"D28uSzCs7-k":{"wy":"HN people point to it as a showcase of CERN's open hardware and as an example of quality metrology content.","q":{"x":"Marco Reps is a treasure.","i":45583324,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45583324,"author":"willis936","month":"2025-10","story":"How bad can a $2.97 ADC be?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Look at CERN's multimeter.","quote":"Marco Reps is a treasure.","text":"Well, if you're trying to measure DC extremely accurately the old stuff is still golden. Multi-slope is the gold standard and no one will sell you one. Delta-sigma get most of the way there. [LINK] And if you want to see CERN's multimeter, check this out. [VIDEO] Marco Reps is a treasure."},{"id":35276518,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2023-03","story":"New ATLAS result weighs in on the W boson","stance":"reference","why":"Build of CERN's open high-precision ADC design.","quote":"I recall watching Marco Reps' video[1] where he built the (relatively) high-speed high-precision ADC they designed","text":"I recall watching Marco Reps' video[1] where he built the (relatively) high-speed high-precision ADC they designed and published as an open hardware project[2], to be used to accurately monitor current in the magnets. A small but important piece of a giant machine puzzle. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":27217533,"author":"anfilt","month":"2021-05","story":"Over the past 10 years, the LHC has found more than 50 new hadrons","stance":"reference","why":"Voltmeter built from CERN open designs.","quote":"Like here is a voltmetere someone built using designs from CERN.","text":"Lots of the designs used by CERN are basically open source hardware some more useful than other depending on how niche it is. Heck CERN helps with KiCADs development which has benefited numerous hobbyists. Like here is a voltmetere someone built using designs from CERN. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["JurplDfPi3U",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["C25qzDhGLx8",1]]},"NdfmMB1E_qk":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to explanation of why landlords refuse to renegotiate leases and leave units empty.","q":{"x":"Louis Rossmann had an excellent breakdown of why the landlords won't budge on the leases","i":24551721,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24551721,"author":"jaaames","month":"2020-09","story":"Almost 90 percent of NYC bars and restaurants couldn’t pay August rent","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why landlords refuse lease renegotiation due to CMBS loan terms","quote":"Louis Rossmann had an excellent breakdown of why the landlords won't budge on the leases","text":"\"Meanwhile, 90 percent reported they have been trying to negotiate their leases, but their landlords wouldn’t budge.\" This is alarming. It may seem like just another statistic lost in a bigger article, but it's symptomatic of a ticking time bomb in NYC, and commercial real estate more broadly. Louis Rossmann had an excellent breakdown of why the landlords won't budge on the leases [VIDEO] tl;dr - the loans on these restaurants are all bundled into commercial mortgage backed securities (sound familiar?) and sold to wholesale investors, and the landlords are unable to change the terms of the lease, or the banks will revalue the underlying asset based on a multiple of the rent, and the owner will either essentially get margin called, or end up with negative equity, and default on the loan. Can someone from Wall Street please add their thoughts?"},{"id":24550924,"author":"xmodem","month":"2020-09","story":"Almost 90 percent of NYC bars and restaurants couldn’t pay August rent","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the NYC commercial rental market is dysfunctional","quote":"doesn't remotely resemble a properly functioning market","text":"The problem is that what is actually going on in NY - and many other markets - doesn't remotely resemble a properly functioning market: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43222279,"author":"afuchs","month":"2025-03","story":"The housing theory of everything (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Speculation owners overstate values, so lowering rents triggers loan obligations","quote":"Louis Rossmann has a video series of his rants about commercial real estate","text":"Louis Rossmann has a video series of his rants about commercial real estate and the refusal of landlords to rent spaces to him which have been vacant for multiple years. One of his videos speculates that real estate is being used to store money and the building owners don't want to rent out anything: [LINK] Another video speculates that the owners of some buildings have made claims to banks and investors that their buildings are worth more than these buildings are actually worth. In this situation, if the buildings' owners lowered rents they would be admitting that the building is not worth as much as they claim. This could trigger contractual obligations they have with their mortgages and investors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38117445,"author":"afuchs","month":"2023-11","story":"Big landlords used software to collude on rent prices, DC lawsuit says","stance":"reference","why":"Financing conditions may push landlords to leave units vacant","quote":"Louis Rossmann has made numerous videos about vacant properties","text":"An asset can be owned by multiple investors who have competing interests with some wanting to take a large amount of risk by allowing real estate to remain vacant if it can mean a larger reward later. Louis Rossmann has made numerous videos about vacant properties where asking prices for rent are significantly above what he considers to be market value. Notably, [VIDEO] and the Reddit post which he commented on: [LINK] The TL;DR is that there's weak evidence that the contractual conditions attached to financing from investors who seek high risk and high reward makes those landlords ignore local market conditions and incentivizes those landlords to leave commercial units vacant instead of renting them out at market value."},{"id":27944494,"author":"conjecTech","month":"2021-07","story":"Empty storefronts are killing neighbourhoods","stance":"reference","why":"How financing constrains landlords from changing rents","quote":"Louis Rossmann covers it in a video","text":"There may be other factors besides what they nominally \"want\". Depending on their source of financing, landlords changing the rent on these spaces can be both logistically difficult and may cause a huge negative cashflow shock because of the way it effects capital requirements on commercial loans. Louis Rossmann covers it in a video ( [VIDEO] ) based on this reddit comment ( [LINK] )."},{"id":24930431,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-10","story":"A Little Money Laundering Can Have a Big Impact on Real Estate Prices (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Hypothesis that loan clauses keep landlords from lowering rents","quote":"Louis Rossmann has a semi-recent YT video exploring one hypothesis","text":"> (Because they don't want to rent it out, they only want to pretend that intend to do so.) Louis Rossmann has a semi-recent YT video exploring one hypothesis: landlords have mortgages that contain clauses in then which specify that if rents have to be a certain amount, and if they drop too much then that means the property value must have dropped. * [VIDEO] And if the property value has dropped, perhaps bringing the mortgage closer to being underwater, then the bank may want the landowner to put more equity in it so that adds to the bank's cushion. And landowners don't want to put more of their own money into a leveraged investment, so they'd rather just deal with the carrying costs rather than trigger an 'equity event'. The hypothesis was originally put forward in a Reddit comment: * [LINK]"}],"r":[["BLGFriOKz6U",1]]},"X3NASGb5m8s":{"wy":"HN cites it as the standard explanation of how finance and sales take over a company, applied to Boeing and other declining firms.","q":{"x":"I keep this clip open in a browser tab so I can go back to it from time-to-time","i":38962683,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38962683,"author":"aslatter","month":"2024-01","story":"Did a 1997 merger ruin Boeing?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs on how product people lose out to sales and marketing","quote":"I keep this clip open in a browser tab so I can go back to it from time-to-time","text":"I keep this clip open in a browser tab so I can go back to it from time-to-time: [VIDEO] It's the same basic message from Jobs, but this time about Xerox."},{"id":41925242,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2024-10","story":"Boeing CEO says the company must fundamentally change","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs's Xerox quote applied to Boeing's finance-driven decline","quote":"The old Steve Jobs Xerox quote [1] applies.","text":"This is really a systemic problem and I'd honestly be shocked if it's even possible for Boeing to dig themselves out of the hole they've dug for themselves. The core problem is the market demands ever-increasing profits and at a certain point the only way to achieve that is to cut costs. Executive compensation won't ever be cut. The management consultants will never be fired because they provide plausible deniability for the executives should anything go wrong. The inevitable result here is that the financial people end up running the company, not the engineers. The old Steve Jobs Xerox quote [1] applies. No, Boeing wasn't technically a monopoly, but in a duopoly where it's supported by the captured US government, it really operates like one. So you have a series of short-term decisions to increase profits that hurt the long-term viability of the company. Layers of subcontracting that add friction but marginally increase profits and lose quality control and institutional knowledge. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39431384,"author":"szasamasa","month":"2024-02","story":"EU to hit Apple with first ever fine in €500M penalty over music streaming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"well in a sense the right word may be oligarch... US companies control all OSs but linux... they are surely working together with NSa and the US government which in turn let them become extremely powerful monopolies... what are oligarchs? people who play together with strong governments... Ithink the upper management of these US companies not just become oligarch-rich from soaring stock prices but the actual thing oligarch enjoy is power which is not 1 to 1 money in the economical sense stock companies have shareholders, simple employees and management and the goal is to maximize profit... totally legit and apple is seemingly doing this by crippling the web because using apps from taxed app store and using browsers to browse simple websites is what fits their thinking and current(!) sales model but you are right, why do people argue for apple I really dont understand... all you have to do if you want to stay simple and apple-safe to follow their suggestions, use safari, never download other browsers, do not use other app stores etc... a choice will not make their system worse and what I want to add, I am not sure Apple actually on the profit max way with these decisions... not because I never buy iphones ever again because most people who buy apple rather tend to beleive them and accept and follow which is an understandable strategy when there is so much information around but they missed making Safari THE browser where they now could distribute Apple products on all OS which"},{"id":39432984,"author":"szasamasa","month":"2024-02","story":"It’s Official, Apple Kills Web Apps in the EU","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I do not use these but I find windows after linux bloated so it may have something to do with microsoft engineers? (teams) spotify can choose its strategy, you can choose apple music native app (I guess there is something like this? I rarely listen to music but on youtube) Idont know about teams but my partner has ipad and iphone and I hear often how teams and microsoft products behave bad on iHardware... it may have to do with the love these two big corps show towards each other :) maybe you have to buy a windows laptop for teams too, that is the walled garden philosophy you actually seem to protect :) my partner now uses my laptop where I have a windows which I did not totally purged because I need this OS for testing :) hell there were chinese text the other day on the front page, you need to scan your finger twice (even if first was a success) sometimes, it cannot find the timezone from time to time :) I am not linux fanboy, the first ubuntu I purged 20 years ago or so I tried, but in the last 5-10 years linux is just great and getting glimpses how windows works by free family IT care, pff... that is the world we live in :) I added outlook as a web app for my partner and it has some apparent problems without testing it, but in the last 3 months I see it getting used and no problems I hear :) I guess the web designers are not web app programmers and it takes time until a new breed of programmers takes over the web... if I were spotify, I would keep the iOS app and you coul"}]},"XRrLyckg8Nc":{"wy":"Cited for Thiel's technological stagnation thesis, especially his argument that energy costs offset IT productivity gains.","q":{"x":"if you have an extra hour, its well worth it to listen to Thiel in this more recent debate","i":4001548,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4001548,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2012-05","story":"Thiel on 60 minutes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hour-long debate on broader technology and growth prospects.","quote":"if you have an extra hour, its well worth it to listen to Thiel in this more recent debate","text":"And if you have an extra hour, its well worth it to listen to Thiel in this more recent debate on the broader issue: Peter Thiel and George Gilder debate on \"The Prospects for Technology and Economic Growth\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":4430282,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2012-08","story":"Startup Stagnation","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel's energy-price argument for technological stagnation.","quote":"The basis of Thiel's argument comes down to the price of energy. He's bearish.","text":"> The truth is, we may well be in the largest slowdown of technological growth in human history. Economist Tyler Cowen calls this the “great stagnation.” relevant relink: Peter Thiel and George Gilder debate the prospects for technology and economic growth[1]. The basis of Thiel's argument comes down to the price of energy. He's bearish. He notes that Warren Buffet's biggest bet, which is on trains that transport 40% by volume coal, is a bet that clean, cheap energy tech will fail. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4213682,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2012-07","story":"U.S. pushes for more scientists, but the jobs aren’t there","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel attributes stagnation to energy cost.","quote":"Peter Thiel argues that its due to the cost of energy.[1]","text":"There's a global Great Recession, and the arguments that its due to a mismatch between available jobs and worker skills are just smoke to rationalize the growing inequality. Peter Thiel argues that its due to the cost of energy.[1] A recent article about the price to import coal to India supports his argument.[2] The Superbubble has popped, and every sector is cutting back for private equity to make inroads. 1. The Prospects for Technology and Econimic Growth [VIDEO] 2. Grinding Energy Shortage Takes Toll on India's Growth [LINK]"},{"id":4092140,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2012-06","story":"The Macroeconomics of Chinese kleptocracy","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel argues rising energy cost offsets IT productivity gains.","quote":"Productivity gains in IT just haven't been enough to offset the rising cost of energy (as argued by Peter Thiel)[5].","text":"Right, corruption is a global pandemic. Who can say how much the bids are rigged in awarding US military-industrial contracts (funded both by US taxpayers and global dollar-demand), to say nothing of state and local gov contracts (eg NYC paying $632m for a 911 phone system upgrade[1]). And wall street fleecing everyone else, as usual.[2] The article makes some very insightful points, but I'm not convinced that Chinese SOEs are more evil than any other centers of power (just one manifestion of growing inequality). One casualty overlooked here are Chinese SMEs: \"The real problem is the limited presence of other financial intermediaries and the rudimentary nature of bond and equity markets, both of which poorly serve the needs of small private companies and local governments.\"[3],[4] The author is right about deflation as the sandtrap up ahead, but that's just the consequence of a decelerating economy and we're still to see how soft/hard China lands. In doing a root cause analysis, one can chase down endless trails corruption. But if you want to find a problem which can be solved with a technological solution (as opposed to a social solution), it comes down to a distinct lack of progress in energy tech. Productivity gains in IT just haven't been enough to offset the rising cost of energy (as argued by Peter Thiel)[5]. Sure, the rising cost of energy is tempered a bit during deflationary cycles. But its a lose-lose, because so is economic growth (harmed both by too much inflation"},{"id":4159570,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2012-06","story":"Malthusianisms","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel quoted on the Simon-Ehrlich bet rolling outcome.","quote":"Ehrlich] has won by such a big margin in the last decade","text":"Note that although Simon won the bet, \"if you re-round the same bet on a rolling basis.. [Ehrlich] has won by such a big margin in the last decade that it has more or less undone the price gains of the previous century.\" (quoting Peter Thiel in a debate with George Gilder: Accelerating or Decelerating? The Prospects for Technology and Economic Growth [VIDEO] )"},{"id":3999233,"author":"Jach","month":"2012-05","story":"With Eye On Future, Billionaire Investor Bets On Paul","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel on whether technology growth is decelerating.","quote":"I was more interested in when Thiel talked about whether technology growth is decelerating.","text":"I was more interested in when Thiel talked about whether technology growth is decelerating. It was back in March, here's the video [VIDEO]"},{"id":3848778,"author":"beefman","month":"2012-04","story":"Using Non-Newtonian Fluids to Fill Potholes","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel's argument on technological stagnation.","quote":"I'm reminded of Peter Thiel's recent comment","text":"Good idea. But how about filling potholes with a Newtonian solid, like asphalt? It isn't terribly hard to do. I'm reminded of Peter Thiel's recent comment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4464626,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2012-09","story":"George Gilder: Capitalism is based on information and knowledge, not greed","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A real piece of work.","text":"A real piece of work. I hadn't seen his wiki article before I watched him debate Peter Thiel on \"Accelerating or Decelerating? Prospects for technology and economic growth\" [VIDEO]"}]},"rcwngbUrZNg":{"wy":"Recommended in Intel ME threads as essential viewing on x86 trust and auxiliary processors.","q":{"x":"Essential reading!!","i":11882813,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11882813,"author":"dlmetcalf","month":"2016-06","story":"Intel and ME, and why we should get rid of ME","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rutkowska on trustworthiness of x86 hardware","quote":"Essential reading!!","text":"Wow, no one here has mentioned Johanna Rutkovska's (Invisible Things Lab & QubesOS), \"Intel x86 Considered Harmful\"? [LINK] [LINK] Essential reading!! [VIDEO]"},{"id":11914342,"author":"kriro","month":"2016-06","story":"The Intel ME subsystem can take over your machine, can't be audited","stance":"rec","why":"32c3 talk on Intel ME worrying implications","quote":"I am pretty worried ever since I watched that","text":"Joanna Rutkowska has written a nice paper on the topic, highly recommended: [LINK] Edit: There's also a talk from 32c3 for those more inclined to watch a video. I am pretty worried ever since I watched that: [VIDEO] (which is why I have researched non-Intel laptop alternatives..cliffnotes: GPUs without BLOBs are hard to find and there will be some severe tradeoffs which is expected)"},{"id":21546297,"author":"emily-c","month":"2019-11","story":"Exploiting Intel’s Management Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of security aspects of auxiliary processors","quote":"Joanna Rutkowska has done a nice overview of some of the security aspects of these topics as well[4].","text":"The CSME is on the PCH[1] (when one exists). (However AMD elected to put their PSP on-die as an IP-block). It is the root of trust of the system and takes the CPU out of reset even though it's not on the CPU itself. x86-based machines have pretty much always had multiple auxiliary processors/SoCs that control various aspects of the system such as the embedded controller (a direct descendant of the 8042 keyboard controller). Another example of this is the AMD SMU which lives on the chipset and coexists with the PSP (the talk in the link gets code running on this as you described)[2]. Interestingly the Chromebook EC firmware is open source[3]. Joanna Rutkowska has done a nice overview of some of the security aspects of these topics as well[4]. Ideally there would be a standard way to verify and measure all the various firmware images (and potentially option ROMs which would be theoretically measured with SRTM via UEFI) for these system processors during boot during a DRTM event (with Intel's STM/AMD SMM supervisor enabled as well) but there is a long way to go for that. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13809414,"author":"moppl","month":"2017-03","story":"Who controls your computer? and how to make sure it's you","stance":"reference","why":"Intel ME removes user control of the platform","quote":"The system is now \"deep pwned","text":"The article is dated. The game changed fundamentally with the introduction of the Intel Management Engine (or the AMD Platform Security Processor) on the x86 platform. The system is now \"deep pwned\" as described in point 3.1.1 of the article. The manufacturer has ultimate control of the platform, the user has been disowned. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["5MgBikgcWnY",1],["pyX8kQ-JzHI",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1]]},"rtd7UzLJHrU":{"wy":"Cited both as a well-crafted monitor review and as evidence for the XDR's shortcomings versus reference displays, though some dispute its fairness.","q":{"x":"I wanted to share this review which I thought was incredibly well crafted and refreshing to watch.","i":22623007,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22623007,"author":"aikinai","month":"2020-03","story":"Pro Display XDR","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-crafted, refreshing monitor review versus a Sony reference monitor","quote":"I wanted to share this review which I thought was incredibly well crafted and refreshing to watch.","text":"I don’t know why this is posted now, but since it’s here I wanted to share this review which I thought was incredibly well crafted and refreshing to watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34078071,"author":"bubblethink","month":"2022-12","story":"Apple Is Struggling to Build Mac Pro Based on Its Own Silicon","stance":"reference","why":"Comparison against reference monitor as evidence","quote":"","text":">a device that is designed to compete with other devices 4x the price That itself is marketing spin. It's not like studios are throwing away their reference monitors for the apple display. You can design anything to compete; winning is the important bit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29470419,"author":"sunaurus","month":"2021-12","story":"Q&A with the developer of BetterDummy: from macOS secrets to his motivations","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes the video claim while disputing its fairness","quote":"","text":"> Totally crushes Apple with straight up numbers and pure unadulterated objectivity: [VIDEO] I don't know a lot about either of these monitors, but I don't find it surprising at all that a 30,000€ monitor would be better than a 6000€ monitor. Saying that the reviewer objectively crushes apple by using these two monitors in a comparison seems quite misleading."},{"id":29470265,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2021-12","story":"Q&A with the developer of BetterDummy: from macOS secrets to his motivations","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"if you want to see how bad Apple Pro Display XDR is, look no further than the legendary HDTV Test channel on YT.","text":"Unrelated but newer Macbook Pros (with M1 Max chips) as well as the Apple Pro Display XDR have entirely gotten rid of ICC profiles which seems crazy to me. Apple's definition of a professional is a more like a hipster. Real pros want no bullshit abstractions and hidden stuff, they want all knobs and switches exposed in all its glory. Btw, if you want to see how bad Apple Pro Display XDR is, look no further than the legendary HDTV Test channel on YT. Totally crushes Apple with straight up numbers and pure unadulterated objectivity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25317194,"author":"rbx","month":"2020-12","story":"Apple’s “Extended Dynamic Range” Brings HDR to Non-HDR Displays","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Comparison with the 40k display here. Apple doesn't come very close.","text":"[VIDEO] Comparison with the 40k display here. Apple doesn't come very close. Sure it's much cheaper. But then why compare."}]},"2qv3fcXW1mg":{"wy":"Referenced as a rare public description of how Google builds software, from which tools like Bazel descend.","q":{"x":"there is an interesting video \"Building Software at Google Scale\" [1], which seems to give an overview of the systems used.","i":6009402,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6009402,"author":"WestCoastJustin","month":"2013-07","story":"The Woman at the Heart of Everything Google Builds","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of Google's build systems","quote":"there is an interesting video \"Building Software at Google Scale\" [1], which seems to give an overview of the systems used.","text":"The Wired article talks about internal tools used to compile code. In line with that, there is an interesting video \"Building Software at Google Scale\" [1], which seems to give an overview of the systems used. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7910978,"author":"chas","month":"2014-06","story":"Programmers’ Build Errors: A Case Study at Google [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"High-fidelity description of Google's build system","quote":"Google has publically described their build system here","text":"Google has publically described their build system here [LINK] (low fidelity) and here [VIDEO] (high fidelity)"},{"id":5005340,"author":"pnathan","month":"2013-01","story":"Behind enemy lines: 3 months as an iOS developer at Google","stance":"reference","why":"Google talk on internal workflows and processes","quote":"I have, however, listened to some of their talks on the internal workflows and processes","text":"Honestly, I've heard the majority of it before, and I have never been a Google employee. I have, however, listened to some of their talks on the internal workflows and processes that they've presented and posted on youtube. I think the only things I didn't hear before were some of the specific toolnames. edit: [LINK] here's their engineering tools blog. [VIDEO] and a talk [LINK] and another talk Really cool stuff."}],"r":[["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["G4hL5Om4IJ4",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["coGpmA4saEk",1],["0qKp-0h9P18",1],["F9cO3-MLHOM",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["PDylgzybWAw",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1]]},"4kpDg7MjHps":{"wy":"HN cites it for Feynman's insights on NASA risk reasoning, blame and engineering culture, and the contrast between Apollo and the Shuttle.","q":{"x":"Richard Feynman served on the Challenger commission and very nicely summarizes the difference between Apollo and Space Shuttle.","i":45008772,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45008772,"author":"prasadjoglekar","month":"2025-08","story":"Starship's Tenth Flight Test","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman summarizes difference between Apollo and Space Shuttle.","quote":"Richard Feynman served on the Challenger commission and very nicely summarizes the difference between Apollo and Space Shuttle.","text":"It was reusable, but way over promised. Don't take my word for it. Richard Feynman served on the Challenger commission and very nicely summarizes the difference between Apollo and Space Shuttle. [VIDEO] SpaceX is doing stuff that's just beyond the scope of what's deemed conventionally realistic. That's achievable and pushes us forward."},{"id":14818555,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-07","story":"A hacker stole $31M of Ether – how it happened, and what it means for Ethereum","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on blame and educating the child.","quote":"Richard Feynman was right during the Challenger investigation","text":"> the model of 'public by default, opt in for security' is fundamentally daft in this context. It's daft in most contexts, and is one of the largest sources of security problems. If your design requires enumerating badness [1], you're doing it wrong. > from: [LINK] >> Just about every ICO, trust and company used the Parity multisig wallet, and that code was considered well-tested. -sigh- This is, unfortunately, a common problem. \"It worked ok the last N times\" and \"It passed a lot of tests\" do not mean it's bug-free and safe to use. Richard Feynman was right during the Challenger investigation when he called this a childish attitude . (He was also right in not wanting to assign blame, instead asking \"How do we educate the child?\"[2]) [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11071384,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-02","story":"Unsafe Lead Levels in Tap Water Not Limited to Flint","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on NASA's it-worked-last-time risk reasoning.","quote":"Feynman had some interesting comments on this problem","text":"> the absence of known danger is not the same as safety. Feynman had some interesting comments on this problem regarding NASA's \"it worked ok last time\" approach to risk assessment before the Challenger Disaster. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10989492,"author":"stepanhruda","month":"2016-01","story":"The result would be a catastrophe (1985)","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on Challenger management issues.","quote":"Here is Richard Feynman talking about the issues","text":"Here is Richard Feynman talking about the issues [VIDEO] Seems not very different from how companies are managed 30 years later."}]},"5t5QK03KXPc":{"wy":"Cited in tariff debates to show Reagan's own words against protectionism.","q":{"x":"would take the time to listen to his actual words regarding tariffs","i":47388740,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47388740,"author":"grim_io","month":"2026-03","story":"Small U.S. town, big company. Can it weather the tariff Blizzard? (Digi-Key)","stance":"rec","why":"Reagan's own words on free trade, contrasting with modern tariff advocates","quote":"would take the time to listen to his actual words regarding tariffs","text":"If only the people responsible for the tariffs, who miss no chance to (miss)quote Reagan, would take the time to listen to his actual words regarding tariffs [VIDEO]"},{"id":44957718,"author":"duxup","month":"2025-08","story":"SoftBank and Trump may not be enough to save Intel","stance":"reference","why":"Reagan's views on tariffs and protectionism.","quote":"When it comes to tariffs and protectionism","text":"I feel like the state propping up a company isn't really \"saving\" it as far as actually competing goes. When it comes to tariffs and protectionism: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43571445,"author":"belter","month":"2025-04","story":"US Administration announces 34% tariffs on China, 20% on EU","stance":"reference","why":"Reagan arguing for free trade over protectionism.","quote":"","text":"Yes of course. What is your solution? \"Warren Buffett Wants Higher Taxes For The Ultra-Rich, Including Himself — Says 'My Friends And I Have Been Coddled Long Enough\" - [LINK] \"Reagan's Radio Address on Free and Fair Trade on April 25, 1987\" - [VIDEO]"}]},"86x-u-tz0MA":{"wy":"HN people link it in threads about creativity, late bloomers and creative burnout as a humble, relatable take on where inspiration comes from.","q":{"x":"The idea of a \"muse\", and \"genius\" is something that visits you (not something you are).","i":28262902,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":4215498,"author":"wglb","month":"2012-07","story":"Late bloomer, not a loser. (I hope) ","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on writing and creativity relevant to late bloomers.","quote":"This is really quite germane","text":"This is really quite germane: [VIDEO] . While she talks about writing, I think it applies to the kind of thing you are talking about here."},{"id":4274866,"author":"bgilroy26","month":"2012-07","story":"The \"Just look at it\" hack for problem solving","stance":"rec","why":"Subjective take on who is responsible for creativity, humble and relatable.","quote":"she is humble and relatable in the video","text":"This TED talk by the author of Eat, Pray, Love addresses the very question at hand: \"Who/What is responsible for creativity?\". It approaches the question from a very subjective point of view. I haven't read her book, but she is humble and relatable in the video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28262902,"author":"hyperpallium2","month":"2021-08","story":"We don't 'do' learning. It does us","stance":"reference","why":"Genius as something that visits you, not something you are.","quote":"The idea of a \"muse\", and \"genius\" is something that visits you (not something you are).","text":"The idea of a \"muse\", and \"genius\" is something that visits you (not something you are). IIRC, this TED talk is about it [VIDEO]"},{"id":2722164,"author":"walexander","month":"2011-07","story":"Hemingway, Hounded by the Feds","stance":"reference","why":"Creative genius talk about the burden of creative output.","quote":"","text":"What are with some of these comments? This is not a story about a man who lost his 401k value. This is about seeing the end of the road and realizing the greatest things you've done you can never do again. Here's a man who is considered by some the greatest american writer (words of my freshman year english prof), who felt like he would \"never achieve what he was capable of\"(paraphrase). See: [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["G1m0aX9Lpts",1],["CbHjcwIoTiY",1],["wtIhVwPruwY",1]]},"bQECSInWVPY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in Vision Pro and AR threads as Jobs's concise take that a headset is like headphones for video.","q":{"x":"I think of the AVP like Steve Jobs once described: headphones for video","i":48276095,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":44272021,"author":"Geee","month":"2025-06","story":"Apple's Liquid Glass is prep work for AR interfaces, not just a design refresh","stance":"rec","why":"Jobs on headphones for video and his Vision Pro opinion.","quote":"Steve Jobs said it best","text":"VR / AR will definitely replace desktop / stationary computers, but they need to be as lightweight as headphones. Steve Jobs said it best (also his opinion of the current Vision Pro at the very end): [VIDEO]"},{"id":48276095,"author":"JeremyHerrman","month":"2026-05","story":"Ask HN: Is anyone working at least 4 hours daily on an Apple Vision Pro?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I think of the AVP like Steve Jobs once described: headphones for video","text":"I think of the AVP like Steve Jobs once described: headphones for video [0] I use it a few hours a day, a few times a week exclusively as a virtual display for my macbook. Being able to put a huge virtual monitor anywhere is the killer feature and I don't tend to use it for anything else. It's indispensable on flights where laptop lids can't even fully open. Apple got the interactions just about perfect for AR window management, etc. Of course the main drawbacks are the weight, size, and overall embarrassment from having a giant face computer strapped to your head. I don't find it very comfortable for more than a couple hours at time, at least with the solo knit band + the belking head strap. I'd like to get a dual knit band at some point. But when this sort of hardware gets miniaturized I think many people will prefer it to hunching over a laptop screen. Just like headphones, wearing glasses while typing at a black screen could signal \"Do not disturb, I'm working here\" [0] Steve Jobs on Virtual Reality (D3, 2005) [VIDEO]"},{"id":39053916,"author":"al_borland","month":"2024-01","story":"YouTube and Spotify won't launch Apple Vision Pro apps, joining Netflix","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Jobs is talking about headphones for video.","text":"[VIDEO] Seems like Walt Mossberg made the girl comment in this video where Jobs is talking about headphones for video. Mossberg also made fun of Dell’s large phone, saying it was like holding a waffle up to your head, and the market proved him wrong there. These devices, in their current form, aren’t something to wear while walking around town, like someone might do with headphones."},{"id":36254334,"author":"neillyons","month":"2023-06","story":"Here’s what Mark Zuckerberg thinks about Apple’s Vision Pro headset","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs compares VR to headphones for video.","quote":"Steve Jobs was making a comparison to headphones","text":"I don't think the experience needs to be shared. Steve Jobs was making a comparison to headphones [VIDEO] That is the way I think of it. A pair of headphones for your eyes."}],"r":[["E7MQb9Y4FAE",1],["-EVqrDlAqYo",1],["xLYYh4aPXAM",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1]]},"cZ7LzE3u7Bw":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in inequality debates as a compact summary of studies linking inequality to worse social outcomes.","q":{"x":"here's a talk that spends 17 minutes listing studies showing that this is in fact about inequality, not average income","i":9577719,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9577719,"author":"lars","month":"2015-05","story":"Policing, Mass Imprisonment, and the Failure of American Lawyers","stance":"rec","why":"Talk listing studies showing effects stem from inequality, not average income.","quote":"here's a talk that spends 17 minutes listing studies showing that this is in fact about inequality, not average income","text":"You seem to be talking about average wealth and social mobility. This is different from inequality, which is about the distance from the bottom to the top. Some of these results are counter intuitive. For example: There's data to show that the top quartile of earners in low inequality countries live longer than their top quartile counterparts in high inequality countries, even if the high inequality counterparts earn much more both in absolute and relative terms. Edit: Since this is getting downvoted, here's a talk that spends 17 minutes listing studies showing that this is in fact about inequality, not average income. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10840914,"author":"dools","month":"2016-01","story":"Economic Inequality: The Simplified Version","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among sources claiming inequality is bad.","quote":"Lots of very serious people say that inequality is bad","text":"Nobody serious said inequality was bad or good Lots of very serious people say that inequality is bad: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":8468491,"author":"asdkl234890","month":"2014-10","story":"Why Inequality Matters","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence that reducing inequality improved countries.","quote":"Things have gotten better in countries which have reduced inequality","text":"One thing that always bothers me about wealth distribution is that you can't simply move wealth around and expect things to get better. Things have gotten better in countries which have reduced inequality: [VIDEO] Don't confuse persons with populations. They are not the same thing. A person is very different from a population."},{"id":3985732,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2012-05","story":"Too Hot for TED: Income Inequality","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence inequality harms outcomes.","quote":"Yes, it is a bad thing in almost all recorded metrics.","text":"- Secondly, assuming that the \"inequality gap\" is necessarily a bad thing, which I don't think anyone has shown Actually they have. [LINK] [VIDEO] In face increasing Gini coefficient for 0.01 increases mortality in state for 122% after 12 years. Yes, it is a bad thing in almost all recorded metrics. - First of all, doesn't anyone see the correlation between increased government power (revenue/spending) Nope. I think revenue spending isn't really good metric of government power and so far no one has proved that. You are free to test your hypothesis though. I'd wager that there is quite a bit of income inequality if your gov. revenue is low but they spend a lot of money."}]},"fCd6B5HRaZ8":{"wy":"Commenters call it the best visualization of how the most common DNA sequencers work, though it predates newer chemistry.","q":{"x":"is the best visualization of how the most popular type of DNA sequencer works (that I've found)","i":15534731,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15534731,"author":"shpx","month":"2017-10","story":"No human genome has ever been completely sequenced","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes how Illumina sequencing-by-synthesis works.","quote":"is the best visualization of how the most popular type of DNA sequencer works (that I've found)","text":"[VIDEO] is the best visualization of how the most popular type of DNA sequencer works (that I've found). Imagine you have a string of length 3 billion made by randomly choosing from 4 characters. Like this dna = ''.join(random.choices('atgc', weights=[30.9, 29.4, 19.9, 19.8], k=3_234_830_000)) you get to randomly sample 1 billion[3, page 7] overlapping substrings of length 200[3, page 7] with .1% of the characters randomly changed[3, page 8]. Trying to find the original string from this is technically an undecidable problem. If there's a sequence 400 characters long that repeats multiple times, how could you know if it repeats 5 times or 50 times? (this would be unlikely to happen with random.choices() but DNA isn't random). This is called sequence alignment and it's one of the hard problems in bioinformatics[4]. [0] [LINK] random.choices() was added in Python 3.6 [1] [LINK] source for `weights` [2] [LINK] source for 3_234_830_000 (python ignores underscores in numbers) [3] [LINK] illumina is the most popular producer of genome sequencers [4] [LINK]"},{"id":20985853,"author":"lkirk","month":"2019-09","story":"DNA Data Storage: The Entirety of YouTube Could Fit on a Teaspoon","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Illumina sequencing process works.","quote":"this video may be able to explain a little better how this process works","text":"A sequencing run (at least in the context of Illumina's technology) requires a few very expensive consumable reagents: First being the flowcell (microscope slide that the dna sticks to while being read by a laser), the reagents (containing enzymes with fluorophores and other reagents for amplifying and manipulating DNA), and the actual power consumed by such machinery. This does not factor in prep/lab costs (which can be kept at a minimum with automation, but that also is a high startup cost endeavor). Each sequencing run can take ~1-3 days depending on the format. Edit: this video may be able to explain a little better how this process works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19943552,"author":"epistasis","month":"2019-05","story":"Genomics – A programmer’s guide","stance":"reference","why":"Describes older sequencing process; commenter notes it predates modern NGS.","quote":"That video describes the process used before NGS was around.","text":"That video describes the process used before NGS was around. These days, using anything with plasmids would be pretty unusual. There are several next generation sequencing technologies: 1) short read - Illumina - dominates most next-generation sequencing 2) long read - nanopore or pacbio. These have very different analysis methods, have measurement errors that are very different, and even have different file formats, etc. Short read is far more common, so you're probably in the \"Data Analysis\" of this: [VIDEO] But you need to know about the adapters and indices (how multiple samples can be sequenced at the same time). But as another commenter mentions, knowing some particulars about the project would really help know what sort of tutorial would be appropriate. You'll need to also know about the biology of the application, in addition to understanding the sequencing technology."}],"r":[["BwGsr3SzCZc",1]]},"jusWW2pPnA0":{"wy":"Cited as a talk full of rendering tricks, and as an example of what a small team can do with custom tech.","q":{"x":"There’s a recent tech talk about it which is full of wizardry","i":42591797,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42591797,"author":"MattRix","month":"2025-01","story":"VoxelSpace: Terrain rendering algorithm in less than 20 lines of code (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Tech talk on Tiny Glade full of rendering wizardry.","quote":"There’s a recent tech talk about it which is full of wizardry","text":"There is lots of cool innovation happening in game tech still. Look at games like Noita, Dreams and Teardown. Or for an even more recent game, look at Tiny Glade. There’s a recent tech talk about it which is full of wizardry: [VIDEO] There is also lots of cool tech happening at the more foundational levels in engines and frameworks, for example Epic’s Nanite/Lumen/MegaLights in UE5."},{"id":45547920,"author":"pixelpoet","month":"2025-10","story":"AMD and Sony's PS6 chipset aims to rethink the current graphics pipeline","stance":"reference","why":"Tiny Glade cited as example of small-team ray tracing.","quote":"","text":"As you can tell, I'm patient :) A very important quality for any ray tracing enthusiast lol The ability to do irregular sampling, efficient shadow computation (every flavour of shadow mapping is terrible!) and global illumination is already making its way into games, and path tracing has been the algorithm of choice in offline rendering (my profession since 2010) for quite a while already. Making a flexible rasterisation-based renderer is a huge engineering undertaking, see e.g. Unreal Engine. With the relentless march of processing power, and finally having hardware acceleration as rasterisation has enjoyed for decades, it's going to be possible for much smaller teams to deliver realistic and creative (see e.g. Dreams[0]) visuals with far less engineering effort. Some nice recent examples of this are Teardown[1] and Tiny Glade[2]. It's even more inevitable from today's point of view than it was back in the 90s :) [0] Dreams: [LINK] [1] Teardown: [LINK] [2] Tiny Glade: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42601128,"author":"pornel","month":"2025-01","story":"Game dev in Rust: a year later","stance":"reference","why":"Tiny Glade developer satisfied with Rust and Bevy ECS.","quote":"TinyGlade's (co)developer said he's very satisfied with Rust and the crate ecosystem.","text":"TinyGlade's (co)developer said he's very satisfied with Rust and the crate ecosystem. They've used Bevy's ECS: [VIDEO] Using individual components instead of the whole big framework isn't necessarily a failure. The components are designed to be usable independently. TinyGlade project started a few years ago, when Bevy's rendering was even more basic. The art style is central to TinyGlade's appeal, so it's not surprising they've built a custom renderer."},{"id":42365228,"author":"kuschku","month":"2024-12","story":"Pat Gelsinger was wrong for Intel","stance":"reference","why":"Tiny Glade ray marching talk loosely related.","quote":"","text":"> Cell required Assembly programming Demoscene skill level to make good use of them, there was a C sdk, but really you wanted to write Assembly, and relatively tricky Assembly at it. > It is one of the reasons why Playstation 3 is the less celebrated one, and the console generation where XBox was able to pull ahead. In retrospect, Cell was a great architecture. In typical Sony fashion it was simply a few years too early. At the time games were single-threaded and many console games still expected CPU and GPU to be in sync. On the PS2, the difference between using the vector accelerator or not was 1.4x – many developers never bothered. But on the PS3 this was simply not an option, as the performance difference was 6x (!). The big issue at the time was dev tooling. Most existing engines had no support for dispatching jobs or any form of pipelining. Adding that required redesigning the engine from the ground up. On top of that the dispatcher/manager and the jobs use two different µarchs. Obviously, game and engine developers at the time hated cell with a passion.[1] But even on the PC and Xbox, frequency scaling ended with the Pentium 4. Multi-core CPUs became mainstream. As CPUs stalled, most of the performance increase on PCs came from GPUs, that had moved from a fixed-function pipeline to shaders. Slowly, even PC games had to do the bulk of their work in jobs dispatched across multiple cores. Engines that had adapted to Cell got a massive head start. Today, the very textbook "}]},"kRlhlCWplqk":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a cool visualization whenever Theia and Moon formation come up.","q":{"x":"the simulation released last year of the hypothesized Earth-Theia moon formation is really cool","i":38112352,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38112352,"author":"thaumaturgy","month":"2023-11","story":"Moon-forming impactor as a source of Earth's basal mantle anomalies","stance":"rec","why":"Simulation of the hypothesized Earth-Theia collision","quote":"the simulation released last year of the hypothesized Earth-Theia moon formation is really cool","text":"If you haven't seen it yet, the simulation released last year of the hypothesized Earth-Theia moon formation is really cool: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38136790,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2023-11","story":"Remnants of Theia, an ancient plant that collided with the Earth","stance":"rec","why":"NASA supercomputer simulation about the Moon's origin","quote":"this video looks interesting and is produced by NASA","text":"It's not my area, but this video looks interesting and is produced by NASA \" New Supercomputer Simulation Sheds Light on Moon’s Origin \" (October 2022) [VIDEO]"},{"id":41359943,"author":"ck2","month":"2024-08","story":"Matching dinosaur footprints found on opposite sides of the Atlantic Ocean","stance":"reference","why":"Close to a realistic Theia collision render, though not blockbuster quality","quote":"I guess this is close but not like the Hollywood blockbuster it should be","text":"What I want to see is the Theia collision rendered in realistic 3D (and if there was enough gas around for sound to carry, that too!) [LINK] I guess this is close but not like the Hollywood blockbuster it should be [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":40080014,"author":"westurner","month":"2024-04","story":"Scientists solve long-standing mystery surrounding the moon's 'lopsided' geology","stance":"reference","why":"Simulated video of moon forming from a collision","quote":"this simulated video of the moon forming?","text":"How does this finding compare with those from this simulated video of the moon forming? \"Collision May Have Formed the Moon in Mere Hours, Simulations Reveal\" [LINK] [VIDEO] And this, from the moon: \"Scientists discover huge, heat-emitting blob on the far side of the moon\" (2023) [LINK] \"Remote detection of a lunar granitic batholith at Compton–Belkovich\" [from a dormant volcano] (2023) [LINK]"},{"id":38065692,"author":"ck2","month":"2023-10","story":"Mars Lost Its Magnetic Field – and Then Its Oceans","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation showing debris swirling back by gravity","quote":"The simulation gives some insight, the debris was fed back by gravity in a swirl","text":"The simulation gives some insight, the debris was fed back by gravity in a swirl [VIDEO] And always a great PBS Space Time on the subject [LINK]"}],"r":[["Gj8IA6xOpSk",1],["lKhAd0Tyny0",1],["X8aWBcPVPMo",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["kya_LXa_y1E",1],["j2Mu8qfVb5I",1],["jcKRGpMiVTw",1],["s86-Z-CbaHA",1],["zUyH3XhpLTo",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["R2paSGQRwvo",1],["LmlAYnFF_s8",1],["ML4tw_UzqZE",1]]},"qrizmAo17Os":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to reference in threads about HOA absurdity, such as fines over small violations and authoritarian rules.","q":{"x":"A fun John Oliver video on how corrupt HOA's are","i":40533271,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40533271,"author":"arghwhat","month":"2024-05","story":"A man ordered to hide his boat painted the boat on his fence","stance":"rec","why":"Fun take on how corrupt HOAs are","quote":"A fun John Oliver video on how corrupt HOA's are","text":"You do not have the freedom to choose an HOA, only the freedom to move somewhere else. Unless you define \"freedom\" as \"not held captive\", I don't think that really counts. A fun John Oliver video on how corrupt HOA's are: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37079608,"author":"anjel","month":"2023-08","story":"Microgrids Are Giving Power to the People","stance":"reference","why":"HOAs can be authoritarian","quote":"HOAs can get a tad 'authoritarian' at least according to John Oliver","text":"HOAs are in many cases a collection of opinionated amateurs making binding decisions on behalf of their disinterested neighbors. See also 'special assessments' And that's the best case scenario. HOAs can get a tad 'authoritarian' at least according to John Oliver: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35827097,"author":"m000","month":"2023-05","story":"Finland Virtually Ended Homelessness–and We Can Too","stance":"reference","why":"HOAs restrict owner control","quote":"Nobody else, except perhaps your local HOA [1].","text":"> Nobody else has any rights to what I own Nobody else, except perhaps your local HOA [1]. So much about your \"property\". /s [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35709549,"author":"gondaloof","month":"2023-04","story":"Woman could lose Florida home over a violation she didn't know existed","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver segment on HOAs","quote":"John Oliver recently did a show on this.","text":"John Oliver recently did a show on this. HOAs were awful enough in my mind before that, now they’re downright scary. [VIDEO]"}]},"rzXPyCY7jbs":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke in toilet-related threads, especially for the line about German hermeneutic stool inspection.","q":{"x":"You might find this short video entertaining (Zizek on toilets)","i":16715622,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16715622,"author":"lsiq","month":"2018-03","story":"E.P.A. Prepares to Roll Back Rules Requiring Cars Be Cleaner and More Efficient","stance":"rec","why":"Short entertaining video on toilet design and ideology","quote":"You might find this short video entertaining (Zizek on toilets)","text":"Toilet design varies globally, and this is not necessarily due to some function-focused reasons of the designers. You might find this short video entertaining (Zizek on toilets): [VIDEO]"},{"id":47045522,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-02","story":"Evaluating AGENTS.md: are they helpful for coding agents?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"According to Slavoj Žižek, Germans love Hermeneutic stool diagnostics","text":"The cursor-mirror skill and cursor_mirror.py script lets you search through and inschpekt all of your chat histories, all of the thinking bubbles and prompts, all of the context assembly, all of the tool and mcp calls and parameters, and analyze what it did, even after cursor has summarized and pruned and \"forgotten\" it -- it's all still there in the chat log and sqlite databases. [LINK] The German Toilet of AI \"The structure of the toilet reflects how a culture examines itself.\" — Slavoj Zizek German toilets have a shelf. You can inspect what you've produced before flushing. French toilets rush everything away immediately. American toilets sit ambivalently between. cursor-mirror is the German toilet of AI. Most AI systems are French toilets — thoughts disappear instantly, no inspection possible. cursor-mirror provides hermeneutic self-examination: the ability to interpret and understand your own outputs. What context was assembled? What reasoning happened in thinking blocks? What tools were called and why? What files were read, written, modified? This matters for: Debugging — Why did it do that? Learning — What patterns work? Trust — Is this skill behaving as declared? Optimization — What's eating my tokens? See: Skill Ecosystem for how cursor-mirror enables skill curation. ---- [LINK] According to Slavoj Žižek, Germans love Hermeneutic stool diagnostics: [VIDEO] >Žižek on toilets. Slavoj Žižek during an architecture congress in Pamplona, Spain. >The German toilets, the old "},{"id":38577122,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-12","story":"Judge: Amazon \"cannot claim shock\" that bathroom spycams were used as advertised","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That's why German toilets have a Hermeneutic stool inschpektion shelf","text":"That's why German toilets have a Hermeneutic stool inschpektion shelf: So they have something to analyze then talk about all day. [VIDEO] According to Slavoj Žižek, Germans love Hermeneutic stool diagnostics. >Žižek on toilets. Slavoj Žižek during an architecture congress in Pamplona, Spain. >The German toilets, the old kind -- now they are disappearing, but you still find them. It's the opposite. The hole is in front, so that when you produce excrement, they are displayed in the back, they don't disappear in water. This is the German ritual, you know? Use it every morning. Sniff, inspect your shits for traces of illness. It's high Hermeneutic. I think the original meaning of Hermeneutic may be this. [LINK] >Hermeneutics (/ˌhɜːrməˈnjuːtɪks/)[1] is the theory and methodology of interpretation, especially the interpretation of biblical texts, wisdom literature, and philosophical texts. Hermeneutics is more than interpretive principles or methods we resort to when immediate comprehension fails. Rather, hermeneutics is the art of understanding and of making oneself understood. >“In a traditional German toilet, the hole into which shit disappears after we flush is right at the front, so that shit is first laid out for us to sniff and inspect for traces of illness. In the typical French toilet, on the contrary, the hole is at the back, i.e. shit is supposed to disappear as quickly as possible. Finally, the American (Anglo-Saxon) toilet presents a synthesis, a mediation between thes"},{"id":33695355,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-11","story":"Why some feces float and others sink","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Žižek on toilets","text":"Žižek on toilets: [VIDEO] >The German toilets, the old type -- now they are disappearing but you still find them -- it's the opposite. The hole is in front. So that when you produce excrement, they are displayed in the back, they don't disappear in water. This is the German ritual, you know, use it every morning, sniff, inspect your shits for traces of illness. It's High Hermeneutic. I think the original meaning of Hermeneutic for Germans may be this. Žižek: Ideology and Toilets: [LINK] >\"It is easy for an academic at a round table to claim that we live in a post-ideological universe, but the moment he visits the lavatory after the heated discussion, he is again knee-deep in ideology.\" [LINK] >Hermeneutics (/ˌhɜːrməˈnjuːtɪks/)[1] is the theory and methodology of interpretation, especially the interpretation of biblical texts, wisdom literature, and philosophical texts. Hermeneutics is more than interpretive principles or methods we resort to when immediate comprehension fails. Rather, hermeneutics is the art of understanding and of making oneself understood."},{"id":28629020,"author":"beaconstudios","month":"2021-09","story":"The smart toilet era is here Ready to share your analprint with big tech?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this just makes me think of the German approach in Slavoj Zizek's random tangent into the ideology of toilets","text":"this just makes me think of the German approach in Slavoj Zizek's random tangent into the ideology of toilets: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23452607,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-06","story":"‘Smart toilet’ monitors for signs of disease","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"According to Slavoj Žižek, Germans love Hermeneutic stool diagnostics.","text":"According to Slavoj Žižek, Germans love Hermeneutic stool diagnostics. [VIDEO] >Žižek on toilets. Slavoj Žižek during an architecture congress in Pamplona, Spain. >The German toilets, the old kind -- now they are disappearing, but you still find them. It's the opposite. The hole is in front, so that when you produce excrement, they are displayed in the back, they don't disappear in water. This is the German ritual, you know? Use it every morning. Sniff, inspect your shits for traces of illness. It's high Hermeneutic. I think the original meaning of Hermeneutic may be this. [LINK] >Hermeneutics (/ˌhɜːrməˈnjuːtɪks/)[1] is the theory and methodology of interpretation, especially the interpretation of biblical texts, wisdom literature, and philosophical texts. Hermeneutics is more than interpretive principles or methods we resort to when immediate comprehension fails. Rather, hermeneutics is the art of understanding and of making oneself understood."}]},"vVgc8ksstyg":{"wy":"Shared as a quick look at a vintage relay and dekatron computer running, along with praise for the museum.","m":[{"id":7735648,"author":"duncanawoods","month":"2014-05","story":"Bletchley Park row rages on as restored site opens to public with 'Berlin Wall'","stance":"rec","why":"Short video of the Dekatron running at TNMOC.","quote":"TNMOC is awesome. Every geek in the UK should go.","text":"TNMOC is awesome. Every geek in the UK should go. You can gift them old computing books for their growing library. Colossus is cool but seeing stuff I never knew about was more exciting e.g. The Dekatron with its decimal memory you can visually see the values, the awesome radar screens with trakker balls from the 40's or 50's, the cabinets full of early calculators (some are white spitting images of iDevices) or the early CAD system with a graphics card in a larger cabinet than the workstation. Early mainframes and minicomputers are just beautifully styled. 1 min video of the Dekatron running [VIDEO] More info and 20 min video [LINK]"},{"id":17275920,"author":"roywiggins","month":"2018-06","story":"The Land Before Binary","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the Harwell WITCH decimal computer.","quote":"The Harwell WITCH used Dekatrons, which is a device that has 10 states and can therefore store one decimal digit each.","text":"The Harwell WITCH used Dekatrons, which is a device that has 10 states and can therefore store one decimal digit each. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5564526,"author":"laumars","month":"2013-04","story":"If It Ain't Broke, Don't Fix It: Ancient Computers in Use Today","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the Harwell Dekatron relay computer running.","quote":"It was a true sight to see in action as the relays spin round with a hypnotic orange glow[2]","text":"Last month I had the pleasure I of seeing a Dekatron[1] in action (for those that don't know, a dekatron is a decimal based computer, rather than binary). The machine in question is the oldest working computer in the world (older machines like the Colossus are actually reconstructions) and uses relays rather than valves. It was a true sight to see in action as the relays spin round with a hypnotic orange glow[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] (the video doesn't really do it justice)"}],"r":[["YX3iRjKj7C0",1]]},"0FPsEwWT6K0":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as an example of speed-talking debate, usually critically, in threads about the Gish gallop and debate culture.","m":[{"id":33278143,"author":"hairofadog","month":"2022-10","story":"Liz Truss resigns as UK prime minister","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the ridiculous style that prompted the rebellion.","quote":"","text":"I agree; the video in the comment above yours was edited specifically to focus hate on a couple of high school students. While I have to admit I'm not a fan of that debate style, if you want to know the context behind it, there's an episode of Radiolab on the topic: [LINK] The short version is that it seems to have been a response and rebellion to what was already an arguably ridiculous style of competitive \"debating\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":23116632,"author":"bobbiechen","month":"2020-05","story":"Gish Gallop","stance":"reference","why":"Wired video with interviews on speed-talking debate.","quote":"Wired video with some interviews","text":"A particularly ridiculous version of this is \"spreading\" in debate, where you speak extremely fast so that your opponent has difficulty addressing all your points. Wired video with some interviews: [VIDEO] And a transcribed video from a highly-prepped debate: [LINK]"},{"id":36927430,"author":"shortrounddev2","month":"2023-07","story":"How critical theory is radicalizing high school debate","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"someone screaming facts at you at 300wpm in between gasps of air","text":"High school debate is not a good event for this kind of argumentation. It's a very technical event with stupid rules and ridiculous \"speeches\" which consiset of someone screaming facts at you at 300wpm in between gasps of air [VIDEO]"},{"id":36927774,"author":"phoenixreader","month":"2023-07","story":"How critical theory is radicalizing high school debate","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Even the Onion could not have written a better skit.","text":"fun fact: an average \"policy debate\" speaker speaks 350 words per minute and is completely unintelligible to an average person. They speak faster than auctioneers. A \"policy debate\" is not your average debate and is more like its own sport. The goal is to cram so many arguments into the limited time that your opponents cannot address them all. Watch these and you definitely cannot understand them: [VIDEO] [LINK] Even the Onion could not have written a better skit."},{"id":25249010,"author":"a9h74j","month":"2020-11","story":"Microsoft patents tech to score meetings with body language, facial expressions","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"State as many points as you can while gasping for breath.","text":"\"Policy\"-style debate[1][2]. What happens when metrics must be gamed to win. State as many points as you can while gasping for breath. Coming to coworkers near you. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19293346,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2019-03","story":"Intellectual Denial of Service Attacks","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"creating a truly absurd spectacle","text":"See also the Gish Gallop - a debating technique named after the creationist Duane Gish, which involves making as many specious arguments as quickly as possible. An unprepared opponent is likely to be overwhelmed by the sheer number of points they need to refute. [LINK] A variant of this technique is now standard in competitive debating, creating a truly absurd spectacle; most teams speak as quickly as humanly possible to fit the greatest number of arguments into a fixed time limit, creating a barely-intelligible blur of words. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":15443506,"author":"smacktoward","month":"2017-10","story":"The Corrosion of High School Debate","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"difficult and unpleasant to listen to","text":"Let's take race and gender off the table and consider this video instead: [VIDEO] Here we see a bunch of (white, male) kids, all of whom are supposed to be experts in persuasive rhetoric, who have been trained to make arguments in a way that is utterly unpersuasive to anyone who has not spent years steeping in the world of academic debate. In fact, not only is it unpersuasive to the untrained ear, it is actively off-putting -- difficult and unpleasant to listen to. Their method demonstrates both of the troubling points that TFA calls out: 1) They have been taught to cram more arguments into a given amount of time than their opponents can, so they race through their arguments at 350 words per minute like meth-fueled auctioneers; and 2) They have been taught to crowd out their opponents' time to make their own arguments by throwing out as many outrageous, nonsensical arguments as they can, since an argument their opponent does not refute will be scored by the judges as a point won regardless of its factual merit. These things are fine if you see debate as a sort of abstract exercise. But if the point of debate is to train young people how to argue persuasively in the real world, they are disastrous , because they do the opposite. The \"overload your opponent with nonsensical arguments\" part teaches them to be aggressively obtuse, and the \"spit out the words as fast as you can\" part teaches them to communicate in a way that no lay person can understand or appreciate. And taken to"}]},"2oEnJfZ9joY":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a joke in threads about Soylent and meal replacements, comparing the slurry to the Matrix scene.","m":[{"id":17810811,"author":"joewee","month":"2018-08","story":"This AI is bad at drawing but will try anyways","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"maybe they couldn’t figure out what chicken taste like, which is why chicken taste like everything.","text":"This reminds me of the scene from the Matrix when they question whether the AI knows how to replicate the taste of human food “maybe they couldn’t figure out what chicken taste like, which is why chicken taste like everything.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":6175485,"author":"deletes","month":"2013-08","story":"Interview with Rob Rhinehart about Soylent","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Nobody noticed this but Soylent actually made its debut in the matrix.","text":"Nobody noticed this but Soylent actually made its debut in the matrix. [VIDEO] Truly the food of the future. (controlled by machines.)"},{"id":5215371,"author":"hammock","month":"2013-02","story":"How I Stopped Eating Food","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It looks remarkably like the \"bowl of snot\" that Neo and his buddies eat in the Matrix.","text":"It looks remarkably like the \"bowl of snot\" that Neo and his buddies eat in the Matrix. \"It's a single-celled protein combined with synthetic aminos, vitamins, and minerals. Everything the body needs.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"5HDN9fUlqU8":{"wy":"Linked as the go-to demonstration that the cyclist-versus-driver conflict at intersections is a solved design problem.","q":{"x":"Well guess what, we've figured this out decades ago in the Netherlands","i":13207338,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":32721965,"author":"NavinF","month":"2022-09","story":"EV adoption in US is happening faster than predicted","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch junction with doubts about US adoption.","quote":"but I found a direct link","text":"That video doesn’t load for me, but I found a direct link: [VIDEO] I’d love to avoid cutting into the bike lane (required for right turns in most of the US), but some of those intersections are massive and the rest (1:18 in the video) replace the sidewalk with a bike lane. I don’t think either of those will be popular in American cities."},{"id":27303458,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-05","story":"How I, as someone who is visually impaired, use my iPhone (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch junction design as desired for US cities.","quote":"If only the US could adopt Dutch junction design [1].","text":"This is mostly a good thing. I say \"mostly\" because it encourages scooter riders to use the sidewalk and pretty much anywhere I've been scooter-share riders are a public menace. One issue with these in NYC at least is the drainage just isn't designed for them. So anytime you get significant rain or snow melt you get pools of water that don't drain because the actual drains are elsewhere. If only the US could adopt Dutch junction design [1]. And don't get me started on the cyclist and pedestrian safety nightmare that is allowing people to turn right at red lights. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25731896,"author":"Muximize","month":"2021-01","story":"The great bicycle boom of 2020","stance":"reference","why":"Examples and US context of Dutch junctions.","quote":"Thousands of these intersections have been build","text":"There's usually no need to yield, traffic signals regulate that. The idea here is to create a more forgiving environment where making mistakes is less likely and less harmful. Thousands of these intersections have been build, here's some examples and some US context and research: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":13207338,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2016-12","story":"How Sweden Has Redesigned Streets to Route Around Bad Behavior","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch junction design solves cyclist-driver conflict.","quote":"we've figured this out decades ago in the Netherlands","text":"As a Dutchman living in Stockholm I can tell you one thing: they should fix their goddamn bike lanes. Sure, they have them, which makes them better than 90% of the rest of the world. They're still shit. There's no separation of car- and bike traffic. To turn left a cyclist first has to cross parallel car traffic, making this unsafer for cyclists (and annoying for the people in cars, for that matter). Key issues: - the cyclist and driver should meet at a (near) 90 degree angle, so neither requires eyes in the back of their head - separate the goddamn lanes so neither car nor cyclist feels uncomfortably close to the other - don't require extra space because every square meter counts in a big city Well guess what, we've figured this out decades ago in the Netherlands: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"8CwoluNRSSc":{"wy":"HN mentions it as a joke when Apple's own security prompts or Gatekeeper restrictions annoy users.","m":[{"id":46922805,"author":"ghgr","month":"2026-02","story":"OpenCiv3: Open-source, cross-platform reimagining of Civilization III","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> How far OSX has come since the days of the “cancel or allow” parody advert. In case you're wondering like me, this is the advert in question: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45917850,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2025-11","story":"Homebrew no longer allows bypassing Gatekeeper for unsigned/unnotarized software","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Remember when Apple made fun of Microsoft for doing exactly this?","text":"> The ridiculous song and dance of \"File is dangerous, delete it?\"->No->Settings->Security->Open Anyway->\"File is dangerous, delete it?\"->No is getting ridiculously old after literally doing it a hundred times at this point. And soon enough Apple will inevitably come up with some additional hurdle like, idk, closing Settings three times in a row while reading a fingerprint during an odd numbered minute. > So in the name of \"increased security\" they've needlessly turned it into a binary thing where it's completely unprotected or accept my own computer that I paid for will deliberately waste my time constantly. Remember when Apple made fun of Microsoft for doing exactly this? [VIDEO]"},{"id":22221741,"author":"xtracto","month":"2020-02","story":"Is Catalina a Good Upgrade Yet?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Macs permission prompts remind me of this: [VIDEO] There is several software that doesn't work with Catalina. In my case RingCentral meetings. But in general people shouldn't downgrade to it... it is 100% a downgrade, as it removes features available in the previous version."}]},"CQJgFh_e01g":{"wy":"Cited as a concise critique of Hyperloop feasibility whenever the idea comes up.","q":{"x":"Some details by Adam Something on the technology itself","i":29896564,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29896564,"author":"fabianhjr","month":"2022-01","story":"Yesterday, US grounded all planes on the west coast for ten minutes","stance":"reference","why":"Details on why Hyperloop technology will not work","quote":"Some details by Adam Something on the technology itself","text":"> When does the next hyperloop leave SF for Tokyo? Never, the whole world has been building more efficient and cost-effective transportation such as high speed rail. Some details by Adam Something on the technology itself: \"The HYPERLOOP Will Never Work, And Here's Why\" < [VIDEO] >"},{"id":29354665,"author":"throwaway946513","month":"2021-11","story":"Hyperloop: Elon Musk merely renamed a 200-year-old dream","stance":"reference","why":"Cost and pollution of a near vacuum versus metros","quote":"the near vacuum costing and polluting more than a conventional metro","text":"But one of the biggest issues I've seen regarding the hyperloop is the near vacuum costing and polluting more than a conventional metro, let alone being slower to shuffle total people than a metro, and more overall more expensive than a metro. For reference: [0] [VIDEO] While this isn't explicitly about the hyperloop, it gives a good similar line with relation to the hyperloop [1] [LINK]"},{"id":33392313,"author":"HyperSane","month":"2022-10","story":"Tesla engineers were on-site to evaluate the Twitter staff’s code, workers said","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Hyperloop is very much a con job.","text":"Hyperloop is very much a con job. \"Full Self Driving is just a year away\" is very much a con job. The cybertruck is a con job. The Tesla Semi is a con job. The tesla robot is a con job. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Mark Spiegel: Elon Musk is ‘a pathological liar’ [LINK] The Fake Futurism of Elon Musk [LINK] DEBUNKING ELON MUSK Pt1 [LINK] The HYPERLOOP Will Never Work, And Here's Why [VIDEO]"}]},"H7Uyfqi_TE8":{"wy":"Referenced as Musk's original statement of SpaceX's multiplanetary plan.","q":{"x":"Elon's original presentation from IAC 2016","i":24057808,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":43020423,"author":"TheMiddleMan","month":"2025-02","story":"A Year of Telepathy","stance":"reference","why":"Older talk showing Musk's passion for multiplanetary goals","quote":"I detect some real passion back in these videos.","text":"You might be right but consider these old talks: Making Humans a Multiplanetary Species (2016) [VIDEO] Elon Musk talks Climate Change and Carbon Tax at the Sorbone 2015 [LINK] It could be that he was just following the trends knowing there was money to be made, but I detect some real passion back in these videos. Of course no motivation comes from a single source and there's some mix of philanthropy and money driving this, among other motivations. Either way his tone has changed a lot, to say the least."},{"id":25109219,"author":"mandeepj","month":"2020-11","story":"SpaceX Crew-1 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's pitch on living off Earth","quote":"","text":"> If you're going to get a significant fraction of the human race living off Earth you'll need to build something nicer than Earth that's what Blue Origin is working-on but their timeline is unsure to me :-) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24057808,"author":"yreg","month":"2020-08","story":"SpaceX test flight of Starship SN-5 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's original 2016 IAC presentation on multiplanetary plans","quote":"Elon's original presentation from IAC 2016","text":"Not the parent poster, but there is [0] Making Humans a Multiplanetary Species / Elon's original presentation from IAC 2016 - [VIDEO] [1] Starship Update / 2019 - [LINK]"}]},"ODZBL80JPqw":{"wy":"Listed among historical GUI and Smalltalk videos and cited for early features such as math in word processing and the Mesa-programmed Star.","m":[{"id":30112637,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-01","story":"Prime Video Uses WebAssembly","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in discussion of UI widget complexity","quote":"","text":"I share that passion too! Most people grossly underestimate how difficult it is to implement something as seemingly simple as a text field or menu, when there are so many hidden issues and techniques that make them easy to use because you don't notice all the support you're getting. Well implemented user interfaces have polish that makes their inherent complexity invisible, but polish is actually millions of tiny little scratches, not just a clean simple perfectly flat surface. Accessibility and internationalization are two crucial dimensions that most people forget about (especially non-sensory/motor-impaired Americans), which each add huge amounts of unavoidable complexity to text fields and the rest of the widget set. Then there's text selection, character/word/paragraph level selection, drag-n-drop, pending delete, scrolling, scrollbar hiding, auto scroll, focus management, keyboard navigation and shortcuts, copy and paste, alternative input methods, type-ahead, etc, all which need to perfectly dovetail together (like auto-scrolling working correctly during selection and drag-n-drop, auto-scrolling triggering on a reasonably scaled timer regardless of mouse position instead of mouse movements only inside the text field, so scrolling is deterministically controllable and happens at a reasonable speed, and doesn't freeze when you stop moving the mouse or move it too far, etc). There are so many half-assed custom text fields out there written by well intentioned people who j"},{"id":13601283,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-02","story":"Grappling with Go","stance":"reference","why":"Background on Xerox Star programmed in Mesa","quote":"Mesa/Cedar was created at Xerox PARC, as an extension of the Xerox Star 8010 system, which was programmed in Mesa","text":"Faire enough, so prepare for an history lesson. :) Mesa/Cedar was created at Xerox PARC, as an extension of the Xerox Star 8010 system, which was programmed in Mesa, the language that inspired Niklaus Wirth to create Modula-2. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] Mesa evolved to Mesa/Cedar, which allowed for automatic memory management via reference counting and a tracing collector for the cycles. [LINK] The environment had a REPL and debugger inspired by the Smalltalk and Lisp systems at Xerox. Think of Swift Playgrounds, kind of. This system inspired Niklaus Wirth to create Oberon, which you can read about in his book, about how the OS was built. [LINK] In 2013, Niklaus Wirth revised the language and updated the book for a FPGA based computer. [LINK] There were several generations of the Oberon OS, the last one before it stop being relevant, the Bluebottle OS coded in Active Oberon variant. [LINK] It had a strange mix of UI, using a mix of text and mouse input. Basically all applications were dynamic modules and the procedures/functions with a special signature could be used from the UI. So you could select text or objects from applications, and invoke such operations on the selection. The Oberon System 3 and Oberon V4 with their Gadgets framework were the best experience, before AOS was designed. Most relevant OS in Modula-3 was SPIN OS, [LINK] There were a few others, this is just a small sample."},{"id":7920840,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2014-06","story":"Web Starter Kit","stance":"reference","why":"One of a list of historical GUI and Smalltalk videos","quote":"Xerox Star User Interface","text":"Self [LINK] Xerox Star User Interface [LINK] [VIDEO] Tektronix 4404 Smalltalk [LINK] The Smalltalk-80 Programming System [LINK] The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer [LINK] Symbolics Hypertext Document [LINK] Warren Teitelman work at Xerox PARC [LINK] The Mesa/Cedar programming environment [LINK] Steve Jobs internal demo of NeXTSTEP 3, [LINK] Alan Kay on Xerox PARC work [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":7565751,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2014-04","story":"Shadow DOM","stance":"reference","why":"One of a list of historical GUI and Smalltalk videos","quote":"Xerox Star User Interface","text":"Mainly videos Self [LINK] Xerox Star User Interface [LINK] [VIDEO] Tektronix 4404 Smalltalk [LINK] The Smalltalk-80 Programming System [LINK] The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer [LINK] Symbolics Hypertext Document [LINK]"},{"id":6011394,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2013-07","story":"Tangle: a JavaScript library for reactive documents","stance":"reference","why":"Part of list of Xerox and Lisp machine presentations","quote":"Xerox presentations about Smalltalk-80","text":"Except the original Lisp and Smalltalk environments are much more than a simple REPL. Sometimes I wish people would learn about computing history. Presentation from Kalman Reti about Lisp Machines, check the interactivity starting at 00:44:00 and how to introduce mouse sensitive images at 01:00:00 [LINK] Xerox presentations about Smalltalk-80 [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5936119,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2013-06","story":"German City Set To Make Linux A Norm For Citizens  ","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox had math formulas in word processing in 1982","quote":"The Xerox Alto had this in 1982[1].","text":"\"Abiword normally does not have built in facilities for mathematical formulas\" The Xerox Alto had this in 1982[1]. NINETEEN FREAKING EIGHTY TWO. How is it possible that any WYSIWYG word processor is allowed to ship without this? [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"TY4s35uULg4":{"wy":"HN cites it as precedent for repurposing mining-only GPUs for graphics, in threads about Nvidia limiting mining on gaming cards.","q":{"x":"I first heard about using the p106-p90 cards on Windows with a modified driver via a LTT video [1].","i":26294350,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":46377609,"author":"theandrewbailey","month":"2025-12","story":"The post-GeForce era: What if Nvidia abandons PC gaming?","stance":"reference","why":"LTT got a mining GPU to render using modified firmware and driver","quote":"LTT did something similar with a mining \"GPU\" several years ago.[0]","text":"LTT did something similar with a mining \"GPU\" several years ago.[0] It involved buying a mining card with modified firmware and installing a modified and unsigned driver which shoved the rendered frames into the integrated GPU to output. It's possible these datacenter AI GPUs are built so different from conventional GPUs that they lack required hardware to draw polygons, like ROPs and texture units. Why waste chip engineering time and silicon die space to support applications that a product isn't designed for? Let me remind you that gaming is a small slice of NVidia's balance sheet[1], so it makes sense to not have to use one chip design for everything. [0] [VIDEO] [1] $4.3 billion out of $57 billion [LINK]"},{"id":27202816,"author":"mikepurvis","month":"2021-05","story":"Nvidia cripples cryptocurrency mining on RTX 3080 and 3070 cards","stance":"reference","why":"Precedent of routing ex-miner card video output over PCI bus","quote":"There is some precedent for hacking ex-miner cards to route the video signal back to a motherboard output over the PCI bus","text":"There is some precedent for hacking ex-miner cards to route the video signal back to a motherboard output over the PCI bus: [VIDEO] With so many more of them out there, it'll be interesting to see if this becomes more of a thing. Alternatively, it's easy to imagine that depending on just how constrained these are, they might eventually find homes doing non-crypto headless DC workloads, such as deep learning stuff."},{"id":27227249,"author":"freeflight","month":"2021-05","story":"Nvidia cripples cryptocurrency mining on RTX 3080 and 3070 cards","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier Nvidia mining-only cards that lacked resale value","quote":"Afaik Nvidia already tried that [0] but miners didn't really bite","text":"> Nvidia is trying to get miners to buy mining-only cards, because those can't be sold to gamers down the line. Afaik Nvidia already tried that [0] but miners didn't really bite because the lack of resale value for those cards cut too much into their profits. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26484442,"author":"eins1234","month":"2021-03","story":"GeForce RTX 3060 Ethereum Mining Restrictions Have Been Broken","stance":"reference","why":"LTT video on gaming on a mining card","quote":"I remember watching a Linus Tech Tips video on it a while ago.","text":"For those looking for details, I remember watching a Linus Tech Tips video on it a while ago. Just managed to dig it up: [VIDEO] Forum thread has more: [LINK]"},{"id":26186175,"author":"arsome","month":"2021-02","story":"Nvidia announces mining GPUs, cuts the hash rate of RTX-3060 in half","stance":"reference","why":"Mining-only cards were hacked to render graphics","quote":"people managed to crack the drivers to render graphics too... this is no exception.","text":"Sounds like it's time to crack some NVIDIA drivers. I already do it for the video encoding limitations, why not this too? Miners are willing to go to some really hacky extremes, reflashing firmwares, custom fan mods, extreme overclock configurations, etc to maximize hashrates and certainly won't care if they have to jump through some hoops to patch a specific version of the Linux driver or enable unsigned drivers on Windows, as long as they make more profit. Good luck with this one NVIDIA but you're playing a losing game. As soon as one un-restricted version of the driver exists, this is as good as useless and demand will be the same as ever for these cards unless they're able to ship CMP cards at a very high rate, but my understanding is the restriction here is silicon. Let's not forget that last time they produced \"mining only\" cards, people managed to crack the drivers to render graphics too... this is no exception. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26188139,"author":"ILikeBikes","month":"2021-02","story":"Nvidia announces mining GPUs, cuts the hash rate of RTX-3060 in half","stance":"reference","why":"LTT showing a mining GPU repurposed for gaming","quote":"as featured in LTT","text":"I do hate miners But I'm concerned that a company can tell you what you can or can't do with your hardware. Where is the spirit behind the hacker community, Jailbreaking iPhones, the right to repair, the ban of tattoos that prevented the use of Linux on PCs,... That's a very concerning move from me. Also, for a company that does morale : (1) Nvidia's marketing is one of the best for making old products look obsolete and replaceable. Most gamers would do great with their old gen card, they just don't need to upgrade already. But they reserve cards for them so that they can create as much e-waste as they want (2) they could just have prevented supply issues if their supply chains were diverse and resilient. But it turns out they aren't. And it's not something that was asked by the gamers (3) for a company that's focused around gamers, they have already prevented powerful \"crypto\" GPUs from being repurposed into gaming gear, as featured in LTT [VIDEO]"},{"id":26212692,"author":"PartiallyTyped","month":"2021-02","story":"Nvidia Limits RTX 3060 Hash Rate","stance":"reference","why":"Shows rendering games on a card and outputting via another display","quote":"It is entirely possible to render video games and stream them to another display [1,2]","text":"It can be. NVDA's stance is utter bull. It is entirely possible to render video games and stream them to another display [1,2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":26215909,"author":"solarkraft","month":"2021-02","story":"Nvidia Limits RTX 3060 Hash Rate","stance":"reference","why":"Attempt to use a mining card for rendering via integrated GPU","quote":"TL;DW: Very weird and extremely unofficial, but it can be done.","text":"While officially impossible, theoretically it should be work, in the same way as laptop GPUs (the dedicated GPU gives the rendered image back to the integrated GPU for seamless switching instead of outputing it directly). It has been tried 2 years ago on LinusTechTips: [VIDEO] TL;DW: Very weird and extremely unofficial, but it can be done. These might still be some cheap rendering cards on Linux when they're used up."},{"id":26294350,"author":"tvb12","month":"2021-02","story":"The GPU Sadness Index: Tracking eBay Pricing","stance":"reference","why":"Mining card reused via modified driver and integrated GPU output","quote":"I first heard about using the p106-p90 cards on Windows with a modified driver via a LTT video [1].","text":"There's a sliver of hope that the mining cards will be reusable as general purpose gpus by having the card stream its output to an integrated gpu. I first heard about using the p106-p90 cards on Windows with a modified driver via a LTT video [1]. I later read a blog post where someone claimed that the card was plug-and-play on Pop!_OS (a Linux distro) [2]. I think this is fairly well known at this point. I would be very curious to see how many were actually reused, though. I remember seeing a listing for a large number of p100 cards a few months ago, but I don't see any listings now. Were they scooped up and reused, or did the sellers give up and trash them? [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] (search for \"following up\")"},{"id":24921653,"author":"colejohnson66","month":"2020-10","story":"AMD Reveals the Radeon RX 6000 Series, Coming November 18th","stance":"reference","why":"LTT tries getting video output from an Nvidia mining card","quote":"I recall a video by LinusTechTips who attempted to get video output from one of them.","text":"Nvidia as well made “crypto mining” cards that were just a mainline card without video output. I recall a video by LinusTechTips who attempted to get video output from one of them. Edit: Here’s the video: [VIDEO]"}]},"TuupoxmeQ6U":{"wy":"Cited as the best available video of Doom on a TI calculator in threads about Doom ports and calculator programming.","q":{"x":"Best video I can find","i":4879144,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":13008598,"author":"dosshell","month":"2016-11","story":"Doom on the MacBook Pro Touch Bar [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm sorry to tell you it is even more complicated than if it can run doom. Does this count as doom? [VIDEO] Another definition i have seen is the QWERTY keyboard restriction. If i remember correctly some good calculators from Texas Instruments have abc keyword to be allowed on some big math tests in US. (Or is this maybe just a myth in Europe?)"},{"id":4879144,"author":"gipsies","month":"2012-12","story":"Portal’s physics engine rebuilt in 25KB—on a graphing calculator","stance":"reference","why":"Best video found of Doom on a TI-83","quote":"Best video I can find","text":"I remember playing a lot with my old TI-83. One of the most impressive games was DOOM: [LINK] Best video I can find: [VIDEO] Unfortunately I never had a TI-89. Would've been awesome if I had one back in the day, and then test these games: [LINK] and [LINK]"},{"id":9802186,"author":"xahrepap","month":"2015-06","story":"A Doom-like engine on the Raspberry Pi – 9800 lines of bare metal assembly","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Porting Doom to everything has always been a thing. Way before Atwood's Law was ever a thing. It was the original Atwood's Law. :) [VIDEO]"}]},"UOWexFaRylM":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that scanning the whole IPv4 address space is trivial and takes only minutes to hours.","q":{"x":"It is trivial to scan the whole ipv4 address space.","i":9797613,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":20941913,"author":"kayfox","month":"2019-09","story":"Show HN: Enter your URL and view CVEs affecting your stack over last 6 months","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the likely DEFCON talk on mass scanning","quote":"That's probably the DEFCON talk the parent poster is talking about","text":"That's probably the DEFCON talk the parent poster is talking about: Massscanning the Internet - Defcon 22 (2014) [VIDEO]"},{"id":9797613,"author":"phatfish","month":"2015-06","story":"Vnc-roulette – Randomly connects to open VNC servers","stance":"reference","why":"Presenters scanned the IPv4 space in about 40 minutes","quote":"It is trivial to scan the whole ipv4 address space.","text":"These guys will tell you: [VIDEO] It is trivial to scan the whole ipv4 address space. I think the guys in this video did it in 40mins or so while presenting."},{"id":8778654,"author":"sysk","month":"2014-12","story":"Scans of North Korean IP Space","stance":"reference","why":"Whole-Internet scan possible in hours","quote":"it was possible to scan the whole Internet in a few hours on a regular connection","text":"As a side note, I recently learned that it was possible to scan the whole Internet in a few hours on a regular connection: [VIDEO]"}]},"Zxk_WywMTzc":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that multi-touch interfaces predated the iPhone, and as an example of Microsoft innovation.","q":{"x":"See for example the Microsoft Surface (now renamed to PixelSense) from 2008","i":4463457,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":14532939,"author":"smt88","month":"2017-06","story":"Ask HN: Other innovative companies like 3M?","stance":"reference","why":"Original Surface listed as a Microsoft innovation","quote":"Original Microsoft Surface (tabletop computing)","text":"Preventing SIDS via data analysis: [LINK] Original Microsoft Surface (tabletop computing): [VIDEO] Real-time language translation on Skype: [LINK] TouchWall: [LINK] F#, TypeScript, P, and possibly other programming languages HoloDesk Kinect Industry-leading speech recognition: [LINK] HomeOS There are more, but I'm too lazy to Google it. They also had a dual-screen, folding tablet that I really wished had made it to the market, but I can't remember the name."},{"id":12780098,"author":"alanh","month":"2016-10","story":"Why Tim Cook Is Steve Ballmer and Why He Still Has His Job at Apple","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of the original Surface smart table","quote":"original Surface” isn’t what you think. It was a “smart table","text":"For the kids out there: “original Surface” isn’t what you think. It was a “smart table” and its most compelling use case was to present a menu right underneath your place setting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4463457,"author":"jkn","month":"2012-09","story":"Apple asks court to ban Samsung’s Galaxy Note and Galaxy S III from US","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Microsoft Surface touch manipulation predating iPhone","quote":"See for example the Microsoft Surface (now renamed to PixelSense) from 2008","text":"Well here is a video of the Neonode prior art: [LINK] It's unlocked with a left to right swipe. Contrary to the iPhone there is no image moving with the finger. I don't think that difference is worth a patent. Moving things (i.e. images) around with the finger is a pretty obvious concept for a purely touch-based device. See for example the Microsoft Surface (now renamed to PixelSense) from 2008: [VIDEO]"},{"id":576882,"author":"tvon","month":"2009-04","story":"Skin Deep Usability (Microsoft Surface)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it does seem like a bit of a waste in the hands of Microsoft","text":"Microsoft Surface Demo @ CES 2008: [VIDEO] Every time I see a Surface demo I'm struck at the whole \"display photos as a jumbled mess, just like real photos!\" thing. It just seems so... idiotic. Anyway, there does seem to be some potential with Surface but it does seem like a bit of a waste in the hands of Microsoft."}]},"aWfYxg-Ypm4":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reference in threads about DevOps, cognitive load and rewrites to Node.js.","m":[{"id":45801529,"author":"jgehrcke","month":"2025-11","story":"Why we migrated from Python to Node.js","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm sure you know what you're taking about -- yet, your response reminds me of [VIDEO] \"drizzle works on the edge\""},{"id":42512064,"author":"AIorNot","month":"2024-12","story":"Cognitive load is what matters","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The FUNDAMENTAL aspect and nature of ALL Software Engineering is: \"Controlling Complexity\" I dare say its the most important part of the \"Engineering\" in software engineering. 'Cognitive Load' is another way of saying this.. 1. programming => wiring shit together and something happens 2. software engineering => using experience, skills and user and fellow engineer empathy to iterate toward a sustainable mass of code that makes something happen in a predictable way It is exceedingly difficult to do this right and even when you do it right, what was right at one time becomes tomorrow's 'Chesterton's Fence' ( [LINK] ) , but I have worked on projects and code where this was achieved at least somewhat sustainably (usually under the vision of a single, great developer). Unfortunately the economics of modern development means we let the tools and environments handle our complexity and just scrap and rewrite code to meet deadlines.. I mean look at the state of web development these days [VIDEO]"},{"id":40826865,"author":"adolph","month":"2024-06","story":"A Eulogy for DevOps","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes, some K8s operators to cost your deployments so you can auto scale based on ROI of a microservice instead of raw utilization. No point in spending on five nines if nobody will even pay for two. It’s all straight out of “programmers are also human” [VIDEO]"}]},"aZ2czFuIYmQ":{"wy":"Cited as the source for claims that PVC records outgas harmful compounds, usually in vinyl revival threads.","q":{"x":"The video creator uses an air pollution meter[0] to find out that his records are causing very high PM 2.5, VOC, and formaldehyde.","i":20917738,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":43221610,"author":"01HNNWZ0MV43FF","month":"2025-03","story":"Vinyl carver sparking a craze for cutting records at home","stance":"reference","why":"Summary: a VOC meter shows records are toxic.","quote":"tl;dw: He puts a VOC meter to a vinyl record and yep they're definitely toxic.","text":"> As far as I know vinyl is toxic [VIDEO] \"Vinyl: Maybe it's time we had an intervention\" by Benn Jordan. tl;dw: He puts a VOC meter to a vinyl record and yep they're definitely toxic. Remember, if you smell something, it's going inside your body. That includes \"new plastic smell\", farts, etc. > This is just my objective opinion based on a whole lotta research... if you're an avid vinyl collector whose pee-pee hurts after watching this video, understand that my pee-pee hurts a whole lot more."},{"id":43482864,"author":"devnullbrain","month":"2025-03","story":"'A walking billboard': The history of San Francisco's most recognizable bag","stance":"reference","why":"Source that PVC records off-gas even when unplayed.","quote":"the PVC used will off-gas even when they're not being played[1]","text":"Books don't give us cancer like records do. Unfortunately, the PVC used will off-gas even when they're not being played[1], so this even applies to the 48%[2] of record buyers who never play them. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":29049955,"author":"ReactiveJelly","month":"2021-10","story":"Taking apart the 2010 Fisher Price re-released Music Box Record Player","stance":"reference","why":"Summarizes claims that records outgas and cannot be recycled.","quote":"Vinyl records outgas harmful things that you can detect with air quality meters.","text":"Re: vinyl records Has anyone proven / disproven Benn Jordan's video about why vinyl is basically poisonous? [VIDEO] I haven't watched it for a while but the summary is: - Vinyl records outgas harmful things that you can detect with air quality meters. (VOCs I think) - You can't dispose of them because they can't be recycled and aren't supposed to go in landfills. So buying new records from new bands violates the \"reduce, reuse, recycle\" principle \"It's just that it's _so_ lame and uncool to shit on vinyl...\" \"This is just my objective opinion based on a whole lotta research... If you're an avid collector whose peepee hurts after watching this video, understand that my peepee hurts a whole lot more.\""},{"id":24440314,"author":"kekebo","month":"2020-09","story":"Vinyl LPs Sell More Than CDs for the First Time in 3 Decades","stance":"reference","why":"Source for vinyl releasing toxic chemicals.","quote":"it seems to release toxic chemicals into the air where its being played or even just stored","text":"There is a bit of a sad downside to vinyl: it seems to release toxic chemicals into the air where its being played or even just stored [0]. I hope there are ways to find alternative materials because I thoroughly enjoy the medium overall. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20917313,"author":"tripzilch","month":"2019-09","story":"Vinyl set to outsell CDs for first time since 1986","stance":"reference","why":"Measures air quality around vinyl records, showing dangerous compounds.","quote":"It talks about, demonstrates and measures the air quality around vinyl.","text":"So I've been thinking a lot about this video recently: [VIDEO] It talks about, demonstrates and measures the air quality around vinyl. Apparently the things are leaching dangerous compounds into the air constantly, especially when being played. It made his air quality measuring device give an alarm, instead of just reporting the number of particles or something. I'm not sure, maybe it's just one of those thousands of every day life things which are technically carcinogenic, or not. It made me pause personally though, having recently lost a friend, who worked at a record store, died at a young age to cancer, had otherwise always been a strong healthy man. I'm actually more comfortable talking about this here, semi-anonymously, I don't think it'd be wise to bring this up IRL with anyone, on account of it possibly not having to do with anything. I kind of half-wish I hadn't seen the video."},{"id":20917738,"author":"consumer451","month":"2019-09","story":"Vinyl set to outsell CDs for first time since 1986","stance":"reference","why":"Meter readings show high PM 2.5, VOCs, formaldehyde from records.","quote":"The video creator uses an air pollution meter[0] to find out that his records are causing very high PM 2.5, VOC, and formaldehyde.","text":"I just watched the GP video, and science does seem to support that vinyl records are likely dangerous. The video creator uses an air pollution meter[0] to find out that his records are causing very high PM 2.5, VOC, and formaldehyde. The vinyl records seem to only need to be taken out of their sleeves. The results look shocking. There have to be some HN users who have the knowledge and equipment to attempt to replicate this experiment. Please do that and share! [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"djV11Xbc914":{"wy":"It is mostly a cultural touchstone on HN, cited for its sketch animation style or the song itself rather than recommended as something to learn from.","q":{"x":"reminds me of the animation style of the 1980s music video \"Take On Me\" by the band a-ha","i":18101874,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":29068851,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-11","story":"Show HN: Use your iPhone's camera to see any photo on your wall","stance":"reference","why":"Takes the music video door idea as an AR feature inspiration.","quote":"","text":"Great job! My ultimate (and totally unrealistic) wish list: Multitouch dragging and resizing and rotating and pushing and pulling images. Really cool if it works seamlessly with camera motion and rotation too. So you can correct the size and depth of misplaced pictures easily. (The dragging gesture currently does, hurrya! Now \"just\" make the resizing and twisting and pulling and pushing gestures work that way by following camera motion too! Yes it's tricky code but it'll be worth it and satisfying.) Bump the edges of dragging and resizing pictures up against the corners of the room. And when picture edges bump together they push each other around, with 2d physics (or use 3d physics with gravity oriented so the wall is a flat or graded floor). So you can't drag a picture into an orthogonal wall, or make pictures on the same wall overlap, and the wall corners and picture frames have more physicality, so you can easily align multiple images along a corner. Scroll all the pictures on the wall at once by dragging the background like a virtual desktop that clips to the corners of the room. Full depth 3d frames, that you can look around and see the back of. Hinged frames that you can open this way or that to reveal a hidden safe (that you can open by twisting the dial to see what's inside) or window (that you can see through in parallax to another outdoor photo (or 3d scene with dancing characters) you've placed outside in the distance, like the a-ha \"Take On Me\" music video door AR"},{"id":28003589,"author":"sundvor","month":"2021-07","story":"Never Gonna Give You Up by Rick Astley reaches one billion views [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Notes the song's absence from the billion-views list and personal fondness.","quote":"it's kind of my lullaby for getting daughter to sleep","text":"Wow, Take On Me doesn't even hit that list. [VIDEO] Maybe it's because it's the 4k remaster, only out for 11 years? Nonetheless, it's got a couple of hundred views from this household - but tuning more into the acoustic one now which is a masterpiece - and was also featured in Deadpool 2 (sadly not prominently in the Disney Plus version). [LINK] Sorry a bit OT; it's kind of my lullaby for getting daughter to sleep, (trying to) sing along with Morten."},{"id":18101874,"author":"adrianmonk","month":"2018-09","story":"E-Ink Monitors: Ready for Prime Time?","stance":"reference","why":"E-ink video's look reminds of the animation style in this video.","quote":"reminds me of the animation style of the 1980s music video \"Take On Me\" by the band a-ha","text":"Tangentially, the article shows video playing through eInk, and its choppy yet paper-like effect really reminds me of the animation style of the 1980s music video \"Take On Me\" by the band a-ha: [VIDEO] I'm not sure if I'd be able to use one of these monitors without constantly thinking of that video."},{"id":17798375,"author":"zaxomi","month":"2018-08","story":"Pop Songs Written by Native Speakers of Swedish","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the song whose title translates to touch me.","quote":"Take on me\" translated word for word will be \"ta på meg\" (Norwegian) or \"ta på mig\" (Swedish), meaning \"touch me\".","text":"Could it be \"Take on me\" with the Norwegian band \"A-ha\" from 1984? [VIDEO] \"Take on me\" translated word for word will be \"ta på meg\" (Norwegian) or \"ta på mig\" (Swedish), meaning \"touch me\"."},{"id":8233703,"author":"muglug","month":"2014-08","story":"Implementing a \"sketch\" style of rendering in webGL","stance":"reference","why":"Sketch-style look suggested by the Take On Me video.","quote":"That'd give it more of a \"Take On Me\"","text":"Cool! It'd be awesome to see hatching that wasn't aligned to the particular planes, but rather the camera normal. That'd give it more of a \"Take On Me\" ( [VIDEO] ) look."}]},"eMJk4y9NGvE":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference whenever a thread involves absurd procedures or manufacturing explanations.","m":[{"id":40074137,"author":"HenryBemis","month":"2024-04","story":"How Matzo Is Made","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"all these vids are ruined :)","text":"I used to enjoy \"how ... is made\" videos on YouTube. I used to work in a dairy factory (in IT) a few decades ago, and it was amazing to see the process. And then after watching \"Rick and Morty - Plumbus: how they do it\" ( [VIDEO] ) all these vids are ruined :)"},{"id":37800174,"author":"HenryBemis","month":"2023-10","story":"The Honeypot Diaries: Thousands of Daily Attacks on My Home Network","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"here are the instructions","text":"Regarding the list of things that people buy, I have to admit that I couldn't resist to buy a Plumbus. And for those who don't want to spend money buying it and prefer to DIY it, here are the instructions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28610806,"author":"codetrotter","month":"2021-09","story":"Introduction to Hippo: The WebAssembly PaaS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I couldn’t help but think of that one scene from Rick & Morty","text":"> An application is packaged as a bindle. Bindles are collected together in a bindle server that you can search. It’s a good article and nice work. But I couldn’t help but think of that one scene from Rick & Morty when I read this that goes like: “Everyone has a plumbus in their home. First they take the dingle bop and they smooth it out with a bunch of schleem. The schleem is then...repurposed for later batches. “They take the dingle bop and they push it through the grumbo, where the fleeb is rubbed against it. It's important that the fleeb is rubbed, because the fleeb has all the fleeb juice. “Then, a schlami shows up, and he rubs it...and spits on it. “They cut the fleeb. There's several hizzards in the way. “The blamfs rub against the chumbles, and the...plubis, and grumbo are shaved away. “That leaves you with...a regular old plumbus.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":11067650,"author":"ishigami","month":"2016-02","story":"What Kind of Name Is That? How to Name Fictional Characters","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That reminds me of \"How Things Are Made\" from Rick and Morty","text":"I thought the \"problem\" with Nightfall is not that Asimov used \"human\" words instead of \"Alien words\", rather that there seemed to be almost one-to-one correspondence between the Earthly things and Kalgash. Kalgashians act exactly the same as humans on Earth(or rather 50s Earth) and have a concept of newspaper :) > If you prefer, you can imagine that the text reads \"vorks\" wherever it says \"miles,\" \"gliizbiiz\" wherever it says \"hours,\" and \"sleshtraps\" where it says \"eyes.\" Or you can make up your own terms. Vorks or miles, it will make no difference when the Stars come out. That reminds me of \"How Things Are Made\" from Rick and Morty: [VIDEO]"}]},"i8ju_10NkGY":{"wy":"Mostly dropped in as a joke or tongue-in-cheek reference about piracy.","m":[{"id":44139997,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-05","story":"At Amazon, some coders say their jobs have begun to resemble warehouse work","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Indeed, but the Apache license is quite different from BSD or GPL v3. In my opinion, less restrictions on all users naturally fragments any attempt to lock-down generic features in the ecosystem. Submarine Patent attacks using FOSS code as a delivery mechanism is far more of a problem these days, as users often can't purchase rights even if trying to observe legal requirements. The first-to-file laws also mean published works may be captured even if already in the public domain. It is a common error to think commercial entities are somehow different from the university legal outreach departments. Yet they often offer the same legal peril, and unfeasible economic barriers to deployment. Best regards, =3 [VIDEO]"},{"id":39237805,"author":"p1mrx","month":"2024-02","story":"Car insurance in America is too cheap","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here's a corroborating source","text":"Here's a corroborating source: [VIDEO] >Being a pirate is alright to be >Do what you want 'cause a pirate is free"},{"id":21308604,"author":"Buge","month":"2019-10","story":"Disney+ streaming uses draconian DRM","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"On the other hand, sometimes \"pirate\" seems to have a positive connotation. [VIDEO] And interestingly the 3rd highest comment on that video currently is about Disney+."}]},"pb5_F4_Eod8":{"wy":"Used as an example of existing anti-drone-swarm defences in threads on drone warfare.","m":[{"id":44158543,"author":"koonsolo","month":"2025-06","story":"Ukraine destroys more than 40 military aircraft in drone attack deep in Russia","stance":"reference","why":"Example of airburst anti-drone ammunition.","quote":"Oh please there are plenty of solutions, see","text":"Oh please there are plenty of solutions, see [VIDEO] for example. Bullets that explode into pellets thanks to an internal radar, etc."},{"id":37362502,"author":"ThrowawayR2","month":"2023-09","story":"The US Military Is Getting Smaller, Cheaper and Smarter","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates anti-drone swarm defense.","quote":"Drone swarm, what drone swarm?","text":"-brrt- Drone swarm, what drone swarm? [VIDEO] And this is simply the first iteration of such systems. Drones are slow (slower than WW2 aircraft), short ranged, and not particularly stealthy. Improving any of those aspects raises the price significantly, defeating the point of cheap drones. They are a threat but not as magical as some seem to imagine."},{"id":37554275,"author":"scotty79","month":"2023-09","story":"A.I. and the Next Generation of Drone Warfare","stance":"reference","why":"Cannon with smart shells that shoot down drone swarms.","quote":"Have you seen modern anti-drone defence?","text":"Have you seen modern anti-drone defence? [VIDEO] Basically a cannon that fires sort of smart bullets that contain shape charge that aims at the drone as it gets close to it and detonantes. Also targets are smartly assigned so you can shoot down small swarm of drones with a short burst from the cannon."},{"id":32572555,"author":"jasonwatkinspdx","month":"2022-08","story":"Military drone swarms and how to combat them","stance":"reference","why":"Point defense system that can engage drones with programmed-burst shells.","quote":"There are a couple point defense systems originally designed to counter rocket and cruise missile attacks that can readily attack drones as well.","text":"Yes. There are a couple point defense systems originally designed to counter rocket and cruise missile attacks that can readily attack drones as well. Example: [VIDEO] This specific system uses a magnetic coil to measure the muzzle velocity of the shell as it's leaving the barrel, and sets a timing delay for the shell to burst. So that combined with the radar means this thing can put flack in very precise places."},{"id":31383588,"author":"ThrowawayR2","month":"2022-05","story":"Shaped Charges – Sheet of copper going through 1ft of solid steel (2010) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Cheap rounds shooting down drones.","quote":"","text":"Then then drones meet [VIDEO] or [LINK] for which each round is even cheaper than the cheap drone it's aimed at and, snap, crackle, pop, no more drones."},{"id":31084753,"author":"ThrowawayR2","month":"2022-04","story":"The tank is dead: Long live the javelin, the switchblade, the ... ?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a truck-mounted system countering drone swarms.","quote":"it always fascinates me that arms manufacturers not only create ads and brochures but that they're often done rather well.","text":"Then the drone swarm meets this and one or the other survives: [VIDEO] (As an aside, it always fascinates me that arms manufacturers not only create ads and brochures but that they're often done rather well.) A lot of the breathless enthusiasm for drones seems premature. SAMs didn't make aircraft obsolete, air to ground missiles from aircraft and helicopters didn't make tanks and other vehicles obsolete, and there's doesn't seem to be any reason to believe that adjustments won't be made by the various militaries to provide protection against drones."},{"id":30743446,"author":"ThrowawayR2","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Are Tanks Obsolete?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of anti-drone air defense system.","quote":"","text":"My understanding is that if a military is not protecting tanks with a screening force of infantry to counter ambushers equipped with anti-tank missiles (since, unlike video games, tanks/armored vehicles and infantry are intended to work together hand-in-hand to support each other) and if that military has not established air superiority or provided proper air defenses against drones (e.g. [VIDEO] ), then yes, their tanks are going to be ineffective and vulnerable. But, as the proverbial doctor said, \" If it hurts when you do that, well, then don't do that. \" [EDIT] It also doesn't help if the opponent has several big friends with immense reconnaissance capabilities that are telling them exactly where the tanks are at all times, allowing them to pick the best spots for ambush to make them much, much more effective than they normally would be."}]},"rDsTc2oWGSI":{"wy":"Cited as a joke or mild criticism of institutional safety culture at Los Alamos.","m":[{"id":23852005,"author":"tmearnest","month":"2020-07","story":"How Harold Lloyd Filmed \"Safety Last!\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I recommend you review this safety video.","text":"If you work at, or are planning to visit Los Alamos National Lab, I recommend you review this safety video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9890062,"author":"jessriedel","month":"2015-07","story":"The Los Alamos Primer: The Introductory Course for Scientists at Los Alamos","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Los Alamos National Lab has been setting its sights a little lower these days","text":"Unfortunately, Los Alamos National Lab has been setting its sights a little lower these days: \"How to use the stairs\" [VIDEO] Nothing a little federal cash can't fix, though: \"The Los Alamos National Laboratory Slip Simulator Experience\" [LINK]"},{"id":7410850,"author":"jessriedel","month":"2014-03","story":"Do Workplace Wellness Programs Save Employers Money?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Los Alamos National Lab's infamous music video to encourage people to use handrails when they're on the stairs","text":"Luckily there's a fix for that too. Behold, Los Alamos National Lab's infamous music video to encourage people to use handrails when they're on the stairs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14609066,"author":"yoloswagins","month":"2017-06","story":"A Critical Problem: Safety at Los Alamos","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Despite two weeks of videos like this [1], managers think it's ok","text":"Incredibly irresponsible, and exceedingly hypocritical. Before you can start work, Los Alamos National Labs requires an inordinate amount of safety training. Despite two weeks of videos like this [1], managers think it's ok to make their reports risk the fate of Slotin. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"raNx9L4VH2k":{"wy":"Referenced as the authoritative talk on Capsicum, including comparisons of how little code it takes to sandbox programs versus SELinux.","q":{"x":"to sandbox chromium with capsicum it took 100 LOC, to sandbox chromium with SELinux it took 200 LOC","i":15744436,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15744436,"author":"2trill2spill","month":"2017-11","story":"Application Container Security Guide [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Capsicum sandboxing needs less code than SELinux","quote":"to sandbox chromium with capsicum it took 100 LOC, to sandbox chromium with SELinux it took 200 LOC","text":"> Sounds like a good use case for SELinux kinda, although you could implement sandboxing with SELinux it's going to take much more effort. SELinux is a Mandatory Access Control Framework and Capsicum is a hybrid Unix capabilities system. While there is overlap in what you can do with them they really our different sets of tools. Mandatory access controls are for system administrators to lock down and control access on a machine. Capsicum is for application authors to sandbox their application. Ideally they should be used together. Back to my point that while MAC(SELinux) can be used for sandboxing but will take more effort then using something like capsicum or pledge. With MAC you have to think of all the things you don't want an application to do. With Capsicum or Pledge you only have to think of the things you want your application to do and everything else will be blocked automatically. So for example to sandbox chromium with capsicum it took 100 LOC, to sandbox chromium with SELinux it took 200 LOC so not bad but it doesn't stop IPC primitives. So half the code and more protection. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13212576,"author":"X86BSD","month":"2016-12","story":"Show HN: FSSB – A filesystem sandbox for Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Capsicum on FreeBSD as comparison","quote":"","text":"I can't even tell what this does. I just woke up but is this like capsicum on freebsd, [VIDEO] , or jails in the FS, or both? Clearly I need to wake up."},{"id":5566520,"author":"emaste","month":"2013-04","story":"FreeBSD Foundation Announces Capsicum Framework Project","stance":"reference","why":"Robert Watson's 2010 Capsicum talk","quote":"There's also a video of Robert Watson's 2010 Capsicum talk","text":"Some background on Capsicum, from [LINK] Capsicum is a lightweight OS capability and sandbox framework developed at the University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory, supported by a grant from Google. Capsicum extends the POSIX API, providing several new OS primitives to support object-capability security on UNIX-like operating systems: * capabilities - refined file descriptors with fine-grained rights * capability mode - process sandboxes that deny access to global namespaces * process descriptors - capability-centric process ID replacement * anonymous shared memory objects - an extension to the POSIX shared memory API to support anonymous swap objects associated with file descriptors (capabilities) * rtld-elf-cap - modified ELF run-time linker to construct sandboxed applications * libcapsicum - library to create and use capabilities and sandboxed components * libuserangel - library allowing sandboxed applications or components to interact with user angels, such as Power Boxes. * chromium-capsicum - a version of Google's Chromium web browser that uses capability mode and capabilities to provide effective sandboxing of high-risk web page rendering. There's also a video of Robert Watson's 2010 Capsicum talk at [VIDEO] Edited to add: The FreeBSD Foundation plans to significantly increase spending on projects (such as this one) as well as full-time staff members in 2013: [LINK]"}]},"uMM0lRfX6YI":{"wy":"Brought up whenever robotic or automatic EV charging comes up, as the memorable 2015 demo that never became a product.","q":{"x":"Tesla demoed that in 2015, but didn't make it into a product.[1]","i":30818458,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":47188059,"author":"Animats","month":"2026-02","story":"The Robotic Dexterity Deadlock","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There was a good application - Tesla's snake charging robot.[2]","text":"This is what a pancake motor looks like.[1] Thin and flat. Wrong form factor for fitting into a wrist. Strings (\"tendons\") have their own problems. Wear at bearing points, mostly. You need an opposed pair, too. So there's a motor, a winding drums, and something like a spring to maintain tension. Or two motors and two winding drums, which gives better control. That's why snake robots have never caught on. They're good painting robots, because they can get into tight spots. But repetitive cycling wears the tendons at the same place on each cycle. There was a good application - Tesla's snake charging robot.[2] All the complicated stuff is in the pedestal, and you can surround that with concrete to prevent damage if it's hit by a car. The snake part has no motors, just discs and strings, plus the connector and probably a cell phone camera at the end. It can be made easily replaceable, like a gas station hose. But some people thought it was too creepy. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42031497,"author":"Animats","month":"2024-11","story":"Boston Dynamics robot Atlas goes hands on [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I liked the Tesla snake robot for charging.[1]","text":"I liked the Tesla snake robot for charging.[1] But it upset many people. It looks complicated but is a rather simple mechanical design. There are several motors in the base pulling on cables. The arm itself is just segments, disks, and cables. So all the complexity is in the base, and you can surround that with a metal or concrete box to prevent damage. Arm replacement wouldn't be too expensive. Tesla is now touting wireless car charging, but that's a lot of power to transmit through air. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30818458,"author":"Animats","month":"2022-03","story":"Tesla's charging ports use a standard msg to open on 315MHz and can be replayed","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tesla demoed that in 2015, but didn't make it into a product.[1]","text":"The robotic arm that connects your Tesla to the charger Tesla demoed that in 2015, but didn't make it into a product.[1] Apparently too many people thought it was \"creepy\". (That's a good way to do the job. Snake arm robots are quite simple. The tube just contains some pull cables running through disks with holes.[2] All the motors are in the base. So you can build one of these with a protected base and an easily replaced arm tube. The usual problem with snake robots is that the cables wear out. But that's in painting applications, where they're in constant motion all day. A charging robot moves, slowly, for maybe one minute per hour. They're not very repeatable, which is fine in this application. The car won't be in the same place every time anyway. There's a TV camera in the end to find the socket. It's too bad that didn't ship as a product.) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27009266,"author":"Animats","month":"2021-05","story":"Types of Robotics Papers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Remember Tesla's robotic charging arm? [1] That was a good idea.","text":"That's very good. Note lack of \"we built an agricultural fruit-picking arm that can survive five years of picking\". Remember Tesla's robotic charging arm? [1] That was a good idea. If Tesla ever gets unsupervised autonomous driving to work reliably in parking lots, that will be useful. Then cars can self-charge. (The snake arm seems complicated, but it's not. The arm is just segments, cables, and discs. The motors are all in the base. So this can be built in a way such that damaged arm replacement is about as hard as gas pump hose replacement. Snake arm robots are not used much because they have a wear problem where the cables go through the discs. But that's for painting robots, which move constantly. A charging robot makes maybe ten moves a day.) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17911650,"author":"xedeon","month":"2018-09","story":"Mercedes Readies First Tesla Rival in $12B Attack","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Another Tesla tech demo","text":"> \"You will have some sort of docking station in your garage and when you park short-term the car flips the batteries for you with a loaded one\" Are you serious? Tesla did this and presented a tech demo in 2013: [LINK] > \"auto-plug itself and charges overnight. No cables, no mess.\" Another Tesla tech demo: [VIDEO] Also, what do you mean \"superchargers disappearing?\". It seems that Tesla is way a head on a lot of things and it will take quite an effort/investment for the incumbents to catch up. Though in reality, wide EV adoption is a WIN for everybody and is actually the core mission of Tesla, which is to \"Accelerate Sustainable Transport\""},{"id":15959242,"author":"gldalmaso","month":"2017-12","story":"What Needs to Happen Before Electric Cars Take Over the World","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Combining auto pilot with this prototype, it is at least feasible that it could work","text":"It seems like Tesla is at least hinting to aim for a system where you can basically send your car out to charge and have it back when you need it. Combining auto pilot with this prototype, it is at least feasible that it could work [VIDEO] Edit: Also this discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":14700894,"author":"worldsayshi","month":"2017-07","story":"Volvo signals 'end of the solely combustion engine-powered car'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Heh, this could work I guess","text":"Can we please standardize charging stations to not require a person to manually establish a connection? It seems that it would pose such an awkward and unnecessary hurdle. Especially in the coming age of self driving cars. Every time you arrive or leave you have to remember to (dis-)connect the charger. Why not just have some kind of inductive energy transfer or I dunno... something. Edit1: Heh, this could work I guess: [VIDEO] - seems it could be too expensive for mass-usage though. Edit2: This is more what I had in mind: [LINK]"},{"id":12787085,"author":"lorenzhs","month":"2016-10","story":"Battery technology may emerge as a trillion-dollar threat to credit markets","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You need a Tesla robotic charger snake","text":"You need a Tesla robotic charger snake: [VIDEO] :) (it's only a prototype for now). But no, it's not a huge issue. New cars seem to come with apps nowadays, so as you said your car can just send you a notification to your phone if you haven't plugged it in five minutes after arriving back home. And maybe another one if it's still not plugged in before you go to bed. Unavailability of charging (see sibling comment) is a bigger issue that won't be as easy to tackle. But reminding users to charge their car isn't what's hindering EV adoption. You can be late if you forget to fill up your petrol car, too. That can easily take ten minutes if there's a bit of a queue."},{"id":11735366,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-05","story":"Destruction of large numbers of jobs by robots unlikely, says new OECD Study","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tesla has a charging robot.[3]","text":"By this logic should we have autonomous refueling already? It's been done a few times.[1][2] The systems are rather slow and clunky, but work. Dealing with all the variation between cars runs up the cost. Tesla has a charging robot.[3] This is much easier, since Tesla controls both sides of the interface. The car actively cooperates, opening the charging port door. Tesla also interlocks the car so that you can't drive away while plugged in. I'm surprised that Tesla hasn't deployed those robot chargers yet. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11752847,"author":"garrettheaver","month":"2016-05","story":"Electric cars are no longer held back by crappy, expensive batteries","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yeah I understand what you mean and I think you're right. 1000 charge points is unacceptable but do we really need that? Given the current generation Tesla can already self navigate pretty well what's stopping the cars navigate the car park in your absence and charge themselves at a few central charge points. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10019057,"author":"corysama","month":"2015-08","story":"Tesla unveils new car-charging robo-snake","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The gif is sped up. This vid is actual speed","text":"The gif is sped up. This vid is actual speed [VIDEO] I think it's awesome. But, it obvious that the current implementation will inspire the creeps in lots of people. Sci-fi horror has put an unfortunate bad rap on robotic tentacles and chromed vertebrae."}]},"w4sLAQvEH-M":{"wy":"Linked as the go-to overview of the long-running bacteria experiment whenever real-time evolution or resistance comes up.","q":{"x":"The longest-running evolution experiment uses bacteria and it has been running for more than 30 years.","i":33983648,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35262243,"author":"vecter","month":"2023-03","story":"Fungus Candida auris infections spreading across US, CDC warns","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how quickly bacteria evolve resistance to high antibiotic levels.","quote":"","text":"There are two factors now which make things different: 1. As other commenters have pointed out, the ease of spread of any disease is orders of magnitude higher now than it was during cave-man days. With crowded cities, public transit, and air travel, pandemic-level diseases can spread across the world in a matter of days or weeks. 2. Much more importantly, our use of medicines (antibiotics, antivirals, antifungals, etc.) have put extreme selective pressure on pathogens to evolve to become resistant to them. We are, in a sense, selectively breeding pathogens that are both good at infecting humans and resistant to our treatments. That is a focused selective pressure that has never existed before, and may pose the biggest threat to us in the next 50-100 years. For a concrete example, look at this experiment which shows how quickly bacteria can evolve to overcome levels of antibiotics that are orders of magnitude more than we would ever give a human [0]. Yes, there will generally always be some subset of the human population that is resistant to any particular disease, but our measure of success is not \"5% of humans will survive the next pandemic, so we're all good, let's it move along nothing to see here.\" If we start to regularly have drug-resistant pathogens that make hospital stays very dangerous for patients (imagine having a 1/3 or even 1/10 chance of dying at a hospital due to an infection entirely irrelevant to your condition) or just more common COVID-level pandemics, th"},{"id":33983648,"author":"fernanDOTdo","month":"2022-12","story":"Recursive Game of Life","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the 30-plus year bacteria evolution experiment.","quote":"The longest-running evolution experiment uses bacteria and it has been running for more than 30 years.","text":"The longest-running evolution experiment uses bacteria and it has been running for more than 30 years. There's a Veritasium video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28998070,"author":"reuben364","month":"2021-10","story":"The danger of hidden functional roles","stance":"reference","why":"Bacteria in an experiment evolving to use a second food source.","quote":"Here is a video on the topic of bacteria in an experiment evolving to use a second source of food","text":"Here is a video on the topic of bacteria in an experiment evolving to use a second source of food: [VIDEO] . I'm not sure if they depend on it being present though."},{"id":28344976,"author":"BatteryMountain","month":"2021-08","story":"Reducing sugar in packaged foods can prevent disease in millions","stance":"reference","why":"Long-running bacteria experiment where glucose availability controls population growth.","quote":"Basically, they are doing experiments to track evolution in \"realtime\" of some organisms.","text":"(Speculation ahead!) Interesting point in video posted recently by Veritasium: Basically, they are doing experiments to track evolution in \"realtime\" of some organisms. During the conversation the guy mentions that the population growth of their organisms are controlled by adding or removing glucose for them to consume - basically as long as glucose is available, there will be growth. (First 5 minutes of video) This is really not my field at all, but I found it interesting that sugar has that great an effect on growth. Considering how much sugar most humans eat, considering the gross amount of other organisms in our bodies.. there must be some kind of measurable effect on our microbiome by eating more sugars (or things that use glucose). What if sugar is ~the~ culprit for many diseases (cancer anyone?)? I'm not anti-sugar, just speculating. Criticism welcome, am I on the right path to think this way? [VIDEO]"}]},"xA4QWwaweWA":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as the making-of companion to IBM's atom stop-motion film.","m":[{"id":43476004,"author":"mncharity","month":"2025-03","story":"What Killed Innovation?","stance":"reference","why":"Making-of video for IBM's stop-motion atom animation.","quote":"Making of","text":"> So what next? LLM discussion of visualizations? I did guerrilla usability testing around teaching scale, which included this video[1] (stop-motion animation using CO molecules). Lots of people asked \"What are those ripples?\". IBM even had a supplementary webpage addressing this (which I no longer see, even on archive). People could easily ask this with me standing beside them, but not so much if viewing the content online. Which raised the UI question of how to encourage such. With LLMs, perhaps people will be able to ask questions of a visualization? What is that? Why is that? What about ...? I don't understand ... Does this mean ...? [1] IBM's A boy and his atom [LINK] Making of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19379161,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2019-03","story":"A Boy and His Atom: The World's Smallest Movie","stance":"reference","why":"Making-of video for the atom movie.","quote":"How it was made","text":"It's nice to see it again, but remember that this was done in (2013). More info: Wikipedia page: [LINK] How it was made: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8618029,"author":"ColinWright","month":"2014-11","story":"A Boy and His Atom: The World's Smallest Movie","stance":"reference","why":"How the atom movie was made.","quote":"How it was made","text":"Submitted many times, and yet it never gets any discussion. The only comment on all the previous submissions (from a quick, desultory search) is this: ======== How it was made : [VIDEO] What are the ripples? : [LINK] ======== Similarly, it never seemed to get much attention, judging by the severe lack of up-votes. Here are the previous submissions I found (some deleted): [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"}]}});
